WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:09.380
&gt;&gt; RICHARD N. HAASS: Well, good afternoon
and welcome to today's lunch program of this

00:00:09.380 --> 00:00:15.230
first ever -- but we hope first of many -- gathering
of the Council of Councils, as we are calling

00:00:15.230 --> 00:00:16.230
it.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:18.580
Just a couple of housekeeping remarks for
a second.

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:24.230
If people would please turn off your cellphones,
that would be a great help -- BlackBerrys

00:00:24.230 --> 00:00:25.480
and all such devices.

00:00:25.480 --> 00:00:29.350
Secondly, this session is on the record.

00:00:29.350 --> 00:00:31.340
Let me just say that.

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:35.480
Let me -- and we'll repeat later, but just
let people know -- the building we're meeting

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:39.969
in is only several years old, and it's environmentally,
shall we say, advanced.

00:00:39.969 --> 00:00:41.739
It's a Gold LEED building.

00:00:41.739 --> 00:00:44.980
And what we'd like to do -- if anyone would
be interested, after the last session today

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:50.989
at 3:45, we are more than happy to give you
a tour of the building, just so you can see

00:00:50.989 --> 00:00:55.770
what sorts of things have been done here,
both environmentally, but also in audio-visual

00:00:55.770 --> 00:01:03.840
type things and all that, which might be (of)
interest to some of you from other organizations.

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:09.929
As I said, this is the inaugural meeting of
the Council of Councils, which is a network

00:01:09.929 --> 00:01:16.220
of policy institutes from around the world,
and the whole idea is to discuss, identify

00:01:16.220 --> 00:01:19.079
global challenges as well as global opportunities.

00:01:19.079 --> 00:01:25.259
And hopefully out of the discussions will
emerge some ideas, some initiatives that people

00:01:25.259 --> 00:01:31.469
then can take home and introduce either into
their own respective public debates or possibly

00:01:31.469 --> 00:01:35.659
as well into decision-making circles within
their own countries.

00:01:35.659 --> 00:01:43.170
Some 20 countries or so are represented here
today, roughly tracking the G-20, the difference

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.969
being that the agenda here is far more comprehensive.

00:01:46.969 --> 00:01:52.219
The agenda here is not simply economic or
even economic and energy and environmental

00:01:52.219 --> 00:01:56.590
issues, but deals also with the full range
of political challenges -- for example, humanitarian

00:01:56.590 --> 00:01:59.950
intervention, those questions -- as well as
security challenges.

00:01:59.950 --> 00:02:07.869
We just spent an hour and a half discussing
-- we didn't quite solve, but discussing the

00:02:07.869 --> 00:02:12.110
challenge to the nonproliferation regime posed
by -- posed by Iran.

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:17.269
We also had a general conversation about global
governance, the whole challenge of coming

00:02:17.269 --> 00:02:23.430
up with common rules and norms and, ultimately,
institutions to bolster what Hedley Bull would've

00:02:23.430 --> 00:02:29.799
called the society part of international relations,
as opposed to the anarchical dimension of

00:02:29.799 --> 00:02:30.799
international relations.

00:02:30.799 --> 00:02:36.720
I would like to offer special thanks to the
Robina Foundation, who have made not just

00:02:36.720 --> 00:02:41.349
this possible, but the larger program, the
council's program on international institutions

00:02:41.349 --> 00:02:43.290
and global governance.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:48.879
And I'd also like to pay a special welcome
to one of my bosses, to Carla Hills, who is

00:02:48.879 --> 00:02:53.439
the co-chair of the board of directors of
the Council on Foreign Relations and a former

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:54.939
U.S. trade representative.

00:02:54.939 --> 00:03:02.480
Indeed, she is one of two former U.S. trade
representatives with us today -- actually,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.530
one of three; we have Charlene, Carla -- Charlene
Barshefsky -- and Bob Zoellick.

00:03:07.530 --> 00:03:13.269
So probably nowhere in the world at this moment
are there more U.S. trade representatives,

00:03:13.269 --> 00:03:16.079
and -- (laughter) -- who knew?

00:03:16.079 --> 00:03:18.260
And so any of you tweeting can tweet that.

00:03:18.260 --> 00:03:19.260
(Laughter.)

00:03:19.260 --> 00:03:23.750
I'm thrilled to welcome Bob Zoellick.

00:03:23.750 --> 00:03:28.900
Bob is the 11th president of the World Bank
Group.

00:03:28.900 --> 00:03:30.499
And he's a longtime personal friend.

00:03:30.499 --> 00:03:32.310
Let me get the conflict of interest out on
the table.

00:03:32.310 --> 00:03:37.220
We've worked together for more years or decades
than either of us probably care to remember.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:42.709
And he's also been a longtime supporter and
active member of the council.

00:03:42.709 --> 00:03:48.450
He first spoke here in the early 1990s, roughly
two decades ago, and he served on the council's

00:03:48.450 --> 00:03:49.450
board.

00:03:49.450 --> 00:03:53.760
And our statisticians tell us that he's actually
spoken here more than 20 times.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:55.519
Did you know that?

00:03:55.519 --> 00:04:00.750
For no fee -- I should point that out.

00:04:00.750 --> 00:04:03.639
Bob is in the nonprofit business, as are we.

00:04:03.639 --> 00:04:04.639
(Chuckles.)

00:04:04.639 --> 00:04:06.430
I won't spend a lot of time on his bio.

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:09.030
I already pointed out he was U.S. trade representative.

00:04:09.030 --> 00:04:16.139
He was deputy secretary of state as well as
several other positions in the State Department.

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:19.720
And Bob played, I thought, an extraordinary
role -- and if I were still teaching at the

00:04:19.720 --> 00:04:25.270
Kennedy School, I would highlight him to show
how in government, you can be entrepreneurial.

00:04:25.270 --> 00:04:29.759
And Bob was truly entrepreneurial after the
fall of the Berlin Wall in stitching together

00:04:29.759 --> 00:04:34.150
what became the German unification inside
of NATO.

00:04:34.150 --> 00:04:40.180
It was policymaking, I thought, at its highest
and finest.

00:04:40.180 --> 00:04:44.650
What we're going to do is have a conversation
for a few minutes about economic issues and

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:47.449
the Bank and all that, and then we're going
to open it up to you all.

00:04:47.449 --> 00:04:52.430
And I won't go on to -- I won't filibuster,
even though we're in the city of filibusters.

00:04:52.430 --> 00:04:57.930
And when we get around to Q-and-A, we'll hand
out microphones, and we'll just ask you to

00:04:57.930 --> 00:05:01.199
identify yourself before you speak.

00:05:01.199 --> 00:05:03.810
You OK there, sir?

00:05:03.810 --> 00:05:05.780
Again, welcome, Bob.

00:05:05.780 --> 00:05:11.979
A few weeks ago you published a piece in our
magazine, Foreign Affairs, which was titled

00:05:11.979 --> 00:05:14.960
"Why We Still Need the World Bank."

00:05:14.960 --> 00:05:16.930
OK, let's start with that.

00:05:16.930 --> 00:05:17.960
Why do we?

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:24.120
&gt;&gt; ROBERT ZOELLICK: Well, first, let me thank
all of you for taking time to come to this.

00:05:24.120 --> 00:05:27.090
Richard explained to me a little bit to me
about this concept, and I think it's a great

00:05:27.090 --> 00:05:28.310
one.

00:05:28.310 --> 00:05:32.379
So I know for many of you, it required a little
bit of a journey, but it sounds like a superb

00:05:32.379 --> 00:05:34.020
idea to be able to get the councils together.

00:05:34.020 --> 00:05:36.960
It's like trick questions, because aren't
they supposed to read the article?

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:37.960
(Laughter.)

00:05:37.960 --> 00:05:42.169
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Give him -- give him the Cliff Notes.

00:05:42.169 --> 00:05:43.169
(Laughs.)

00:05:43.169 --> 00:05:47.520
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, let me -- let me take this
approach.

00:05:47.520 --> 00:05:52.830
When I came to the World Bank Group about
a little less than five years ago, I had a

00:05:52.830 --> 00:05:59.770
slightly different perspective than some had
in that some who come purely from the economics

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:11.720
profession or even development economics -- and
that is I view the World Bank as part of this

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:19.330
very thin tissue of multilateral institutions
that was created during and after World War

00:06:19.330 --> 00:06:26.090
II to try to deal with the problems that were
seen as -- the political-economic problems

00:06:26.090 --> 00:06:32.400
that were seen as driving the crisis in the
'30s and leading to the world war.

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:38.379
And this includes the IMF; it included at
that time the GATT that became the WTO for

00:06:38.379 --> 00:06:41.940
trade; it's got the World Bank, other institutions.

00:06:41.940 --> 00:06:46.229
And it's striking if you go back and you look
at the issues that people were concerned about:

00:06:46.229 --> 00:06:52.460
currency in international capital flows, you
know, free trade, reconstruction of broken

00:06:52.460 --> 00:06:55.650
states, development, investment flows.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:07:00.979
Those remain very vital issues, but obviously,
the circumstances changed enormously.

00:07:00.979 --> 00:07:06.289
So I saw part of the challenge for the Bank
and my challenge as a leader of the bank as

00:07:06.289 --> 00:07:11.759
how do you modernize multilateralism; how
do you -- recognizing that nation-states are

00:07:11.759 --> 00:07:17.240
still going to be the dominant player, how
do we take this thin interconnecting tissue,

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:22.620
if you want to call it, related to global
governance, and make sure that it's updated

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:25.039
for the current age.

00:07:25.039 --> 00:07:28.350
That gives you a slightly different strategic
perspective.

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:34.699
So for example, if you look at this solely
from an economics perspective, in 2007 you'd

00:07:34.699 --> 00:07:40.949
say, well, in 1945 you didn't really have
private capital flows, and so -- but now you

00:07:40.949 --> 00:07:46.220
had a whole large flow of private capital
that dominate the official sector, so why

00:07:46.220 --> 00:07:48.560
do you need this anymore.

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:52.590
OK, well, problem number one is that what
economists thought in 2007 -- (chuckles) -- turned

00:07:52.590 --> 00:07:53.870
out not to be the case.

00:07:53.870 --> 00:07:58.129
In 2008, 2009, we learned economists can be
wrong.

00:07:58.129 --> 00:08:04.050
But even more than that, it missed the fact
that a lot of what the Bank does is not just

00:08:04.050 --> 00:08:08.909
invest money; it's the knowledge and learning
and interconnecting transfer.

00:08:08.909 --> 00:08:14.610
Or another perspective -- this, again, seems
obvious to me, but it may come back as a debating

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:15.610
item.

00:08:15.610 --> 00:08:19.950
When I came to the Bank, there was a school
of thought that said, really, the middle-income

00:08:19.950 --> 00:08:23.490
countries should just be sort of taken out;
you just focused on the poorest.

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:28.639
Well, if you believe in this as a multilateral
institution, that would be a strategic mistake

00:08:28.639 --> 00:08:33.750
of huge proportions because actually you want
to get -- first off, there's some huge development

00:08:33.750 --> 00:08:35.820
challenges in the so-called middle-income
countries.

00:08:35.820 --> 00:08:40.680
About, you know, 75 percent of the people
living under $2 a day are in those countries.

00:08:40.680 --> 00:08:45.420
But, in addition, you actually want to get
those countries to be responsible stakeholders

00:08:45.420 --> 00:08:46.650
in the system.

00:08:46.650 --> 00:08:51.110
And so just to give you a very current example,
the Bank's got a lot of attention recently

00:08:51.110 --> 00:08:55.460
because of a report we did called "China 2030,"
which is looking at the structural changes

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:59.270
of China, that it will need to have a different
growth model.

00:08:59.270 --> 00:09:03.760
And I've found this has got interest in other
middle-income countries; it's got the investment

00:09:03.760 --> 00:09:06.690
community; it's got the interest of Congress.

00:09:06.690 --> 00:09:10.780
You know, if you said, well, we weren't supposed
to deal with the Chinas of the world, then

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:12.290
you wouldn't be doing reports like that.

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:14.200
And yet it's fundamental to the rebalancing.'

00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:20.660
So part of what I try to do in the article
is talk about the intellectual and strategic

00:09:20.660 --> 00:09:25.390
position of the Bank, but then I also talk
about some of the challenges of modernizing

00:09:25.390 --> 00:09:30.550
the institution and opening it up, changing
the development model so there's not a monopolist

00:09:30.550 --> 00:09:35.450
of sort of people with Ph.D.s from certain
universities and sort of democratizing the

00:09:35.450 --> 00:09:37.210
development process.

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:38.690
So that's part of the challenge.

00:09:38.690 --> 00:09:41.740
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I've noticed from your previous
lives -- and we've talked about it before

00:09:41.740 --> 00:09:43.600
-- you're a great believer in institutions.

00:09:43.600 --> 00:09:48.520
Let me take a second, because it's so close
to this -- what we're talking about here,

00:09:48.520 --> 00:09:49.520
governance.

00:09:49.520 --> 00:09:51.580
What do you see as the real advantage of institutions?

00:09:51.580 --> 00:09:53.040
Why are they so important?

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:58.160
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, since you got a pretty
erudite group here, I mean, I could talk about

00:09:58.160 --> 00:10:00.420
Edmund Burke and kind of the view of institutions
that --

00:10:00.420 --> 00:10:01.730
&gt;&gt; HAASS: He couldn't come today, but we've
got -- (inaudible) --

00:10:01.730 --> 00:10:05.880
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: (Chuckles) -- he couldn't come
today, but he'll be here in the evening session

00:10:05.880 --> 00:10:07.330
when you do the Ouija Board.

00:10:07.330 --> 00:10:08.890
&gt;&gt; HAASS: (Laughs.)

00:10:08.890 --> 00:10:19.830
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, I think that the institutions,
whether domestic or international, play critical

00:10:19.830 --> 00:10:22.150
organizing roles, intermediating roles.

00:10:22.150 --> 00:10:28.230
It -- and in an international system, I think
what sometimes people underappreciate is they

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:36.370
can play a role in identifying issues, sometimes
helping to bring people together in a mutual

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:42.080
fashion on -- try to address those issues,
sometimes they can bring additional resources

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:43.350
to bear.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:46.100
And so they're the heart of problem-solving.

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:53.310
If you think about challenges, whether it
be private sector or public sector, it's a

00:10:53.310 --> 00:10:55.720
little hard to do in an atomistic way.

00:10:55.720 --> 00:11:01.410
So institutions become the enabling power;
they become the societal -- the social capital.

00:11:01.410 --> 00:11:03.170
They become the glue.

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:05.670
But a critical point: Institutions have to
adapt too.

00:11:05.670 --> 00:11:11.070
And I think one of my lessons from the public
sector is that, in the private sector, the

00:11:11.070 --> 00:11:16.320
market demands are such that institutions
are compelled to adapt or die; in the public

00:11:16.320 --> 00:11:18.860
sector, they can drift for a long time.

00:11:18.860 --> 00:11:24.810
And so this is -- this has implications for
how you open up the institution to criticism

00:11:24.810 --> 00:11:28.370
and engagement (and ?) others, but it also
has implications for the nature of how you

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:32.820
lead the institution because you really -- you
have to keep folk -- there's a tendency in

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:40.100
public sector institutions where process becomes
all controlling.

00:11:40.100 --> 00:11:47.190
So I have this debate sometimes in other international
forum(s) where people talk about quote, "legitimacy,"

00:11:47.190 --> 00:11:51.750
as -- and they define it simply by the participation.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:54.240
My view is legitimacy also has to be driven
by effectiveness.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.210
My view is legitimacy also has to be driven
by effectiveness.

00:11:56.210 --> 00:11:59.250
The League of Nations had one legitimate model
that turned out not to be very effective,

00:11:59.250 --> 00:12:04.170
OK, so the U.N. overhaul was designed to partly
deal with that.

00:12:04.170 --> 00:12:09.040
But I find when I deal with other international
institutions like some of those in the U.N.

00:12:09.040 --> 00:12:12.520
system, they've drifted a little further away
from the effectiveness model.

00:12:12.520 --> 00:12:18.890
And I think that it's incumbent on people
running international institutions to try

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:20.800
to sort of get that balance right.

00:12:20.800 --> 00:12:26.770
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Before you said the World Bank does
a lot more than dispense money.

00:12:26.770 --> 00:12:29.010
It's almost something of a misnomer.

00:12:29.010 --> 00:12:31.960
So if you could rename it, what would you
call it?

00:12:31.960 --> 00:12:33.270
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: (Chuckles.)

00:12:33.270 --> 00:12:36.350
Well, I don't think the name is so bad.

00:12:36.350 --> 00:12:41.420
I once had a friend who's now deceased who,
when he talked to me about the institution,

00:12:41.420 --> 00:12:46.020
said, look, if you have an institution that
has the two worlds -- words "world" and "bank"

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:50.450
and you can't do something with it, you know,
you're missing -- (laughter) -- but the reason

00:12:50.450 --> 00:12:58.030
that I highlight the -- why the Bank causes
it a problem is that you'll see when many

00:12:58.030 --> 00:13:01.700
people assess the Bank -- and you see this
when it's -- they'll look at the -- sort of

00:13:01.700 --> 00:13:06.340
the loan flows or -- so you now have people
comparing it with some of the big development

00:13:06.340 --> 00:13:08.670
banks in developing countries or others.

00:13:08.670 --> 00:13:15.870
And my view is is that when the World Bank
is most effective, it combines three things:

00:13:15.870 --> 00:13:21.920
One, we really take knowledge and learning
from all quarters of the world.

00:13:21.920 --> 00:13:25.790
And what's really changed with incredible
rapidity over the past decade is the degree

00:13:25.790 --> 00:13:30.580
to which that is now coming from developing
countries to other developing countries.

00:13:30.580 --> 00:13:34.910
And we try to update it, customize it, share
it with others.

00:13:34.910 --> 00:13:41.810
And then second, you try to have your investments
or projects so that they have a multiplier

00:13:41.810 --> 00:13:42.810
effect.

00:13:42.810 --> 00:13:46.840
So you're looking at building markets or institutions
or capacity.

00:13:46.840 --> 00:13:51.340
And then third, what distinguishes us from
a university or the OECD is that we can be

00:13:51.340 --> 00:13:53.620
innovative with different financial ways.

00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:58.610
And so, again, many people may not be aware
we have a private sector arm, IFC.

00:13:58.610 --> 00:14:00.620
So we make equity investments.

00:14:00.620 --> 00:14:03.800
One of the things we created was an asset
management company to change our financial

00:14:03.800 --> 00:14:09.250
intermediation model so that people will invest
in a fund that we create.

00:14:09.250 --> 00:14:13.700
We invest in private equity funds, about 180
around the world.

00:14:13.700 --> 00:14:20.330
So there's diverse ways of having the financial
innovation, and that's again where sometimes

00:14:20.330 --> 00:14:25.430
I think people, when they think about the
Bank, think about it as traditional, you know,

00:14:25.430 --> 00:14:27.160
1960s major loan program.

00:14:27.160 --> 00:14:35.510
You know, we use rain index futures for countries
because you can see, in certain countries,

00:14:35.510 --> 00:14:39.260
that are (heavy with ?) agriculture that if
the rain is below a certain level, you're

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:41.000
going to have a huge hit to the crop.

00:14:41.000 --> 00:14:43.000
You can use insurance programs.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:48.650
So, part of it is how do you use the tools
of financial innovation for development, but

00:14:48.650 --> 00:14:52.140
always keeping an idea on the sharing of knowledge
and information.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:58.170
Let me give you two current examples that
I've been focusing on.

00:14:58.170 --> 00:15:03.300
The safety net programs -- we're in a world
environment where I think we're going to have

00:15:03.300 --> 00:15:06.110
to expect considerable volatilities and uncertainties.

00:15:06.110 --> 00:15:09.100
You'll see some policy efforts to try to control
those.

00:15:09.100 --> 00:15:13.240
I'm not sure how successful those are going
to be, whether it's food or energy or natural

00:15:13.240 --> 00:15:15.320
disasters or others.

00:15:15.320 --> 00:15:23.630
So it makes sense, as a matter of policy,
to try to see if you can have effective, efficient,

00:15:23.630 --> 00:15:27.290
low-cost programs that provide a basic safety
net.

00:15:27.290 --> 00:15:31.550
Well, many of you may know that there's a
program that started in Mexico -- it's got

00:15:31.550 --> 00:15:36.200
a lot of attention in Brazil -- called "Oportunidades";
"Bolsa Familia" in Brazil -- they're called

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:42.250
conditional cash-transfer programs, and they
give money to the poorest people in communities.

00:15:42.250 --> 00:15:47.440
Varies on the program, normally maybe 15 to
20 percent of the population, on the conditions

00:15:47.440 --> 00:15:51.320
that the families send their children to school,
and they get health checkups.

00:15:51.320 --> 00:15:55.010
So it's probably done more for women's health
than anything in the history of the Mexico.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:16:00.100
The Bank has worked with over 40 countries
to expand this program, OK?

00:16:00.100 --> 00:16:03.080
And they customize it for different circumstances.

00:16:03.080 --> 00:16:07.560
And what's striking is in Mexico and Brazil,
it's done for about a half of 1 percent of

00:16:07.560 --> 00:16:09.170
GDP, OK?

00:16:09.170 --> 00:16:12.560
So if you consider entitlement programs in
the U.S. and Europe, this is a pretty darn

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:14.180
efficient investment, OK?

00:16:14.180 --> 00:16:19.910
Now, you have other countries that may not
have the capacity to have those set of programs.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:24.390
But -- so what have we learned about school
feeding programs, community-based efforts,

00:16:24.390 --> 00:16:29.090
so that you have some basic safety-net protection?

00:16:29.090 --> 00:16:35.980
So that's an area where you take sort of knowledge
and learning and expand it across regions

00:16:35.980 --> 00:16:37.830
but always have to customize.

00:16:37.830 --> 00:16:41.740
And I'll give you the direct relevance: You
look at a lot of the subsidy programs across

00:16:41.740 --> 00:16:44.310
North Africa, and they're a huge waste of
money.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:48.540
I mean -- and so you're spending a lot of
money, not getting much for it, not focusing

00:16:48.540 --> 00:16:51.520
on those most in need, not protecting people.

00:16:51.520 --> 00:16:55.700
So one of the things -- I just came from a
function over the weekend, which was talking

00:16:55.700 --> 00:16:57.460
more about U.S. policy.

00:16:57.460 --> 00:17:02.790
What I find somewhat striking in the world
of so-called development is that there's a

00:17:02.790 --> 00:17:08.839
-- much more of a -- what I'll call a relentless
pragmatism about what works, as opposed to

00:17:08.839 --> 00:17:13.549
getting locked into ideologies, which I sometimes
see in the developed world.

00:17:13.549 --> 00:17:17.380
And just to give you one other example that
I think will be very important -- and this

00:17:17.380 --> 00:17:21.610
is true for developed countries as well -- I
can't go to a developing country that isn't

00:17:21.610 --> 00:17:25.020
interested in infrastructure investment, and
there's a certain amount you get from the

00:17:25.020 --> 00:17:29.890
public sector, but obviously, people want
to get public-private partnerships, not only

00:17:29.890 --> 00:17:33.149
for the money, but increasingly, in middle-
income countries, it's because they're run

00:17:33.149 --> 00:17:37.870
more efficiently; they -- the design package
makes more sense.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:45.919
So we've created a hub in Singapore that is
trying to sort of take the knowledge of that

00:17:45.919 --> 00:17:51.789
region and others about how to design these
models so we can move beyond a series of one-offs.

00:17:51.789 --> 00:17:55.590
But to give you a sense of the changing Bank,
we're also working with Singapore to create

00:17:55.590 --> 00:17:59.750
a special infrastructure investment fund that
will go along with this process.

00:17:59.750 --> 00:18:05.289
So -- and then to bring it back to, say, the
United States, obviously, a lot of the states

00:18:05.289 --> 00:18:10.419
are having financial problems, but people
are only looking at the liability side of

00:18:10.419 --> 00:18:12.259
the balance sheet; they're not looking at
the assets.

00:18:12.259 --> 00:18:17.470
So Mitch Daniels, the governor of Indiana,
was able to privatize or monetize his toll

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:19.279
road for about $4 billion.

00:18:19.279 --> 00:18:22.779
It was controversial at the time -- ended
up being a big success.

00:18:22.779 --> 00:18:24.450
The state of Pennsylvania couldn't do it.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:30.370
So I point out to people that, you know, in
communist Chongqing, you can monetize a toll

00:18:30.370 --> 00:18:33.360
road, and in capitalist Pennsylvania, you
can't.

00:18:33.360 --> 00:18:39.030
So the -- part of the message of this is -- and
it's relevant, I think, for a group like this

00:18:39.030 --> 00:18:44.950
-- there's -- even 20 years ago, OK, when
Carla was working -- (inaudible) -- USTR,

00:18:44.950 --> 00:18:51.289
Charlene, there was still a common assumption
about a North-South knowledge transfer.

00:18:51.289 --> 00:18:54.860
Now it's increasingly South-South, South-North.

00:18:54.860 --> 00:18:55.900
And that's a good thing.

00:18:55.900 --> 00:19:00.070
&gt;&gt; HAASS: That actually segues naturally to
my next question, which is I -- you made the

00:19:00.070 --> 00:19:03.970
shocking pronouncement a few minutes ago that
economists have been known to be wrong.

00:19:03.970 --> 00:19:04.970
(Laughter.)

00:19:04.970 --> 00:19:09.440
And it's good we're all sitting down in this
room.

00:19:09.440 --> 00:19:11.530
Where are we now, then, with this debate about
models?

00:19:11.530 --> 00:19:15.670
I remember when I was a graduate student looking
-- and studying economic development, and

00:19:15.670 --> 00:19:19.571
you used to have the debate between sort of
top-down and bottom-up approaches, market

00:19:19.571 --> 00:19:25.420
approaches versus so-called social overhead
capital and, you know, large state role.

00:19:25.420 --> 00:19:26.420
Where's the debate now intellectually?

00:19:26.420 --> 00:19:31.029
What do -- what -- how has the balance swung,
if you will -- and maybe not -- in this debate

00:19:31.029 --> 00:19:33.539
about markets, private sectors versus public?

00:19:33.539 --> 00:19:35.360
You just talked about public-private a bit.

00:19:35.360 --> 00:19:38.020
Where's the theology or the ideology now?

00:19:38.020 --> 00:19:39.020
What's happening?

00:19:39.020 --> 00:19:40.750
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, it varies a huge amount.

00:19:40.750 --> 00:19:46.490
And my major point is that I think in the
developing world, the key is that it's not

00:19:46.490 --> 00:19:49.700
theological; it's pragmatic about what works.

00:19:49.700 --> 00:19:55.019
And I guess I'd take the question and tilt
it in one direction that I've been more worried

00:19:55.019 --> 00:19:58.470
about because it may be relevant for the work
of these councils.

00:19:58.470 --> 00:20:04.719
There's a lot of focus in the globe today
about the macroeconomic stabilization, the

00:20:04.719 --> 00:20:08.259
-- you know, basic fiscal and monetary policies.

00:20:08.259 --> 00:20:12.639
One of my concerns is that -- and this is
another way of answering your question about

00:20:12.639 --> 00:20:20.970
debate -- is that there's going to be a need
in all countries to focus on the structural

00:20:20.970 --> 00:20:21.970
change.

00:20:21.970 --> 00:20:24.970
So if you're talking about models, this is
-- really takes you more to the microeconomic.

00:20:24.970 --> 00:20:30.000
So the China 2030 report was about this in
the Chinese context, but it's true in Brazil;

00:20:30.000 --> 00:20:31.190
it's true in India.

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:36.440
And it's definitely true in the United States
-- (chuckles) -- Japan and in Europe as well.

00:20:36.440 --> 00:20:42.970
And the reason I worry this doesn't quite
get enough attention is if political leaders

00:20:42.970 --> 00:20:48.740
are unable to take this on, what I suspect
we'll see happen over the next year or two

00:20:48.740 --> 00:20:52.559
is that the monetary authorities -- and I'm
not being critical of what they've done so

00:20:52.559 --> 00:20:58.090
far, but the monetary authorities will feel
the pressure to use a tool that really isn't

00:20:58.090 --> 00:20:59.809
designed to fit the problem.

00:20:59.809 --> 00:21:06.120
And you're going to continue to see monetary
policies that are extraordinarily unusual

00:21:06.120 --> 00:21:10.820
by historical standards, and I think they
will plant the seeds of future problems, OK?

00:21:10.820 --> 00:21:14.440
Now, the nature of those problems is it's
always very difficult to predict, but I'll

00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:16.419
just give you one.

00:21:16.419 --> 00:21:19.190
I've spoken to some of the Federal Reserve
banks in the United States.

00:21:19.190 --> 00:21:21.870
I was out at the Kansas City Fed last year.

00:21:21.870 --> 00:21:24.429
And you know, when you go to places like that,
you ask local questions.

00:21:24.429 --> 00:21:26.870
I was asking about farmland prices.

00:21:26.870 --> 00:21:32.950
And farmland prices are really going up, really
at real levels, as I recall, similar to where

00:21:32.950 --> 00:21:36.690
they were at the height that led to the farm
credit system crisis in the early '80s.

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:41.309
Now, right now they're still covered by cash
flow, but farm prices are relatively high.

00:21:41.309 --> 00:21:48.679
So what I'm -- my point is that I think if
the economies, developed and developing, don't

00:21:48.679 --> 00:21:54.669
focus on the structural issues and the microeconomic
issues that are fundamentals to growth, we

00:21:54.669 --> 00:21:59.450
could see that as we, you know, continue to
go to phases of this recovery, we could be

00:21:59.450 --> 00:22:03.130
planting the seeds for a very serious future
problem.

00:22:03.130 --> 00:22:07.840
And for emerging markets, which are the ones
I'm most concerned about, this will mean you'll

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:12.730
probably have a rather modest growth environment
with lots of volatility and uncertainty, which

00:22:12.730 --> 00:22:19.390
means go back and build domestic demand, infrastructure
and be uncertain and be prepared for difficult

00:22:19.390 --> 00:22:22.489
moments like I mentioned with safety-net policies.

00:22:22.489 --> 00:22:26.409
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I'll just ask a couple more questions,
then we'll open it up.

00:22:26.409 --> 00:22:31.149
One of the issues historically has been uncertain
American and -- popular and congressional

00:22:31.149 --> 00:22:33.749
support for international institutions.

00:22:33.749 --> 00:22:36.860
Where does that stand with your institution?

00:22:36.860 --> 00:22:43.610
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, at least as of the last
year we're a bit of an anomaly, but this is

00:22:43.610 --> 00:22:49.519
-- let me explain the context.

00:22:49.519 --> 00:22:54.240
We achieved the first general capital increase
in the Bank in over 22 years.

00:22:54.240 --> 00:22:58.399
The last one, I was actually working for Secretary
Baker at the Treasury Department.

00:22:58.399 --> 00:23:03.860
So in an environment in which people are watching
their dollars closely, an environment which

00:23:03.860 --> 00:23:09.509
is sort of anti-foreign aid, where there's
populism, we were able to get this through

00:23:09.509 --> 00:23:12.879
a Democratic Senate and a Republican House.

00:23:12.879 --> 00:23:14.350
There's a actually a little bit of a lesson
there.

00:23:14.350 --> 00:23:15.350
(Chuckles.)

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:20.890
And the lesson is I spend a reasonable amount
of my time working with legislators -- and

00:23:20.890 --> 00:23:28.080
I do this in other countries as well -- but
part of this, in the case of the United States,

00:23:28.080 --> 00:23:34.679
was trying to get an appreciation of the value
of the Bank, having those that might be against

00:23:34.679 --> 00:23:37.990
it to recognize -- at least maybe not attack
it.

00:23:37.990 --> 00:23:43.080
So it's a -- it's an -- I don't want to make
too much of it, but I happen to believe that

00:23:43.080 --> 00:23:47.210
on the key issues that are going to be facing
the United States, things like broad-based

00:23:47.210 --> 00:23:52.470
tax reform and entitlement reform, things
can be done, but you're going to need an executive

00:23:52.470 --> 00:23:57.789
leader, and you're going to have to figure
out how to work the system a little bit better

00:23:57.789 --> 00:23:58.789
than has been done before.

00:23:58.789 --> 00:24:01.169
But that takes us in another direction.

00:24:01.169 --> 00:24:05.200
But I -- but I would -- I guess I would say
you got, you know, Charlene here and Carla

00:24:05.200 --> 00:24:06.200
here too.

00:24:06.200 --> 00:24:07.769
Let's take trade, where we worked together.

00:24:07.769 --> 00:24:12.029
You know, in -- it just -- the reality is
unless an executive pushes this, it's not

00:24:12.029 --> 00:24:13.029
going to happen, you know.

00:24:13.029 --> 00:24:18.350
So don't -- you know, and it was actually
kind of amazing that in the case of the three

00:24:18.350 --> 00:24:22.559
FTAs that got done -- of course, that happened
because Congress decided they wanted three,

00:24:22.559 --> 00:24:23.559
not one, done.

00:24:23.559 --> 00:24:25.081
So that was to Congress' benefit.

00:24:25.081 --> 00:24:32.179
But the reason I say this is -- again, I don't
want to get too U.S.-centric here, but I was

00:24:32.179 --> 00:24:37.530
looking at the fact that, you know, with the
Colombia and Panama FTAs done, the United

00:24:37.530 --> 00:24:42.669
States will have FTAs with about 50 percent
of the GDP in the hemisphere, not including

00:24:42.669 --> 00:24:45.470
the U.S., so not a bad starting point.

00:24:45.470 --> 00:24:51.360
And we had tried the FTAA -- Charlene and
I had tried this, and it ran into difficulties

00:24:51.360 --> 00:24:52.360
with Brazil.

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:56.820
The dynamic has changed, in part because of
the rise of China.

00:24:56.820 --> 00:25:01.360
So there could be some interesting opportunities
here in the Western Hemisphere, but they're

00:25:01.360 --> 00:25:03.389
not going to come just out of the sky.

00:25:03.389 --> 00:25:04.910
Somebody is going to have to push them.

00:25:04.910 --> 00:25:08.320
Again, that means organizing -- by the way,
this goes back to the Bank story.

00:25:08.320 --> 00:25:12.240
In the case of the Bank, I organized the Chamber
of Commerce; I organized other groups and

00:25:12.240 --> 00:25:16.830
so on and so forth, so I could go to members
and say, here are people that support it.

00:25:16.830 --> 00:25:20.700
And there is -- there is a -- there are -- there
are -- if you think about the U.S. role in

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:22.991
the world, there's actually a lot of opportunities
out there.

00:25:22.991 --> 00:25:26.519
The United States is still the linchpin on
a lot of this.

00:25:26.519 --> 00:25:28.419
But it -- that doesn't just happen.

00:25:28.419 --> 00:25:32.419
So going back to working with the Congress
on international stuff, you kind of really

00:25:32.419 --> 00:25:36.730
have to -- the -- it's the responsibility
of the executive branch, and then working

00:25:36.730 --> 00:25:38.140
the others levers in the system.

00:25:38.140 --> 00:25:41.429
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I guess I'll ask you ask one last
question, which is close to our intellectual

00:25:41.429 --> 00:25:42.429
--

00:25:42.429 --> 00:25:45.480
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Just to -- well, just to give
you -- just in the spirit, because it might

00:25:45.480 --> 00:25:49.169
of interest of other people here: Take this
"China 2030" report we did.

00:25:49.169 --> 00:25:54.260
You take that report apart, and there's a
good discussion to be had about what China

00:25:54.260 --> 00:25:55.260
will do.

00:25:55.260 --> 00:26:00.519
But there are lots of opportunities there
for -- to change what is kind of the zero-sum

00:26:00.519 --> 00:26:08.320
U.S.-China economic agenda into cross-investment
issues, service industry expansion, energy

00:26:08.320 --> 00:26:11.049
sector, IPR and innovation.

00:26:11.049 --> 00:26:16.529
So you could build off what China itself wants
to do in a win-win fashion.

00:26:16.529 --> 00:26:20.700
So does that maybe make a little bit better
sense than continuing to have the same debates

00:26:20.700 --> 00:26:22.789
that people have over the rather narrow issues?

00:26:22.789 --> 00:26:28.290
But the point is -- I mean, I was actually
pleased Chairman Baucus raised this with the

00:26:28.290 --> 00:26:29.549
USTR in a hearing.

00:26:29.549 --> 00:26:30.549
OK.

00:26:30.549 --> 00:26:33.740
Well, it wasn't accidental, because Chairman
Baucus was interested in this, and I've gone

00:26:33.740 --> 00:26:35.220
up and talked to him about it.

00:26:35.220 --> 00:26:39.970
But to be honest, it should kind of come from
the other side too.

00:26:39.970 --> 00:26:47.210
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Duly noted.

00:26:47.210 --> 00:26:52.020
One thing that's come up here in our talks
this morning is the relationship between efforts

00:26:52.020 --> 00:26:53.579
at the regional and global level.

00:26:53.579 --> 00:26:54.579
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah.

00:26:54.579 --> 00:26:57.179
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Could you just say something about
-- and you got all these regional development

00:26:57.179 --> 00:26:58.710
banks.

00:26:58.710 --> 00:26:59.809
What's the relationship?

00:26:59.809 --> 00:27:02.250
And is there a conscious division of labor?

00:27:02.250 --> 00:27:03.340
How does that work?

00:27:03.340 --> 00:27:04.340
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah.

00:27:04.340 --> 00:27:08.780
Well, you know, my -- this happened in trade
as well.

00:27:08.780 --> 00:27:14.559
I believe multilateralism includes regionalism,
and I think, you know, there's a lot that

00:27:14.559 --> 00:27:19.679
we've learned about while economics are a
component of it, but whether it's immigration,

00:27:19.679 --> 00:27:22.980
whether it's security, whether it's narcotics
-- I mean, the whole history of the European

00:27:22.980 --> 00:27:30.559
Union -- and so people who decry regionalism,
I think, are not recognizing sort of realities.

00:27:30.559 --> 00:27:37.590
And so -- and moreover, the ability to interconnect
these regions in an open regionalism, as was

00:27:37.590 --> 00:27:39.980
discussed in the Asia-Pacific.

00:27:39.980 --> 00:27:44.269
So one of the things -- just a small insight
-- at the bank when I came, which we really

00:27:44.269 --> 00:27:47.980
really weren't doing that much of, is that
-- (I see Yusuf in the audience ?) -- we started

00:27:47.980 --> 00:27:52.450
to work much more with regional institutions
at institutions, ASEAN as an institution,

00:27:52.450 --> 00:27:56.480
in Africa not only the African Union but sort
of regional institutions.

00:27:56.480 --> 00:28:01.039
And so part of our service is to try to understand
how to help their capacity.

00:28:01.039 --> 00:28:05.690
And if you take development issues in sub-Saharan
Africa, for example, they're going to be critical

00:28:05.690 --> 00:28:09.440
to sort of build larger markets and how do
you have the infrastructure and how do you

00:28:09.440 --> 00:28:10.440
reduce the cost.

00:28:10.440 --> 00:28:16.830
Now, in the case of regional development banks,
one aspect of that was that I tried to organize

00:28:16.830 --> 00:28:20.629
in a more coherent fashion our cooperation
with the regional development banks.

00:28:20.629 --> 00:28:24.499
In some cases, they may take the lead; in
some cases, we take the lead; we work over

00:28:24.499 --> 00:28:25.499
that.

00:28:25.499 --> 00:28:30.960
The one part, though, where I think regionalism
could go to far is, remember, I talked about

00:28:30.960 --> 00:28:33.340
a knowledge -- a global knowledge transfer.

00:28:33.340 --> 00:28:37.760
One of the -- where the Bank is at its best
is to -- being able to transfer knowledge

00:28:37.760 --> 00:28:38.779
across regions.

00:28:38.779 --> 00:28:41.559
If you're a regional bank, you don't really
have that facility.

00:28:41.559 --> 00:28:45.070
But they undoubtedly will have capacities
that the World Bank doesn't have.

00:28:45.070 --> 00:28:52.090
So I tend to, in structural terms, look at
the world more as a Venn diagram with intersecting

00:28:52.090 --> 00:28:53.090
circles.

00:28:53.090 --> 00:28:57.889
And so I -- it doesn't -- in a sense, the
-- some people describe multilateralism in

00:28:57.889 --> 00:29:01.619
a(n) old- fashioned -- what I'll call mid-20th-century
hierarchical model.

00:29:01.619 --> 00:29:04.179
I think that's not going to work; it's a mistake.

00:29:04.179 --> 00:29:08.679
And I think it doesn't recognize the diversity
of the international system.

00:29:08.679 --> 00:29:13.029
And I'll just -- I'll give one other little
insight on this that some people here might

00:29:13.029 --> 00:29:14.029
be interested in.

00:29:14.029 --> 00:29:19.720
You know, climate change is an issue that
I first started to work on, actually, when

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:24.350
I helped get the Rio agreement through in
Bush 41's administration.

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:31.110
And the -- what happened in -- with the UNFCCC
process is there's kind of -- talking about

00:29:31.110 --> 00:29:33.779
international governance; I think that was
one of your topics -- there is this view,

00:29:33.779 --> 00:29:39.070
well, we must get all 195 countries to agree
to everything big enough for, you know, climate

00:29:39.070 --> 00:29:40.749
change (for the world ?) all at once.

00:29:40.749 --> 00:29:41.749
Well, good freaking luck.

00:29:41.749 --> 00:29:47.409
You know, and so to be honest, you know, I've
been part of more agreements and -- bilateral,

00:29:47.409 --> 00:29:52.409
multilateral than probably most people, and
I -- so I can know what they take to get done.

00:29:52.409 --> 00:29:54.700
But I'll give you a very practical aspect
to this.

00:29:54.700 --> 00:30:00.299
I went down to see President Calderon in Mexico
about six or nine months before the Cancun

00:30:00.299 --> 00:30:03.999
meeting, and I outlined this "weltanschauung."

00:30:03.999 --> 00:30:05.549
And then I said, you know, what I would do?

00:30:05.549 --> 00:30:11.289
I said, here's individual papers on building
blocks: energy efficiency, technology, avoided

00:30:11.289 --> 00:30:14.519
deforestation, less plant soil carbon.

00:30:14.519 --> 00:30:18.419
And if you actually go back and look at the
real agreement, it was that countries were

00:30:18.419 --> 00:30:19.980
supposed to build national action plans.

00:30:19.980 --> 00:30:24.950
I said, so what if you have 150 countries
agree on combined national action plans?

00:30:24.950 --> 00:30:27.299
So you don't have 190, but you got 150.

00:30:27.299 --> 00:30:33.450
And that's exactly the model that they followed,
and I -- that's the reason they were successful.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:38.600
And ironically, the more that you don't allow
one country to hold it up, the more you're

00:30:38.600 --> 00:30:40.779
actually going to have a chance of getting
it done.

00:30:40.779 --> 00:30:45.879
And the key on this was then I went to see
him the week of the final meeting, and I said,

00:30:45.879 --> 00:30:50.529
Mr. President, I said, you know, you probably
think that consensus means all countries.

00:30:50.529 --> 00:30:51.529
(Laughs.)

00:30:51.529 --> 00:30:55.480
I said, go talk to your lawyers in the Foreign
Ministry because there's varying views of

00:30:55.480 --> 00:30:56.480
consensus.

00:30:56.480 --> 00:30:59.999
And I said, you might find one country, like
Bolivia, that decides it wants to hold you

00:30:59.999 --> 00:31:03.850
up after you've got everything else all lined
up for whatever reason they have.

00:31:03.850 --> 00:31:08.710
And I said, so, you know, be prepared to think
of a way you can describe consensus without

00:31:08.710 --> 00:31:11.119
a country, and it was one of these forecaster
-- (inaudible).

00:31:11.119 --> 00:31:12.980
It was Bolivia that tried to hold them up.

00:31:12.980 --> 00:31:18.320
So the -- I emphasize these because, as you
-- as Richard and I both know, there are views

00:31:18.320 --> 00:31:22.480
in America that say -- that kind of says,
oh, well, you can't make any of these multilateral

00:31:22.480 --> 00:31:24.320
systems work, and they're sort of imperfect.

00:31:24.320 --> 00:31:26.999
I think that's a terrible mistake.

00:31:26.999 --> 00:31:32.779
But, on the other hand, I constantly find
myself having to sort of issue -- kind of

00:31:32.779 --> 00:31:38.790
argue a pragmatism or realism against those
who argue, you know, a rather utopian view.

00:31:38.790 --> 00:31:41.129
&gt;&gt; HAASS: All or nothing, you may end up with
nothing.

00:31:41.129 --> 00:31:46.490
Why don't we open it up and, again, just wait
for a microphone, and just introduce yourself,

00:31:46.490 --> 00:31:52.539
your name and your institution, and toss up
a difficult question for Bob.

00:31:52.539 --> 00:31:56.690
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Or an easy one.

00:31:56.690 --> 00:31:57.690
(Laughter.)

00:31:57.690 --> 00:32:02.669
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Or an easy one, if you insist.

00:32:02.669 --> 00:32:03.669
Sure.

00:32:03.669 --> 00:32:04.669
Let's start here.

00:32:04.669 --> 00:32:05.669
QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:32:05.669 --> 00:32:07.669
My name is Kuseni (Dlamini ?) from the South
African Institute of International Affairs.

00:32:07.669 --> 00:32:11.190
My question has to do with the World Bank
and its leadership.

00:32:11.190 --> 00:32:17.409
There've been some debates around the process
that's used to choose the head of the Bank.

00:32:17.409 --> 00:32:23.940
I'll be interested to hear your views, Bob,
as you're about to leave this very important

00:32:23.940 --> 00:32:26.489
institution and having done such a great job.

00:32:26.489 --> 00:32:32.830
Do you think we should move away from the
established practice of having America decide

00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:38.480
and having Europe decide on the IMF and allow
the emerging powers from emerging markets

00:32:38.480 --> 00:32:40.669
to also contest for the position?

00:32:40.669 --> 00:32:46.869
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, the process has gotten
better in the sense that our board -- our

00:32:46.869 --> 00:32:52.369
board meets and decides that there are certain
characteristics that they're looking for and

00:32:52.369 --> 00:32:55.460
whatever nominations will go forward.

00:32:55.460 --> 00:32:58.029
People will have to present their case and
so on and so forth.

00:32:58.029 --> 00:33:03.600
But the United States has 15 percent of the
votes and Europe has a larger amount, so that's

00:33:03.600 --> 00:33:06.859
the kabal that could guide it in the past.

00:33:06.859 --> 00:33:10.659
Let me start this level.

00:33:10.659 --> 00:33:19.659
I think it is actually critical for the institutions
to broaden their engagement at multiple levels

00:33:19.659 --> 00:33:24.369
with emerging markets, but it's -- and a lot
of this focused on voting shares, OK?

00:33:24.369 --> 00:33:26.820
And we made adjustments in voting shares.

00:33:26.820 --> 00:33:31.840
I honestly think sometimes that becomes an
overstated argument.

00:33:31.840 --> 00:33:32.919
So let me start with this.

00:33:32.919 --> 00:33:38.129
One thing is, we added a chair for sub-Saharan
Africa on our board, another chair, which

00:33:38.129 --> 00:33:40.480
actually South Africa and Nigeria share.

00:33:40.480 --> 00:33:45.249
And because we don't really take votes, that
was probably more important than kind of a

00:33:45.249 --> 00:33:46.289
voting adjustment.

00:33:46.289 --> 00:33:48.830
Second is how you run the institution.

00:33:48.830 --> 00:33:53.609
So yeah, you can actually see some of the
commentary about the Bank now is that I was

00:33:53.609 --> 00:33:57.389
-- been very much driven by a client-driven
approach, what are the clients interested

00:33:57.389 --> 00:33:59.720
in, and then share the knowledge, so on and
so forth.

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:05.269
The -- interestingly, some people who actually
would come more from what I'll call the left-of-center

00:34:05.269 --> 00:34:08.791
side have what I'll call the monopolist view
that -- you know, that you should do this,

00:34:08.791 --> 00:34:09.791
this and this.

00:34:09.791 --> 00:34:12.520
That's actually, I think, what the Bank needs
to get away from.

00:34:12.520 --> 00:34:15.580
So -- and then another aspect is the staffing.

00:34:15.580 --> 00:34:19.429
And as you probably know, I brought -- about
half the officers are now women and in much

00:34:19.429 --> 00:34:22.200
larger numbers from the emerging markets.

00:34:22.200 --> 00:34:24.850
Then it just leads to the question on the
U.S.

00:34:24.850 --> 00:34:27.010
And I guess I'll pose -- for the presidency.

00:34:27.010 --> 00:34:30.140
I'll just pose this part.

00:34:30.140 --> 00:34:32.960
Some people pose this question to me in an
interesting way.

00:34:32.960 --> 00:34:37.860
They said, is it time for the U.S., you know,
not to be president of the Bank anymore, OK?

00:34:37.860 --> 00:34:41.620
And that's an interesting perspective because
it would suggest, in a merit-based system,

00:34:41.620 --> 00:34:44.830
that an American cannot run the Bank, OK?

00:34:44.830 --> 00:34:50.850
And here's where I guess I'll -- going to
twist this in a little bit different way.

00:34:50.850 --> 00:34:55.690
As someone who's worked a lot with multilateral
institutions and tried to get the United States

00:34:55.690 --> 00:35:00.340
to work more effectively in that system, I
think it's very important that the United

00:35:00.340 --> 00:35:04.970
States have the responsibility of running
some.

00:35:04.970 --> 00:35:10.180
And I'm not going to say which ones, but I
will say this: There's never been an American

00:35:10.180 --> 00:35:15.340
who's headed the WTO; there's never been an
American who's a U.N. secretary-general; there's

00:35:15.340 --> 00:35:20.000
never going to be an American who is going
to be head of the regional development banks.

00:35:20.000 --> 00:35:24.760
So you know, there's -- never had an American
who's headed the IMF.

00:35:24.760 --> 00:35:28.980
So if you're going to change the whole thing,
all I'm saying is somewhere in that system,

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:32.880
I think it's good for the United States to
bear the responsibility of the multilateral

00:35:32.880 --> 00:35:37.020
system because, going back a little bit to
what Richard said, and some of my colleagues

00:35:37.020 --> 00:35:42.820
in the room here, I've found that my being
in that role actually compels people to engage

00:35:42.820 --> 00:35:45.590
with these institutions more than they might
otherwise.

00:35:45.590 --> 00:35:50.060
So I won't necessarily say which one and others,
but be careful, if you push the U.S. out of

00:35:50.060 --> 00:35:52.620
this system, what the net effect will be.

00:35:52.620 --> 00:35:56.430
&gt;&gt; HAASS: (Off mic.)

00:35:56.430 --> 00:36:00.230
QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:36:00.230 --> 00:36:06.230
Gilad Sher (ph), from the Institute for National
Security Studies in Israel, and excuse me

00:36:06.230 --> 00:36:08.190
for being a bit region- oriented.

00:36:08.190 --> 00:36:09.190
MR.

00:36:09.190 --> 00:36:10.190
: (Laughs.)

00:36:10.190 --> 00:36:11.190
QUESTIONER: In the absence of the --

00:36:11.190 --> 00:36:12.190
MR.

00:36:12.190 --> 00:36:13.190
: What's the World Bank going to do about
the Iranian nuclear program?

00:36:13.190 --> 00:36:14.190
(Laughter.)

00:36:14.190 --> 00:36:15.190
QUESTIONER: No --

00:36:15.190 --> 00:36:16.190
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: I was just at the Herzliya Conference.

00:36:16.190 --> 00:36:17.190
(Laughter.)

00:36:17.190 --> 00:36:18.190
I know the topic du jour.

00:36:18.190 --> 00:36:23.990
QUESTIONER: In the absence of any substantive
negotiation process between Israel and the

00:36:23.990 --> 00:36:31.310
Palestinians, what role do you see for the
World Bank in the Israel-Palestinian arena

00:36:31.310 --> 00:36:34.260
in particular and in the changing Middle East
in general?

00:36:34.260 --> 00:36:35.260
Thank you.

00:36:35.260 --> 00:36:36.260
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah.

00:36:36.260 --> 00:36:37.260
Very important one.

00:36:37.260 --> 00:36:44.340
Well, I've had the good fortune that Prime
Minister Fayyad in the Palestinian Authority

00:36:44.340 --> 00:36:49.530
is somebody I've got the highest respect for,
and I think he's got actually a very sound

00:36:49.530 --> 00:36:53.540
economic and political model, which is he's
trying to build the state from the bottom

00:36:53.540 --> 00:36:56.290
up.

00:36:56.290 --> 00:37:01.150
And because the Palestinian Authority is not
a member of the World Bank, I actually -- to

00:37:01.150 --> 00:37:07.300
support (by ?) them, we have to make a special
allocation out of our income, which we do.

00:37:07.300 --> 00:37:10.560
But we also, as we do in other areas, run
sort of trust funds.

00:37:10.560 --> 00:37:15.400
So one of the problems in the developing world
is people may have good intentions, but if

00:37:15.400 --> 00:37:19.810
they have 50 or a hundred different programs
for one small authority, you overwhelm it.

00:37:19.810 --> 00:37:22.780
So we will coordinate with that authority.

00:37:22.780 --> 00:37:30.250
So I think Fayyad has done a very important
job of building the institutions of governments,

00:37:30.250 --> 00:37:36.010
more transparency, fighting corruption, building
capabilities that, at least from my conversations

00:37:36.010 --> 00:37:40.380
with Israelis, are probably important for
Israeli security as well as economic development.

00:37:40.380 --> 00:37:44.480
So I certainly think it's short-sighted -- (chuckles)
-- when people do things like cut off the

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:49.790
money flow of their own tax revenues for those
institutions, and I've said that to some of

00:37:49.790 --> 00:37:50.790
the Israeli authorities.

00:37:50.790 --> 00:37:55.650
And I think the Israelis, at least the vast
majority, recognize it too.

00:37:55.650 --> 00:38:06.710
And I think the bigger issue there also is
one of contesting in an open fashion kind

00:38:06.710 --> 00:38:09.010
of a modernization agenda.

00:38:09.010 --> 00:38:12.770
So I -- when I went to the Herzliya Conference,
I also went to see Fayyad in Ramallah; I thought

00:38:12.770 --> 00:38:15.020
it was a good thing to do.

00:38:15.020 --> 00:38:20.720
I have no idea what will happen, but he's
using modern communication -- Facebook, social

00:38:20.720 --> 00:38:25.800
media and other things, things his kids have
taught him -- to engage Palestinians -- public

00:38:25.800 --> 00:38:29.810
-- on these issues, to take a sense of responsibility.

00:38:29.810 --> 00:38:37.330
And he believes, actually, that in an electoral
sense, that what he stands for could do better

00:38:37.330 --> 00:38:41.340
than some people think, because he's been
actually trying to build the base up.

00:38:41.340 --> 00:38:47.320
Now, the real problem there -- and this is
one where I can observe; I don't -- I can't

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:51.900
say with great specificity -- much of the
growth in the Palestinian Authority has been

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:53.710
developed through public sector funding.

00:38:53.710 --> 00:38:56.120
He's trying to wean it off public sector funding.

00:38:56.120 --> 00:38:57.850
You need more of a private sector development.

00:38:57.850 --> 00:39:03.280
And it's going to be very hard to get the
private sector development with some of the

00:39:03.280 --> 00:39:04.780
movement restrictions.

00:39:04.780 --> 00:39:08.480
On the other hand, I understand the security
logic for some of those movement restrictions.

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:14.410
But I think if it were up to me, I'd be testing
the border of what more I could do on movement

00:39:14.410 --> 00:39:19.870
to allow a greater sense of private sector
development and, frankly, personal dignity

00:39:19.870 --> 00:39:23.990
that will be the important part of building
that state and capacity.

00:39:23.990 --> 00:39:33.830
And I guess the -- what this would also suggest,
as I saw -- Dennis Ross, a former colleague,

00:39:33.830 --> 00:39:39.930
wrote an op-ed not too long ago about whether
one could open up other sections of the West

00:39:39.930 --> 00:39:43.410
Bank to be able to encourage this sort of
development.

00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:45.680
And the logic would lead me into that direction.

00:39:45.680 --> 00:39:49.730
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Do you want to say something about
the broader Middle East that would say that

00:39:49.730 --> 00:39:54.030
where you had regime changes -- places like
Egypt, Tunisia, Libya -- what sort of a role

00:39:54.030 --> 00:39:55.570
the bank is playing?

00:39:55.570 --> 00:40:02.390
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, you know, one of the other
things -- this gives you a little sense of

00:40:02.390 --> 00:40:06.450
how I try to guide the bank -- we need to
learn too, OK.

00:40:06.450 --> 00:40:09.500
So we've got a lot of smart people, but the
world changes and so on.

00:40:09.500 --> 00:40:13.550
We're trying to learn our own lessons from
this, I mean, not the least of which was,

00:40:13.550 --> 00:40:20.020
you know, an Egypt that was growing well and
frankly had a pretty good economic team, but

00:40:20.020 --> 00:40:24.120
nevertheless that wasn't sufficient -- and
this goes to some of these issues of dignity

00:40:24.120 --> 00:40:31.580
and also kind of, I think, a system whereby
you need to combine economic growth with a

00:40:31.580 --> 00:40:36.079
sense that people (feel ?), well, it's not
crony- and favoritism and others.

00:40:36.079 --> 00:40:41.180
What this has led us to is the idea of trying
to work with governments to build on their

00:40:41.180 --> 00:40:48.620
own movements to have greater openness, transparency,
social accountability, and this again connects

00:40:48.620 --> 00:40:49.620
from other regions.

00:40:49.620 --> 00:40:57.310
So what you now have with modern telecommunications
is the ability to have, you know, people in

00:40:57.310 --> 00:41:03.710
villages with cellphones be able to comment
and guide about the delivery of services.

00:41:03.710 --> 00:41:07.760
(Right ?). And this is -- this opens up not
only anti-corruption things, but it opens

00:41:07.760 --> 00:41:11.460
up a challenge for sort of bureaucratic performance.

00:41:11.460 --> 00:41:17.680
So with countries such as Tunisia and Morocco,
we've been trying to take those steps and

00:41:17.680 --> 00:41:18.680
encourage them.

00:41:18.680 --> 00:41:24.620
And that will be a big issue for us with Egypt,
and -- because we have certain programs that

00:41:24.620 --> 00:41:30.010
are of investment projects in agriculture
or energy or others, but the bank sometimes

00:41:30.010 --> 00:41:31.660
gives what's called development policy loans.

00:41:31.660 --> 00:41:36.620
This is money that goes to the budgets, but
it's based on various policy changes.

00:41:36.620 --> 00:41:41.950
And it's my own view that those need to include
these types of social accountability and openness

00:41:41.950 --> 00:41:43.670
and transparency measures.

00:41:43.670 --> 00:41:45.950
And it's things that we're applying to the
bank as well.

00:41:45.950 --> 00:41:50.390
So -- and just to give you one other connection,
because -- something probably many people

00:41:50.390 --> 00:41:54.910
may not be aware of, but with your research
institutes, I really hope you are -- one of

00:41:54.910 --> 00:42:00.220
the -- one of the most lasting things that
we've done at the bank during my time is we

00:42:00.220 --> 00:42:02.610
created an Open Data initiative.

00:42:02.610 --> 00:42:08.350
So we've taken all our data sets going back
decades and made them for -- available for

00:42:08.350 --> 00:42:09.930
free on the Web.

00:42:09.930 --> 00:42:11.970
And there's this interesting little bureaucratic
story behind this.

00:42:11.970 --> 00:42:12.970
(Chuckles.)

00:42:12.970 --> 00:42:17.340
I used to go to meetings, and I'd have, you
know, some slightly wizened professor come

00:42:17.340 --> 00:42:20.590
up to me and say, oh, you've got some great
data sets, but you still charge for them.

00:42:20.590 --> 00:42:24.160
And I'd go back to my economic staff, just
as you would, and say, why are we charging

00:42:24.160 --> 00:42:25.160
for them?

00:42:25.160 --> 00:42:27.130
And they'd say, well, we add value, and we
need to get compensated.

00:42:27.130 --> 00:42:31.130
You know, it took me about three rounds to
realize it was, like, a $3 million offset

00:42:31.130 --> 00:42:34.480
to their budget, right, or -- so -- and so
finally, we said, OK, we're going to wipe

00:42:34.480 --> 00:42:37.100
this out, and we're going to open all this
up.

00:42:37.100 --> 00:42:42.060
And then we started to get our economic staff
to work on different applications to make

00:42:42.060 --> 00:42:43.060
it easier to access.

00:42:43.060 --> 00:42:46.220
And we created an Apps for Development competition.

00:42:46.220 --> 00:42:50.450
So we opened up to the software community
and said, use our data, and the only requirement

00:42:50.450 --> 00:42:54.280
is relate it to the Millennium Development
Goals and use our data.

00:42:54.280 --> 00:42:59.890
And so we came up with mapping, games, other
things that you could never, you know, even

00:42:59.890 --> 00:43:02.510
with a very brilliant staff, have been able
to produce.

00:43:02.510 --> 00:43:06.890
What I'm finding is, as is often with an idea
like this, it starts to drive a whole series

00:43:06.890 --> 00:43:08.340
of other possibilities.

00:43:08.340 --> 00:43:15.170
So for example, when we're working with countries
on gender, another great possibility for growth,

00:43:15.170 --> 00:43:18.940
it's a little hard to know the effect of the
policies if people don't keep gender statistics,

00:43:18.940 --> 00:43:23.610
or beyond-border trade barriers -- getting
beyond the quotas and the tariffs to some

00:43:23.610 --> 00:43:27.770
of the logistics and the customs systems -- unless
you have the information, you can't do it.

00:43:27.770 --> 00:43:32.400
So this notion of -- that we've talked about
of democratizing development -- so it's the

00:43:32.400 --> 00:43:37.600
exact opposite of the monopolist model, but
openness, transparency -- and the relevance

00:43:37.600 --> 00:43:43.570
to the broader Middle East is that I think
this is going to be -- this is going to take

00:43:43.570 --> 00:43:48.560
awhile -- (chuckles) -- but I think building
in this concept of openness and engagement

00:43:48.560 --> 00:43:55.280
for the society and the government is important,
as well was for the Bank.

00:43:55.280 --> 00:44:00.910
QUESTIONER: Michael Emerson, Centre for European
Policy Studies in Brussels.

00:44:00.910 --> 00:44:02.590
Palestinian question.

00:44:02.590 --> 00:44:05.110
You mentioned Morocco.

00:44:05.110 --> 00:44:10.480
I wonder whether you could broaden this out
to talk a bit about the response to the Arab

00:44:10.480 --> 00:44:15.400
Spring, or let us call it the Arab political
earthquake, that happened last year.

00:44:15.400 --> 00:44:24.780
I'm sure that you've been faced with the task
of defining the World Bank's strategic response

00:44:24.780 --> 00:44:32.840
to this huge strategic event, earthquake,
and I'm wondering what conclusions you -- you

00:44:32.840 --> 00:44:33.840
came to.

00:44:33.840 --> 00:44:40.591
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, just to build on a couple
of the points I said: Number one, development

00:44:40.591 --> 00:44:42.440
has to be owned by the local people.

00:44:42.440 --> 00:44:44.040
So -- and this is a lesson from everywhere.

00:44:44.040 --> 00:44:48.340
You could have great money, ideas, so on and
so forth, but so this goes to the governance

00:44:48.340 --> 00:44:49.340
issue.

00:44:49.340 --> 00:44:54.440
So you have to build your development strategies
with the evolution of the governance topic.

00:44:54.440 --> 00:44:57.010
And obviously, you have countries at different
states.

00:44:57.010 --> 00:45:01.690
So some have had a revolution and are on the
way of developing a political system.

00:45:01.690 --> 00:45:07.580
Tunisia, Morocco and Jordan have sort of a
fast-paced evolution, OK?

00:45:07.580 --> 00:45:11.290
Egypt is -- I mean, I don't know, coup, partial
revolution.

00:45:11.290 --> 00:45:13.530
I mean, I can't tell yet, right?

00:45:13.530 --> 00:45:21.690
And so the key point is, we have to recognize
the local ownership but also that it may be

00:45:21.690 --> 00:45:26.850
going through phases of development, and then
what can we share -- and this is the important

00:45:26.850 --> 00:45:28.130
part about the south- south.

00:45:28.130 --> 00:45:32.270
So it's not just U.S. or the European, but
some of the ideas of what's worked with other

00:45:32.270 --> 00:45:33.550
developing countries.

00:45:33.550 --> 00:45:37.350
So if you work in this area, I'm sure you've
encountered there's a huge, huge interest

00:45:37.350 --> 00:45:42.150
in Turkish economic development, but also
there's an interest in some of the other models

00:45:42.150 --> 00:45:44.830
from East Asia as we go forward.

00:45:44.830 --> 00:45:49.950
And so what can we share about safety net
systems, governance systems, transparency

00:45:49.950 --> 00:45:50.950
systems?

00:45:50.950 --> 00:45:56.420
Then, as you get to other countries, in the
Gulf, for example, that don't necessarily

00:45:56.420 --> 00:46:01.290
need our money, or let's take Libya as a -- we
had the Libyan prime minister here.

00:46:01.290 --> 00:46:08.080
They don't need our money, but what they will
need is the ability to frankly develop public

00:46:08.080 --> 00:46:13.430
financial management systems so that the money
is effectively used, so it's not stolen, and

00:46:13.430 --> 00:46:17.200
more effective -- more importantly, how do
you avoid the resource curse?

00:46:17.200 --> 00:46:20.580
So this is an issue in much of the development
in sub-Saharan Africa.

00:46:20.580 --> 00:46:25.580
Many countries have great natural resources,
but how do you do it in a way that has inclusive

00:46:25.580 --> 00:46:30.490
growth, avoids the Dutch disease on the exchange
rate and sort of builds for a longer term?

00:46:30.490 --> 00:46:35.950
So we're working -- you know, we do some just
-- we get paid sometimes for some of our knowledge

00:46:35.950 --> 00:46:40.940
work with some of the Gulf countries, not
so we don't do loans, to be able to share

00:46:40.940 --> 00:46:41.990
that information.

00:46:41.990 --> 00:46:45.010
And this is, again, the neat thing about what's
happening in the world.

00:46:45.010 --> 00:46:49.490
You're finding that there's more and more
countries that now -- for example, I was thinking

00:46:49.490 --> 00:46:53.740
about the Gulf -- some of them are looking
at -- on Singapore as a logistics model.

00:46:53.740 --> 00:46:56.500
I'm going to be speaking to the Panamanians
here in town in a couple days.

00:46:56.500 --> 00:46:58.360
They're trying to develop this as a model.

00:46:58.360 --> 00:47:03.500
So I think it's a very healthy think that
people are sort of looking more broadly, and

00:47:03.500 --> 00:47:09.410
if we can share the information about choices,
and that there's an increasing number from

00:47:09.410 --> 00:47:13.350
the developing world and being able to draw
on it.

00:47:13.350 --> 00:47:17.040
This wasn't your question, but I want to -- I'll
take it one step further, because that's sort

00:47:17.040 --> 00:47:20.550
of -- I find it intriguing and so may some
of you.

00:47:20.550 --> 00:47:23.380
You know, as China's invested in sub-Saharan
Africa, there's been a lot of focus on resource

00:47:23.380 --> 00:47:24.400
development or infrastructure or others.

00:47:24.400 --> 00:47:32.910
And literally there's a small publication
we have come out, and I had -- was -- in discussion

00:47:32.910 --> 00:47:38.750
I had in China in recently, in Guangdong,
we were talking about it.

00:47:38.750 --> 00:47:42.150
China has about 85 million low-wage manufacturing
jobs.

00:47:42.150 --> 00:47:45.760
They got to move up the value added chain
if they want higher wages, they're going to

00:47:45.760 --> 00:47:50.480
have higher wages, and part of their whole
strategy is increased productivity, higher

00:47:50.480 --> 00:47:51.480
wages.

00:47:51.480 --> 00:47:55.710
Sub-Saharan Africa has about 8 million of
those jobs, North Africa maybe 1 (million)

00:47:55.710 --> 00:47:58.289
to 2 million, so you got about 10 million
of those jobs.

00:47:58.289 --> 00:48:04.150
We're in discussions with the Chinese, both
officials and companies, to say what is it

00:48:04.150 --> 00:48:08.620
that we could do to help the enabling environment
-- you know, ports, energy, infrastructure,

00:48:08.620 --> 00:48:13.520
customs rules, and also maybe IFC private
investment, to help some of that low-wage

00:48:13.520 --> 00:48:15.730
manufacturing move to sub-Saharan Africa?

00:48:15.730 --> 00:48:20.720
Because if you have 85 million versus 8 (million)
to 10 million, even if you just get 5 million,

00:48:20.720 --> 00:48:23.210
you've increased it by 50 percent.

00:48:23.210 --> 00:48:26.050
So I say that not that it's going to happen
overnight.

00:48:26.050 --> 00:48:31.320
And when I was in Guangdong province talking
to the provincial party secretary about this,

00:48:31.320 --> 00:48:35.850
my former colleague, Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala,
who is now the Nigerian economic coordinating

00:48:35.850 --> 00:48:38.770
minister, had been there visiting companies
that developed this.

00:48:38.770 --> 00:48:43.070
It will require some interesting intersectoral,
interministerial work in sub-Saharan Africa

00:48:43.070 --> 00:48:44.070
to make it happen.

00:48:44.070 --> 00:48:50.320
But it just gives you a sense of how fast
this is changing and if the bank as an institution

00:48:50.320 --> 00:48:55.160
can help prod it or push it or interconnect
it, you could have bit payoffs economically,

00:48:55.160 --> 00:48:58.730
politically and in stability terms.

00:48:58.730 --> 00:49:00.370
QUESTIONER: (Inaudible.)

00:49:00.370 --> 00:49:02.830
QUESTIONER: (Off mic.)

00:49:02.830 --> 00:49:05.700
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Is this my euro zone question?

00:49:05.700 --> 00:49:06.730
Don't I get a euro zone question?

00:49:06.730 --> 00:49:07.730
(Laughter.)

00:49:07.730 --> 00:49:08.770
QUESTIONER: No, no, no, it's not a euro zone.

00:49:08.770 --> 00:49:12.980
But you can't answer a question that hasn't
been asked to you.

00:49:12.980 --> 00:49:14.650
That's typically what de Gaulle used to do.

00:49:14.650 --> 00:49:16.319
No, in the last --

00:49:16.319 --> 00:49:17.319
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: (Inaudible.)

00:49:17.319 --> 00:49:18.319
(Laughs.)

00:49:18.319 --> 00:49:24.400
QUESTIONER: In the last five years or so,
the IMF has been at the forefront of all debates

00:49:24.400 --> 00:49:31.700
on the global, economic and financial governance,
and this not only because of mistrust or scandal.

00:49:31.700 --> 00:49:39.520
Apparently, the World Bank has been less on
the forefront of the debate, less visible.

00:49:39.520 --> 00:49:46.020
So my question is, how do you assess today
the importance of the role of the World Bank

00:49:46.020 --> 00:49:50.320
in the global economic governments debate?

00:49:50.320 --> 00:49:55.350
And to put it more sharply, could we live
without the World Bank?

00:49:55.350 --> 00:50:01.530
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, you know, you -- your -- (even
?) -- you're such a strategic thinker, Terry

00:50:01.530 --> 00:50:03.460
(sp), your perspective is a little short term.

00:50:03.460 --> 00:50:04.460
(Laughter.)

00:50:04.460 --> 00:50:08.520
Actually five years ago, we were kind of in
the news too at the bank on some governance

00:50:08.520 --> 00:50:09.520
issues.

00:50:09.520 --> 00:50:10.520
(Laughter continues.)

00:50:10.520 --> 00:50:12.690
So, to be honest, I take this as a --

00:50:12.690 --> 00:50:13.690
MR.

00:50:13.690 --> 00:50:14.690
HAASS (?): (Laughs.)

00:50:14.690 --> 00:50:15.690
(Inaudible) -- indirect compliment for the
--

00:50:15.690 --> 00:50:17.410
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: -- to stewardship.

00:50:17.410 --> 00:50:21.850
No, the World Bank has been very much a part
of this as well.

00:50:21.850 --> 00:50:27.050
I think the IMF -- and actually, five years
ago, the IMF was more -- there was as a sense

00:50:27.050 --> 00:50:28.770
of, what's its role in the system?

00:50:28.770 --> 00:50:34.870
But the international crisis, with its macroeconomic
nature, you know, sort of pushed that to the

00:50:34.870 --> 00:50:35.920
forefront.

00:50:35.920 --> 00:50:43.970
As for whether you could live without a World
Bank, I think the title of my article is,

00:50:43.970 --> 00:50:45.090
"Why We Need the World Bank."

00:50:45.090 --> 00:50:46.090
So you got to -- (inaudible) --

00:50:46.090 --> 00:50:47.090
&gt;&gt; HAASS: "Why We Still Need" it.

00:50:47.090 --> 00:50:48.580
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: -- "Why We Still Need the World
Bank" -- but it really goes to this point

00:50:48.580 --> 00:50:54.810
that I've touched on, Terry (sp), which is,
is that I think that the international system

00:50:54.810 --> 00:51:00.700
actually has a dearth of these intermediating
institutions.

00:51:00.700 --> 00:51:06.390
And so -- and you know, so we have one in
the IMF and -- so we have one in trade, and

00:51:06.390 --> 00:51:10.540
we have one in macroeconomic, and we have
one in sort of microeconomic structural.

00:51:10.540 --> 00:51:14.710
You've got the U.N. system, some of which
is political, some of which is some very valuable

00:51:14.710 --> 00:51:15.710
operational arms.

00:51:15.710 --> 00:51:17.560
You've got some of the regional loan banks.

00:51:17.560 --> 00:51:20.670
In some cases, you have sort of military alliance.

00:51:20.670 --> 00:51:25.300
But given the nature of the interdependency
and interconnected problems -- and what I

00:51:25.300 --> 00:51:31.190
think will be the big issue for the rest of
our time, which is the fast rise of emerging

00:51:31.190 --> 00:51:38.270
markets and powers in the system -- I think
it helps enormously to have structures, institutions,

00:51:38.270 --> 00:51:44.820
regimes, whatever you want to describe them
as, that can play a mediating role to try

00:51:44.820 --> 00:51:47.920
to come together cooperatively on problems.

00:51:47.920 --> 00:51:52.460
And let me just take two dimensions of this.

00:51:52.460 --> 00:51:55.220
And I bet Carla and Charlene might have this
sense too.

00:51:55.220 --> 00:52:01.240
You know, this is sort of serendipitous, but
I became USTR in 2001, and now it's 2012.

00:52:01.240 --> 00:52:02.830
So it's a little bit over a decade.

00:52:02.830 --> 00:52:06.470
In historical terms, that's a rather short
period of time.

00:52:06.470 --> 00:52:13.060
And so I had the USTR, the State Department,
briefly at Goldman, then at the bank.

00:52:13.060 --> 00:52:21.120
What I have seen in terms of the role of emerging
markets, development, trade, investment, you

00:52:21.120 --> 00:52:26.130
know, climate change and, I think, increasingly,
security (issues ?), it's just ramped up enormously,

00:52:26.130 --> 00:52:27.130
OK?

00:52:27.130 --> 00:52:31.860
And so it's hard for me to conceive of an
issue now that can be addressed without their

00:52:31.860 --> 00:52:33.120
engagement and involvement.

00:52:33.120 --> 00:52:38.260
But -- and this is the big "but" -- the -- a
number of those countries, understandably,

00:52:38.260 --> 00:52:39.780
are still relatively poor.

00:52:39.780 --> 00:52:45.280
And so they want -- they're concerned about
having all the responsibilities of the developed

00:52:45.280 --> 00:52:46.280
countries.

00:52:46.280 --> 00:52:49.750
But then we had this economic crisis, so then
the developed countries are worried about

00:52:49.750 --> 00:52:50.970
sort of their sense of responsibility.

00:52:50.970 --> 00:52:55.960
So I know if you're going to discuss this
in your governance session, how you get that,

00:52:55.960 --> 00:53:00.390
quote, right -- you know, and we were talking
a little bit before as we were just coming

00:53:00.390 --> 00:53:07.050
in -- you know, the Kagan view of do you need
sort of a prime player to guide the system,

00:53:07.050 --> 00:53:10.040
which was a little bit -- in the economic
sphere, it was the Kindleberger view about

00:53:10.040 --> 00:53:11.850
the causes of the Great Depression.

00:53:11.850 --> 00:53:15.900
Britain was used to world leadership, no longer
had the capabilities in the '20s and '30s;

00:53:15.900 --> 00:53:18.610
U.S. had capabilities, wasn't used to the
leadership.

00:53:18.610 --> 00:53:22.970
How does the change in this system -- or the
Eikenberry view that the rules and norms of

00:53:22.970 --> 00:53:27.200
the international order are in place enough,
and so you -- that's going to be a very big

00:53:27.200 --> 00:53:28.200
topic.

00:53:28.200 --> 00:53:31.300
But what I've seen in a practical way and
what you'll see in the things I've written

00:53:31.300 --> 00:53:37.490
is it's fun to talk about this stuff, but
the reality is then you have to do it, OK?

00:53:37.490 --> 00:53:43.030
And so the best way to make it -- to make
a difference is to show how you can bring

00:53:43.030 --> 00:53:44.500
parties together.

00:53:44.500 --> 00:53:48.050
I have 187 shareholders -- (chuckles) -- in
very different countries.

00:53:48.050 --> 00:53:53.560
But you know, you could look at the record
on things from, you know, climate change to

00:53:53.560 --> 00:54:00.580
trade and structural reform and new capital
infusion, $90 billion raised for the poorest

00:54:00.580 --> 00:54:01.580
countries for IDA.

00:54:01.580 --> 00:54:05.630
We're able to bring people together in the
process.

00:54:05.630 --> 00:54:09.130
And if you didn't have the institution, that
wouldn't happen.

00:54:09.130 --> 00:54:12.210
And again, let me give you another practical
one from the climate area.

00:54:12.210 --> 00:54:18.000
You know, Hank Paulson, when he was secretary
of the Treasury, you know, came to me and

00:54:18.000 --> 00:54:21.890
we had this idea about creating global climate
investment funds.

00:54:21.890 --> 00:54:27.710
And so he and I went and got about $6 billion
of contributions from primarily developed

00:54:27.710 --> 00:54:28.930
countries, in different forms.

00:54:28.930 --> 00:54:30.830
It's gone up a little bit.

00:54:30.830 --> 00:54:35.470
And we created some for technology, some for
energy efficiency, some for adaptation.

00:54:35.470 --> 00:54:40.300
We've been able to leverage that to about
$50 billion and about 30 to 40 percent from

00:54:40.300 --> 00:54:45.010
private sector of that 50 billion (dollars),
the other from other bank and regional bank

00:54:45.010 --> 00:54:47.470
resources and so on and so forth.

00:54:47.470 --> 00:54:54.190
And equally important, we've now had sort
of projects launched in about 45 emerging

00:54:54.190 --> 00:54:58.690
market countries, and every time you launch
one of these projects, their investment in

00:54:58.690 --> 00:55:01.900
the climate change issue becomes somewhat
different.

00:55:01.900 --> 00:55:06.490
And the key example I'll give you is, with
India, you know, it's a very proud country

00:55:06.490 --> 00:55:09.910
about its role and sensitive to some of the
climate change topics.

00:55:09.910 --> 00:55:13.970
They wanted to do a project with us through
the Climate Investment Fund, so as to give

00:55:13.970 --> 00:55:16.880
the Climate Investment Fund additional legitimacy.

00:55:16.880 --> 00:55:20.640
So that was -- they wanted to do that support,
because we worked out the governance structure

00:55:20.640 --> 00:55:21.830
and the financing.

00:55:21.830 --> 00:55:26.200
Now this might just seem like it's, you know,
sort of an obscure thing, but you open up

00:55:26.200 --> 00:55:31.200
the newspaper and you've got endless discussions
about the green fund in the U.N., OK?

00:55:31.200 --> 00:55:33.600
So -- and people are still, you know, mapping
stuff on paper.

00:55:33.600 --> 00:55:38.430
Now and then I have to kind of pinch myself
because you got endless discussions about

00:55:38.430 --> 00:55:42.860
the green fund -- we got one up and running
with $50 billion that we'll learn lessons

00:55:42.860 --> 00:55:43.860
from.

00:55:43.860 --> 00:55:48.250
So I use that as an example of saying that
at the end of the day, you still have to make

00:55:48.250 --> 00:55:54.260
stuff happen, and then we as an institution
have to be more rigorous in measuring results,

00:55:54.260 --> 00:55:56.990
in learning where we screw it up and sort
of adapt the process.

00:55:56.990 --> 00:56:02.510
So for those reasons, if -- or post-conflict
states -- you know, we're probably the most

00:56:02.510 --> 00:56:04.410
effective player in Afghanistan today.

00:56:04.410 --> 00:56:08.840
So if you think the bottom billion matters,
if you think that climate change matters,

00:56:08.840 --> 00:56:12.880
if you think that trade matters, if you think
that emerging markets in the international

00:56:12.880 --> 00:56:16.550
system and how they fit in matter, then I
think there's a role for the bank, and for

00:56:16.550 --> 00:56:20.470
me that's a pretty clear exposition of why
you do need it.

00:56:20.470 --> 00:56:24.060
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Do we have any non-European hands
here?

00:56:24.060 --> 00:56:25.060
MR.

00:56:25.060 --> 00:56:26.060
: (Laughs.)

00:56:26.060 --> 00:56:27.060
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Sure.

00:56:27.060 --> 00:56:28.060
(Inaudible.)

00:56:28.060 --> 00:56:29.060
MR.

00:56:29.060 --> 00:56:30.060
: (Laughs.)

00:56:30.060 --> 00:56:31.060
QUESTIONER: (Off mic) -- non-European.

00:56:31.060 --> 00:56:33.460
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Oh, Turkey's -- I'm not sure how
we include Turkey on that one.

00:56:33.460 --> 00:56:35.060
QUESTIONER: This is, I suppose, partly European.

00:56:35.060 --> 00:56:39.200
Elizabeth Sidiropoulos, but from the South
African Institute of International Affairs.

00:56:39.200 --> 00:56:46.730
A question around a recent initiative by -- spearheaded,
seemingly, by India, which apparently will

00:56:46.730 --> 00:56:50.600
be publicly announced at the end of this month,
around the establishment of a BRICS development

00:56:50.600 --> 00:56:51.600
bank.

00:56:51.600 --> 00:56:58.700
I attended a presentation by one of the concept
writers in that regard, and he said, you know,

00:56:58.700 --> 00:57:03.040
could it play a role that the World Bank played
after World War II.

00:57:03.040 --> 00:57:08.610
And is it -- and my question is, I mean, how
would you at the World Bank view such an initiative,

00:57:08.610 --> 00:57:14.140
notwithstanding that I think the concept still
has to be further explored and defined, and

00:57:14.140 --> 00:57:15.250
also its purpose.

00:57:15.250 --> 00:57:19.640
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah, I'm going to be in India
in about two weeks, so I'll have a better

00:57:19.640 --> 00:57:21.590
sense when I talk with people.

00:57:21.590 --> 00:57:24.710
And it -- and your -- the last phrase that
you -- (the clause ?) -- that those are actually

00:57:24.710 --> 00:57:29.030
kind of important, because you had -- you
had a Bank of a (sic) South movement here

00:57:29.030 --> 00:57:33.010
led by Chavez that I didn't fight because
I didn't think it was going to do anything

00:57:33.010 --> 00:57:35.180
and it didn't do anything, you know.

00:57:35.180 --> 00:57:39.970
And -- but also, as part of that, you have
to decide what they resisted.

00:57:39.970 --> 00:57:44.050
In that case, I think it was sometimes openness,
transparency, governance, things I think are

00:57:44.050 --> 00:57:45.060
good things.

00:57:45.060 --> 00:57:52.980
I think in this case what's driving it is
an Indian concern that because of some of

00:57:52.980 --> 00:57:58.140
the borrowing limits the bank has had, that
it's running up against (ceilings ?) about

00:57:58.140 --> 00:58:00.010
sort of investable money.

00:58:00.010 --> 00:58:05.070
I think that's a serious issue, and actually
it's a -- I've had to actually fight my board

00:58:05.070 --> 00:58:09.980
on this because I feel that if the bank takes
itself out of dealing with the Indias, the

00:58:09.980 --> 00:58:14.940
Brazils and the Chinas, as I said, it's bad
for these countries and the international

00:58:14.940 --> 00:58:15.940
system.

00:58:15.940 --> 00:58:17.510
How we engage can be very different.

00:58:17.510 --> 00:58:21.789
So with China, actually a lot of it is more
analytical work, and they use programs to

00:58:21.789 --> 00:58:25.790
-- as pilots that they test.

00:58:25.790 --> 00:58:32.570
India's at a stage where it is -- it's -- it's
-- is concerned for infrastructure development.

00:58:32.570 --> 00:58:37.630
I really don't know for sure how much of it
is capital and how much of it is the enabling

00:58:37.630 --> 00:58:38.630
environment.

00:58:38.630 --> 00:58:40.500
I kind of get mixed messages about that.

00:58:40.500 --> 00:58:45.630
But one of the things, just to give you a
sense, is, I actually pushed for an expansion

00:58:45.630 --> 00:58:50.140
of our borrowing limit for India -- if we
could get the Indians, for example, to invest

00:58:50.140 --> 00:58:53.550
in some of our securities, which would offset
it, so we've actually expanded it another

00:58:53.550 --> 00:58:55.100
4 1/2 billion (dollars) or something.

00:58:55.100 --> 00:58:58.950
I think for infrastructure, it relates to
this public-private partnership model.

00:58:58.950 --> 00:59:03.850
So I think what is often the case when somebody
promotes something, it's important to know

00:59:03.850 --> 00:59:05.510
kind of what is driving it, OK?

00:59:05.510 --> 00:59:10.940
Now, that's separate from the issue of will
this be -- is this positioning or reality.

00:59:10.940 --> 00:59:16.410
So -- because -- you know, it'd be interesting
to decide whether the BRIC countries decide

00:59:16.410 --> 00:59:19.900
to actually fund it, and where, for example,
will they put it, you know?

00:59:19.900 --> 00:59:26.890
So -- but I don't mean to be -- I think it's
a -- it's an idea that you need to understand

00:59:26.890 --> 00:59:30.230
what is driving it and to try to address it.

00:59:30.230 --> 00:59:35.250
And if we as an institution can't address
it effectively, well, then there may be other

00:59:35.250 --> 00:59:36.600
ways that people try to address it.

00:59:36.600 --> 00:59:37.600
So I mean, I'm not a monopolist.

00:59:37.600 --> 00:59:38.600
(Chuckles.)

00:59:38.600 --> 00:59:39.600
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Brenda (sp).

00:59:39.600 --> 00:59:41.510
Brenda (sp) there in the first table.

00:59:41.510 --> 00:59:46.590
QUESTIONER: Your -- is this working?

00:59:46.590 --> 00:59:49.110
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Yeah.

00:59:49.110 --> 00:59:51.620
Go ahead.

00:59:51.620 --> 00:59:53.500
QUESTIONER: Your comment about --

00:59:53.500 --> 00:59:55.190
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Where are you from?

00:59:55.190 --> 00:59:56.550
QUESTIONER: I'm from Turkey.

00:59:56.550 --> 00:59:57.570
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Turkey, great.

00:59:57.570 --> 01:00:01.640
QUESTIONER: Your comment about moving some
manufacturing jobs from China to sub-Saharan

01:00:01.640 --> 01:00:07.400
Africa -- I think that's indicative of a broader
question in that given the demographic expectations,

01:00:07.400 --> 01:00:13.060
probably sub- Saharan Africa and parts of
the Indian subcontinent is where we expect

01:00:13.060 --> 01:00:14.730
the youth bulge.

01:00:14.730 --> 01:00:17.610
And that's probably bringing them into the
global system.

01:00:17.610 --> 01:00:20.950
Global trade and commerce will be a priority.

01:00:20.950 --> 01:00:28.030
But when that happens, if that happens, the
abundance of labor will probably impact intrastate

01:00:28.030 --> 01:00:32.020
income equality across the world, including
the Western world.

01:00:32.020 --> 01:00:34.940
I don't know if that's sustainable politically.

01:00:34.940 --> 01:00:40.570
So I'm wondering if when you look at the broader
picture in a 10, 20-year perspective, are

01:00:40.570 --> 01:00:46.180
we only talking about interstate sort of equality
and convergence and moving people -- sort

01:00:46.180 --> 01:00:52.960
of the average upwards, or do we also care
about intrastate equality as a development

01:00:52.960 --> 01:00:53.990
objective?

01:00:53.990 --> 01:00:59.750
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: I think we definitely have to.

01:00:59.750 --> 01:01:04.190
In our work in emerging-market countries,
in developing countries, we're very much focused

01:01:04.190 --> 01:01:06.000
on that, what we call inclusive growth.

01:01:06.000 --> 01:01:11.490
And in some cases, it starts with having the
fundamentals of nutrition and education.

01:01:11.490 --> 01:01:16.210
And in every country I deal with -- and I'm
going to come to the developed countries as

01:01:16.210 --> 01:01:21.270
well -- there's a huge interest in this connection
between education, skills and workforce.

01:01:21.270 --> 01:01:27.820
And our next World Development Report is actually
on jobs, just as we did one on gender and

01:01:27.820 --> 01:01:29.060
post-conflict.

01:01:29.060 --> 01:01:31.070
And this may seem, well, that's obvious.

01:01:31.070 --> 01:01:37.370
But what we were trying to get at is when
I studied labor economics, now getting to

01:01:37.370 --> 01:01:42.790
be quite distant, it -- the field actually
didn't strike me as too interesting because

01:01:42.790 --> 01:01:47.180
it was either discussions of kind of unions
and economies or kind of the labor market

01:01:47.180 --> 01:01:49.640
was a derivative of your macroeconomic policies.

01:01:49.640 --> 01:01:55.160
This is trying to look at the -- from a different
issue about the social capital that's developed,

01:01:55.160 --> 01:01:59.480
the productivity of individuals, how to sort
of see this as part of your development model.

01:01:59.480 --> 01:02:05.410
And the work isn't done yet, but it's an example
of where I hope we can make an added contribution,

01:02:05.410 --> 01:02:09.280
though the way that you started the question
is -- let me just say that I think is going

01:02:09.280 --> 01:02:15.640
to be a big issue for developed economies,
OK, and their willingness to sustain and participate

01:02:15.640 --> 01:02:20.180
in an open international system and resist
protectionism and populism and so on and so

01:02:20.180 --> 01:02:21.180
forth.

01:02:21.180 --> 01:02:24.020
And I think that's extremely important.

01:02:24.020 --> 01:02:28.880
It happens not to be the remit of the bank
-- (chuckles) -- although one of the things

01:02:28.880 --> 01:02:33.000
I've tried -- you know, and I do this with
the Congress as well as other forums in the

01:02:33.000 --> 01:02:37.600
United States -- to try to say there's actually
some very interesting lessons out there coming

01:02:37.600 --> 01:02:41.730
from emerging markets that you should be -- can
be able to draw from, OK?

01:02:41.730 --> 01:02:43.501
So I mentioned the infrastructure one.

01:02:43.501 --> 01:02:46.430
But in education, you know, it's very interesting.

01:02:46.430 --> 01:02:49.460
You see the debate in the United States about
measurement and performance standards.

01:02:49.460 --> 01:02:52.100
At least in the developing countries I deal
with, it's a no-brainer.

01:02:52.100 --> 01:02:54.510
Of course you've got to measure what the performance
is.

01:02:54.510 --> 01:02:57.030
You know, how else are you going to know what
the results are, you know?

01:02:57.030 --> 01:03:00.570
And -- but here if you test, oh, you might
learn something, you know?

01:03:00.570 --> 01:03:08.520
So I'm hopeful that over time -- you know,
each of these things create a risk and an

01:03:08.520 --> 01:03:13.870
opportunity that you could actually create
a greater flow of information and experience

01:03:13.870 --> 01:03:17.210
that developed countries could manage as well.

01:03:17.210 --> 01:03:22.500
But the core point that I just -- I want to
emphasize is -- was the one sort of inherent

01:03:22.500 --> 01:03:23.690
in this.

01:03:23.690 --> 01:03:27.800
Look, there's problems in the international
system, but by and large if you look at the

01:03:27.800 --> 01:03:32.110
growth rates for sort of the whole world over
the past 50 or 60 years, it hasn't been too

01:03:32.110 --> 01:03:33.510
darn bad, OK?

01:03:33.510 --> 01:03:35.510
And in -- from historical terms.

01:03:35.510 --> 01:03:37.600
But this is not pre-ordained.

01:03:37.600 --> 01:03:39.240
You could lose this, you know?

01:03:39.240 --> 01:03:43.370
And these factors that I mentioned in the
answer to Terry (sp), you know, the emerging

01:03:43.370 --> 01:03:47.670
markets feeling, oh, well, they have to take
on additional responsibilities, but you know,

01:03:47.670 --> 01:03:51.300
what's their say in the system, and have they
really -- you know, they've still got a lot

01:03:51.300 --> 01:03:52.300
of development issues.

01:03:52.300 --> 01:03:56.110
The developed countries, particularly ones
that are aging -- (inaudible) -- sort of their

01:03:56.110 --> 01:03:57.660
sort of role in the system.

01:03:57.660 --> 01:04:00.670
And that really goes back, I guess, to the
question that Terri (sp) and Richard (sp)

01:04:00.670 --> 01:04:01.670
mentioned.

01:04:01.670 --> 01:04:04.370
It's why institutions like the World Bank
-- (chuckles) -- they're important to try

01:04:04.370 --> 01:04:09.740
to interconnect people, and as opposed to
shout at each other, try to come up with cooperative

01:04:09.740 --> 01:04:10.740
solutions.

01:04:10.740 --> 01:04:14.300
But the other piece of it is my difference
with sort of -- it's not enough just to have

01:04:14.300 --> 01:04:18.470
the institution and talk to each other; you
have to try to resolve real problems.

01:04:18.470 --> 01:04:21.350
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I know there's several of you who
still have questions.

01:04:21.350 --> 01:04:22.940
I -- OK, we'll do one last question.

01:04:22.940 --> 01:04:36.090
I want to make sure -- (inaudible) -- from
Japan -- I -- and then we'll do that.

01:04:36.090 --> 01:04:39.060
QUESTIONER: (Through interpreter.)

01:04:39.060 --> 01:04:45.380
I come from Japan.

01:04:45.380 --> 01:04:55.770
I have -- 
just have a couple of questions.

01:04:55.770 --> 01:04:58.760
The World Bank and China are doing 2030.

01:04:58.760 --> 01:05:03.810
And what is the significance of conducting
this kind of a research, right -- investigation

01:05:03.810 --> 01:05:08.420
at the time when China is to change their
guards in Beijing, so to speak?

01:05:08.420 --> 01:05:15.400
And what's your view on the dollar- renminbi
relationship in 2030 if the structural, you

01:05:15.400 --> 01:05:16.970
know, improvement succeeds?

01:05:16.970 --> 01:05:23.280
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, let me focus on the first
question -- (laughter) -- because it's actually

01:05:23.280 --> 01:05:29.110
the more important question, just so people
have a little sense of this.

01:05:29.110 --> 01:05:35.530
The bank has had a very good brand in China,
dating back to Robert McNamara opening relations

01:05:35.530 --> 01:05:38.170
in 1980 with Deng Xiaoping.

01:05:38.170 --> 01:05:45.040
And over the course of some 30 years, there
were points in the idea development process

01:05:45.040 --> 01:05:50.800
in China where the bank seemed to play a catalytic
role.

01:05:50.800 --> 01:05:55.620
There -- I know this from talking -- is more
to the Chinese counterparts.

01:05:55.620 --> 01:06:00.410
In 1985 there was a floating seminar down
the Yangtze River for four or five days that

01:06:00.410 --> 01:06:01.410
led to a report.

01:06:01.410 --> 01:06:04.210
In the late '90s there was something held
in Dalian.

01:06:04.210 --> 01:06:10.610
And the relevance of this was -- a Chinese
official actually came to me before the 30th

01:06:10.610 --> 01:06:11.610
anniversary.

01:06:11.610 --> 01:06:15.450
And he said, Bob, you know, everybody thinks
China loves anniversaries and that we just

01:06:15.450 --> 01:06:19.490
want to celebrate, and he said, that's true,
but when you come for the 30th anniversary,

01:06:19.490 --> 01:06:25.270
he said, what about planting the idea of a
report that would deal with some of the issues

01:06:25.270 --> 01:06:30.520
that are stirring in China, like how to avoid
the middle-income trap, and kind of how do

01:06:30.520 --> 01:06:35.950
we -- we've talked in the next five-year plan
about rebalancing domestic demand, consumer

01:06:35.950 --> 01:06:37.900
-- but how do you really do that?

01:06:37.900 --> 01:06:42.800
And quite intriguingly, he also said -- he
said, as you know -- so you work a lot with

01:06:42.800 --> 01:06:46.790
China; people in Washington think, you know,
people are waking up in Beijing trying to

01:06:46.790 --> 01:06:48.070
take over the world every day.

01:06:48.070 --> 01:06:51.200
And he said, most of the time they're trying
to just, you know, work on the problems in

01:06:51.200 --> 01:06:52.200
Beijing and China.

01:06:52.200 --> 01:06:56.390
So he said, what would be the implications
of this internationally?

01:06:56.390 --> 01:06:59.780
First off -- and then -- so then I went to
China.

01:06:59.780 --> 01:07:01.910
I proposed this study.

01:07:01.910 --> 01:07:09.010
And Li Keqiang, the next -- or it looks like
the next premier, was very enthusiastic about

01:07:09.010 --> 01:07:13.290
it and, quite importantly, asked us to work
with the Development Research Council, which

01:07:13.290 --> 01:07:19.100
is the State Council under -- is the -- is
the research think thank under the State Council.

01:07:19.100 --> 01:07:21.020
We also work with the Ministry of Finance.

01:07:21.020 --> 01:07:27.450
And it was blessed by President Hu and Xi
Jinping, but Li Keqiang is kind of the godfather

01:07:27.450 --> 01:07:28.530
of the process.

01:07:28.530 --> 01:07:31.940
And we've been at it for about 18 months.

01:07:31.940 --> 01:07:36.570
We had a -- in September, we had a draft;
we had various international commentaries

01:07:36.570 --> 01:07:38.540
about, and so on and so forth.

01:07:38.540 --> 01:07:43.900
Now, the first -- from a strategic perspective,
since all of you aspire to strategy, I find

01:07:43.900 --> 01:07:49.390
it very impressive that China after 30 years
of 10 percent growth could say, you know what,

01:07:49.390 --> 01:07:53.300
this may not work in the future; we got to
rethink this model, OK?

01:07:53.300 --> 01:07:55.300
Whether they accomplish it, that's a different
question.

01:07:55.300 --> 01:08:00.170
But again, I would suggest that maybe the
U.S., Europe and Japan without 10 percent

01:08:00.170 --> 01:08:02.320
growth should be thinking a little bit in
these terms.

01:08:02.320 --> 01:08:11.130
The second thing is that what I think is also
going on was that this is to contribute to

01:08:11.130 --> 01:08:14.200
their own internal debate and discussion and
to be catalytic.

01:08:14.200 --> 01:08:17.679
So I asked Justin Lin, our chief economist
from China.

01:08:17.679 --> 01:08:20.759
And I said, Justin, what do you think is going
on here?

01:08:20.759 --> 01:08:23.679
Because you know, Chinese economists could
come up with a lot of this.

01:08:23.679 --> 01:08:27.989
And he said, yes; he said, but as you know,
in any country, people work on their own particular

01:08:27.989 --> 01:08:28.989
piece of the problem.

01:08:28.989 --> 01:08:33.499
This is a catalyst to get people to look at
the economywide pieces.

01:08:33.499 --> 01:08:37.230
And frankly, he said, the bank's got a very
good standing and reputation, and so it will

01:08:37.230 --> 01:08:41.309
be taken very seriously as we come in with
it together.

01:08:41.309 --> 01:08:45.589
Next point is, of course, the devil will be
in the details of implementation.

01:08:45.589 --> 01:08:48.829
But you know, even the summary report is 450
pages long.

01:08:48.829 --> 01:08:51.049
So I wouldn't expect everybody to have read
it.

01:08:51.049 --> 01:08:56.759
But you'll see, if you skim it, there's things
about phasing, transition, (quick wins ?) -- and

01:08:56.759 --> 01:09:01.259
going back to this question a little bit -- how
do you build public participation in it to

01:09:01.259 --> 01:09:04.150
build support along the process?

01:09:04.150 --> 01:09:06.420
So I think it's a very serious exercise.

01:09:06.420 --> 01:09:11.599
Now, the bottom line, my own guess -- but
it's only a guess -- is, is that, you know,

01:09:11.599 --> 01:09:15.880
this will wait until the 18th party congress;
there'll be some settling down after that.

01:09:15.880 --> 01:09:19.920
I don't expect China to follow a "big bang"
approach, like Margaret Thatcher did, because

01:09:19.920 --> 01:09:21.670
that's not the Chinese way.

01:09:21.670 --> 01:09:27.650
But my best guess is you'll see these ideas
pursued in pilots at local level, in some

01:09:27.650 --> 01:09:31.630
sense, to kind of test them as they move forward.

01:09:31.630 --> 01:09:35.850
And again, you know, some people are quick
to judge, oh, well, will China do this or

01:09:35.850 --> 01:09:36.850
this?

01:09:36.850 --> 01:09:40.319
And I just think it's important to also look
at how other countries are doing this.

01:09:40.319 --> 01:09:45.180
And then, again, what I suggested is in my
comments on the international side, there

01:09:45.180 --> 01:09:50.019
are some very interesting opportunities here
for creative minds to kind of develop some

01:09:50.019 --> 01:09:51.019
win-win agenda.

01:09:51.019 --> 01:09:56.599
And the last point is -- you know, what's
quite interesting, the reaction -- and this

01:09:56.599 --> 01:10:00.060
is what I'd hoped for -- we're getting -- one
of my colleagues was in Brazil last week.

01:10:00.060 --> 01:10:05.530
The topic was a very -- the report was a very
big topic because people are facing similar

01:10:05.530 --> 01:10:06.530
problems.

01:10:06.530 --> 01:10:10.750
And this also gives you, going back to where
I started, a little idea that if the bank

01:10:10.750 --> 01:10:14.679
were only working on the 79 poorest countries,
we'd actually miss out on this.

01:10:14.679 --> 01:10:19.650
And this question of rebalancing the international
system, how middle-income countries fit in

01:10:19.650 --> 01:10:23.940
the system, you know, their own growth model
-- those are going to be important questions

01:10:23.940 --> 01:10:24.940
for the world.

01:10:24.940 --> 01:10:27.849
So I'm -- at least so far, I'm very pleased
with the report.

01:10:27.849 --> 01:10:31.460
&gt;&gt; HAASS: We have a tradition here where we
end meetings on time, and today we violated

01:10:31.460 --> 01:10:32.790
that tradition because we went a few minutes
over, but I'm glad we did.

01:10:32.790 --> 01:10:33.790
Bob, thank you for being with us today, and
I think more important, thank you for all

01:10:33.790 --> 01:10:34.790
you've done over the last five years at the
bank.

01:10:34.790 --> 01:10:35.790
It's a -- (inaudible).

01:10:35.790 --> 01:10:36.790
(Applause.)

01:10:36.790 --> 01:10:37.790
RICHARD N. &gt;&gt; HAASS: Well, good afternoon
and welcome to today's lunch program of this

01:10:37.790 --> 01:10:38.790
first ever -- but we hope first of many -- gathering
of the Council of Councils, as we are calling

01:10:38.790 --> 01:10:39.790
it.

01:10:39.790 --> 01:10:40.790
Just a couple of housekeeping remarks for
a second.

01:10:40.790 --> 01:10:41.790
If people would please turn off your cellphones,
that would be a great help -- BlackBerrys

01:10:41.790 --> 01:10:42.790
and all such devices.

01:10:42.790 --> 01:10:43.790
Secondly, this session is on the record.

01:10:43.790 --> 01:10:44.790
Let me just say that.

01:10:44.790 --> 01:10:45.790
Let me -- and we'll repeat later, but just
let people know -- the building we're meeting

01:10:45.790 --> 01:10:46.790
in is only several years old, and it's environmentally,
shall we say, advanced.

01:10:46.790 --> 01:10:47.790
It's a Gold LEED building.

01:10:47.790 --> 01:10:48.790
And what we'd like to do -- if anyone would
be interested, after the last session today

01:10:48.790 --> 01:10:49.790
at 3:45, we are more than happy to give you
a tour of the building, just so you can see

01:10:49.790 --> 01:10:50.790
what sorts of things have been done here,
both environmentally, but also in audio-visual

01:10:50.790 --> 01:10:51.790
type things and all that, which might be (of)
interest to some of you from other organizations.

01:10:51.790 --> 01:10:52.790
As I said, this is the inaugural meeting of
the Council of Councils, which is a network

01:10:52.790 --> 01:10:53.790
of policy institutes from around the world,
and the whole idea is to discuss, identify

01:10:53.790 --> 01:10:54.790
global challenges as well as global opportunities.

01:10:54.790 --> 01:10:55.790
And hopefully out of the discussions will
emerge some ideas, some initiatives that people

01:10:55.790 --> 01:10:56.790
then can take home and introduce either into
their own respective public debates or possibly

01:10:56.790 --> 01:10:57.790
as well into decision-making circles within
their own countries.

01:10:57.790 --> 01:10:58.790
Some 20 countries or so are represented here
today, roughly tracking the G-20, the difference

01:10:58.790 --> 01:10:59.790
being that the agenda here is far more comprehensive.

01:10:59.790 --> 01:11:00.790
The agenda here is not simply economic or
even economic and energy and environmental

01:11:00.790 --> 01:11:01.790
issues, but deals also with the full range
of political challenges -- for example, humanitarian

01:11:01.790 --> 01:11:02.790
intervention, those questions -- as well as
security challenges.

01:11:02.790 --> 01:11:03.790
We just spent an hour and a half discussing
-- we didn't quite solve, but discussing the

01:11:03.790 --> 01:11:04.790
challenge to the nonproliferation regime posed
by -- posed by Iran.

01:11:04.790 --> 01:11:05.790
We also had a general conversation about global
governance, the whole challenge of coming

01:11:05.790 --> 01:11:06.790
up with common rules and norms and, ultimately,
institutions to bolster what Hedley Bull would've

01:11:06.790 --> 01:11:07.790
called the society part of international relations,
as opposed to the anarchical dimension of

01:11:07.790 --> 01:11:08.790
international relations.

01:11:08.790 --> 01:11:09.790
I would like to offer special thanks to the
Robina Foundation, who have made not just

01:11:09.790 --> 01:11:10.790
this possible, but the larger program, the
council's program on international institutions

01:11:10.790 --> 01:11:11.790
and global governance.

01:11:11.790 --> 01:11:12.790
And I'd also like to pay a special welcome
to one of my bosses, to Carla Hills, who is

01:11:12.790 --> 01:11:13.790
the co-chair of the board of directors of
the Council on Foreign Relations and a former

01:11:13.790 --> 01:11:14.790
U.S. trade representative.

01:11:14.790 --> 01:11:15.790
Indeed, she is one of two former U.S. trade
representatives with us today -- actually,

01:11:15.790 --> 01:11:16.790
one of three; we have Charlene, Carla -- Charlene
Barshefsky -- and Bob Zoellick.

01:11:16.790 --> 01:11:17.790
So probably nowhere in the world at this moment
are there more U.S. trade representatives,

01:11:17.790 --> 01:11:18.790
and -- (laughter) -- who knew?

01:11:18.790 --> 01:11:19.790
And so any of you tweeting can tweet that.

01:11:19.790 --> 01:11:20.790
(Laughter.)

01:11:20.790 --> 01:11:21.790
I'm thrilled to welcome Bob Zoellick.

01:11:21.790 --> 01:11:22.790
Bob is the 11th president of the World Bank
Group.

01:11:22.790 --> 01:11:23.790
And he's a longtime personal friend.

01:11:23.790 --> 01:11:24.790
Let me get the conflict of interest out on
the table.

01:11:24.790 --> 01:11:25.790
We've worked together for more years or decades
than either of us probably care to remember.

01:11:25.790 --> 01:11:26.790
And he's also been a longtime supporter and
active member of the council.

01:11:26.790 --> 01:11:27.790
He first spoke here in the early 1990s, roughly
two decades ago, and he served on the council's

01:11:27.790 --> 01:11:28.790
board.

01:11:28.790 --> 01:11:29.790
And our statisticians tell us that he's actually
spoken here more than 20 times.

01:11:29.790 --> 01:11:30.790
Did you know that?

01:11:30.790 --> 01:11:31.790
For no fee -- I should point that out.

01:11:31.790 --> 01:11:32.790
Bob is in the nonprofit business, as are we.

01:11:32.790 --> 01:11:33.790
(Chuckles.)

01:11:33.790 --> 01:11:34.790
I won't spend a lot of time on his bio.

01:11:34.790 --> 01:11:35.790
I already pointed out he was U.S. trade representative.

01:11:35.790 --> 01:11:36.790
He was deputy secretary of state as well as
several other positions in the State Department.

01:11:36.790 --> 01:11:37.790
And Bob played, I thought, an extraordinary
role -- and if I were still teaching at the

01:11:37.790 --> 01:11:38.790
Kennedy School, I would highlight him to show
how in government, you can be entrepreneurial.

01:11:38.790 --> 01:11:39.790
And Bob was truly entrepreneurial after the
fall of the Berlin Wall in stitching together

01:11:39.790 --> 01:11:40.790
what became the German unification inside
of NATO.

01:11:40.790 --> 01:11:41.790
It was policymaking, I thought, at its highest
and finest.

01:11:41.790 --> 01:11:42.790
What we're going to do is have a conversation
for a few minutes about economic issues and

01:11:42.790 --> 01:11:43.790
the Bank and all that, and then we're going
to open it up to you all.

01:11:43.790 --> 01:11:44.790
And I won't go on to -- I won't filibuster,
even though we're in the city of filibusters.

01:11:44.790 --> 01:11:45.790
And when we get around to Q-and-A, we'll hand
out microphones, and we'll just ask you to

01:11:45.790 --> 01:11:46.790
identify yourself before you speak.

01:11:46.790 --> 01:11:47.790
You OK there, sir?

01:11:47.790 --> 01:11:48.790
Again, welcome, Bob.

01:11:48.790 --> 01:11:49.790
A few weeks ago you published a piece in our
magazine, Foreign Affairs, which was titled

01:11:49.790 --> 01:11:50.790
"Why We Still Need the World Bank."

01:11:50.790 --> 01:11:51.790
OK, let's start with that.

01:11:51.790 --> 01:11:52.790
Why do we?

01:11:52.790 --> 01:11:53.790
ROBERT &gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, first, let me thank
all of you for taking time to come to this.

01:11:53.790 --> 01:11:54.790
Richard explained to me a little bit to me
about this concept, and I think it's a great

01:11:54.790 --> 01:11:55.790
one.

01:11:55.790 --> 01:11:56.790
So I know for many of you, it required a little
bit of a journey, but it sounds like a superb

01:11:56.790 --> 01:11:57.790
idea to be able to get the councils together.

01:11:57.790 --> 01:11:58.790
It's like trick questions, because aren't
they supposed to read the article?

01:11:58.790 --> 01:11:59.790
(Laughter.)

01:11:59.790 --> 01:12:00.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Give him -- give him the Cliff Notes.

01:12:00.790 --> 01:12:01.790
(Laughs.)

01:12:01.790 --> 01:12:02.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, let me -- let me take this
approach.

01:12:02.790 --> 01:12:03.790
When I came to the World Bank Group about
a little less than five years ago, I had a

01:12:03.790 --> 01:12:04.790
slightly different perspective than some had
in that some who come purely from the economics

01:12:04.790 --> 01:12:05.790
profession or even development economics -- and
that is I view the World Bank as part of this

01:12:05.790 --> 01:12:06.790
very thin tissue of multilateral institutions
that was created during and after World War

01:12:06.790 --> 01:12:07.790
II to try to deal with the problems that were
seen as -- the political-economic problems

01:12:07.790 --> 01:12:08.790
that were seen as driving the crisis in the
'30s and leading to the world war.

01:12:08.790 --> 01:12:09.790
And this includes the IMF; it included at
that time the GATT that became the WTO for

01:12:09.790 --> 01:12:10.790
trade; it's got the World Bank, other institutions.

01:12:10.790 --> 01:12:11.790
And it's striking if you go back and you look
at the issues that people were concerned about:

01:12:11.790 --> 01:12:12.790
currency in international capital flows, you
know, free trade, reconstruction of broken

01:12:12.790 --> 01:12:13.790
states, development, investment flows.

01:12:13.790 --> 01:12:14.790
Those remain very vital issues, but obviously,
the circumstances changed enormously.

01:12:14.790 --> 01:12:15.790
So I saw part of the challenge for the Bank
and my challenge as a leader of the bank as

01:12:15.790 --> 01:12:16.790
how do you modernize multilateralism; how
do you -- recognizing that nation-states are

01:12:16.790 --> 01:12:17.790
still going to be the dominant player, how
do we take this thin interconnecting tissue,

01:12:17.790 --> 01:12:18.790
if you want to call it, related to global
governance, and make sure that it's updated

01:12:18.790 --> 01:12:19.790
for the current age.

01:12:19.790 --> 01:12:20.790
That gives you a slightly different strategic
perspective.

01:12:20.790 --> 01:12:21.790
So for example, if you look at this solely
from an economics perspective, in 2007 you'd

01:12:21.790 --> 01:12:22.790
say, well, in 1945 you didn't really have
private capital flows, and so -- but now you

01:12:22.790 --> 01:12:23.790
had a whole large flow of private capital
that dominate the official sector, so why

01:12:23.790 --> 01:12:24.790
do you need this anymore.

01:12:24.790 --> 01:12:25.790
OK, well, problem number one is that what
economists thought in 2007 -- (chuckles) -- turned

01:12:25.790 --> 01:12:26.790
out not to be the case.

01:12:26.790 --> 01:12:27.790
In 2008, 2009, we learned economists can be
wrong.

01:12:27.790 --> 01:12:28.790
But even more than that, it missed the fact
that a lot of what the Bank does is not just

01:12:28.790 --> 01:12:29.790
invest money; it's the knowledge and learning
and interconnecting transfer.

01:12:29.790 --> 01:12:30.790
Or another perspective -- this, again, seems
obvious to me, but it may come back as a debating

01:12:30.790 --> 01:12:31.790
item.

01:12:31.790 --> 01:12:32.790
When I came to the Bank, there was a school
of thought that said, really, the middle-income

01:12:32.790 --> 01:12:33.790
countries should just be sort of taken out;
you just focused on the poorest.

01:12:33.790 --> 01:12:34.790
Well, if you believe in this as a multilateral
institution, that would be a strategic mistake

01:12:34.790 --> 01:12:35.790
of huge proportions because actually you want
to get -- first off, there's some huge development

01:12:35.790 --> 01:12:36.790
challenges in the so-called middle-income
countries.

01:12:36.790 --> 01:12:37.790
About, you know, 75 percent of the people
living under $2 a day are in those countries.

01:12:37.790 --> 01:12:38.790
But, in addition, you actually want to get
those countries to be responsible stakeholders

01:12:38.790 --> 01:12:39.790
in the system.

01:12:39.790 --> 01:12:40.790
And so just to give you a very current example,
the Bank's got a lot of attention recently

01:12:40.790 --> 01:12:41.790
because of a report we did called "China 2030,"
which is looking at the structural changes

01:12:41.790 --> 01:12:42.790
of China, that it will need to have a different
growth model.

01:12:42.790 --> 01:12:43.790
And I've found this has got interest in other
middle-income countries; it's got the investment

01:12:43.790 --> 01:12:44.790
community; it's got the interest of Congress.

01:12:44.790 --> 01:12:45.790
You know, if you said, well, we weren't supposed
to deal with the Chinas of the world, then

01:12:45.790 --> 01:12:46.790
you wouldn't be doing reports like that.

01:12:46.790 --> 01:12:47.790
And yet it's fundamental to the rebalancing.'

01:12:47.790 --> 01:12:48.790
So part of what I try to do in the article
is talk about the intellectual and strategic

01:12:48.790 --> 01:12:49.790
position of the Bank, but then I also talk
about some of the challenges of modernizing

01:12:49.790 --> 01:12:50.790
the institution and opening it up, changing
the development model so there's not a monopolist

01:12:50.790 --> 01:12:51.790
of sort of people with Ph.D.s from certain
universities and sort of democratizing the

01:12:51.790 --> 01:12:52.790
development process.

01:12:52.790 --> 01:12:53.790
So that's part of the challenge.

01:12:53.790 --> 01:12:54.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I've noticed from your previous
lives -- and we've talked about it before

01:12:54.790 --> 01:12:55.790
-- you're a great believer in institutions.

01:12:55.790 --> 01:12:56.790
Let me take a second, because it's so close
to this -- what we're talking about here,

01:12:56.790 --> 01:12:57.790
governance.

01:12:57.790 --> 01:12:58.790
What do you see as the real advantage of institutions?

01:12:58.790 --> 01:12:59.790
Why are they so important?

01:12:59.790 --> 01:13:00.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, since you got a pretty
erudite group here, I mean, I could talk about

01:13:00.790 --> 01:13:01.790
Edmund Burke and kind of the view of institutions
that --

01:13:01.790 --> 01:13:02.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: He couldn't come today, but we've
got -- (inaudible) --

01:13:02.790 --> 01:13:03.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: (Chuckles) -- he couldn't come
today, but he'll be here in the evening session

01:13:03.790 --> 01:13:04.790
when you do the Ouija Board.

01:13:04.790 --> 01:13:05.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: (Laughs.)

01:13:05.790 --> 01:13:06.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, I think that the institutions,
whether domestic or international, play critical

01:13:06.790 --> 01:13:07.790
organizing roles, intermediating roles.

01:13:07.790 --> 01:13:08.790
It -- and in an international system, I think
what sometimes people underappreciate is they

01:13:08.790 --> 01:13:09.790
can play a role in identifying issues, sometimes
helping to bring people together in a mutual

01:13:09.790 --> 01:13:10.790
fashion on -- try to address those issues,
sometimes they can bring additional resources

01:13:10.790 --> 01:13:11.790
to bear.

01:13:11.790 --> 01:13:12.790
And so they're the heart of problem-solving.

01:13:12.790 --> 01:13:13.790
If you think about challenges, whether it
be private sector or public sector, it's a

01:13:13.790 --> 01:13:14.790
little hard to do in an atomistic way.

01:13:14.790 --> 01:13:15.790
So institutions become the enabling power;
they become the societal -- the social capital.

01:13:15.790 --> 01:13:16.790
They become the glue.

01:13:16.790 --> 01:13:17.790
But a critical point: Institutions have to
adapt too.

01:13:17.790 --> 01:13:18.790
And I think one of my lessons from the public
sector is that, in the private sector, the

01:13:18.790 --> 01:13:19.790
market demands are such that institutions
are compelled to adapt or die; in the public

01:13:19.790 --> 01:13:20.790
sector, they can drift for a long time.

01:13:20.790 --> 01:13:21.790
And so this is -- this has implications for
how you open up the institution to criticism

01:13:21.790 --> 01:13:22.790
and engagement (and ?) others, but it also
has implications for the nature of how you

01:13:22.790 --> 01:13:23.790
lead the institution because you really -- you
have to keep folk -- there's a tendency in

01:13:23.790 --> 01:13:24.790
public sector institutions where process becomes
all controlling.

01:13:24.790 --> 01:13:25.790
So I have this debate sometimes in other international
forum(s) where people talk about quote, "legitimacy,"

01:13:25.790 --> 01:13:26.790
as -- and they define it simply by the participation.

01:13:26.790 --> 01:13:27.790
My view is legitimacy also has to be driven
by effectiveness.

01:13:27.790 --> 01:13:28.790
My view is legitimacy also has to be driven
by effectiveness.

01:13:28.790 --> 01:13:29.790
The League of Nations had one legitimate model
that turned out not to be very effective,

01:13:29.790 --> 01:13:30.790
OK, so the U.N. overhaul was designed to partly
deal with that.

01:13:30.790 --> 01:13:31.790
But I find when I deal with other international
institutions like some of those in the U.N.

01:13:31.790 --> 01:13:32.790
system, they've drifted a little further away
from the effectiveness model.

01:13:32.790 --> 01:13:33.790
And I think that it's incumbent on people
running international institutions to try

01:13:33.790 --> 01:13:34.790
to sort of get that balance right.

01:13:34.790 --> 01:13:35.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Before you said the World Bank does
a lot more than dispense money.

01:13:35.790 --> 01:13:36.790
It's almost something of a misnomer.

01:13:36.790 --> 01:13:37.790
So if you could rename it, what would you
call it?

01:13:37.790 --> 01:13:38.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: (Chuckles.)

01:13:38.790 --> 01:13:39.790
Well, I don't think the name is so bad.

01:13:39.790 --> 01:13:40.790
I once had a friend who's now deceased who,
when he talked to me about the institution,

01:13:40.790 --> 01:13:41.790
said, look, if you have an institution that
has the two worlds -- words "world" and "bank"

01:13:41.790 --> 01:13:42.790
and you can't do something with it, you know,
you're missing -- (laughter) -- but the reason

01:13:42.790 --> 01:13:43.790
that I highlight the -- why the Bank causes
it a problem is that you'll see when many

01:13:43.790 --> 01:13:44.790
people assess the Bank -- and you see this
when it's -- they'll look at the -- sort of

01:13:44.790 --> 01:13:45.790
the loan flows or -- so you now have people
comparing it with some of the big development

01:13:45.790 --> 01:13:46.790
banks in developing countries or others.

01:13:46.790 --> 01:13:47.790
And my view is is that when the World Bank
is most effective, it combines three things:

01:13:47.790 --> 01:13:48.790
One, we really take knowledge and learning
from all quarters of the world.

01:13:48.790 --> 01:13:49.790
And what's really changed with incredible
rapidity over the past decade is the degree

01:13:49.790 --> 01:13:50.790
to which that is now coming from developing
countries to other developing countries.

01:13:50.790 --> 01:13:51.790
And we try to update it, customize it, share
it with others.

01:13:51.790 --> 01:13:52.790
And then second, you try to have your investments
or projects so that they have a multiplier

01:13:52.790 --> 01:13:53.790
effect.

01:13:53.790 --> 01:13:54.790
So you're looking at building markets or institutions
or capacity.

01:13:54.790 --> 01:13:55.790
And then third, what distinguishes us from
a university or the OECD is that we can be

01:13:55.790 --> 01:13:56.790
innovative with different financial ways.

01:13:56.790 --> 01:13:57.790
And so, again, many people may not be aware
we have a private sector arm, IFC.

01:13:57.790 --> 01:13:58.790
So we make equity investments.

01:13:58.790 --> 01:13:59.790
One of the things we created was an asset
management company to change our financial

01:13:59.790 --> 01:14:00.790
intermediation model so that people will invest
in a fund that we create.

01:14:00.790 --> 01:14:01.790
We invest in private equity funds, about 180
around the world.

01:14:01.790 --> 01:14:02.790
So there's diverse ways of having the financial
innovation, and that's again where sometimes

01:14:02.790 --> 01:14:03.790
I think people, when they think about the
Bank, think about it as traditional, you know,

01:14:03.790 --> 01:14:04.790
1960s major loan program.

01:14:04.790 --> 01:14:05.790
You know, we use rain index futures for countries
because you can see, in certain countries,

01:14:05.790 --> 01:14:06.790
that are (heavy with ?) agriculture that if
the rain is below a certain level, you're

01:14:06.790 --> 01:14:07.790
going to have a huge hit to the crop.

01:14:07.790 --> 01:14:08.790
You can use insurance programs.

01:14:08.790 --> 01:14:09.790
So, part of it is how do you use the tools
of financial innovation for development, but

01:14:09.790 --> 01:14:10.790
always keeping an idea on the sharing of knowledge
and information.

01:14:10.790 --> 01:14:11.790
Let me give you two current examples that
I've been focusing on.

01:14:11.790 --> 01:14:12.790
The safety net programs -- we're in a world
environment where I think we're going to have

01:14:12.790 --> 01:14:13.790
to expect considerable volatilities and uncertainties.

01:14:13.790 --> 01:14:14.790
You'll see some policy efforts to try to control
those.

01:14:14.790 --> 01:14:15.790
I'm not sure how successful those are going
to be, whether it's food or energy or natural

01:14:15.790 --> 01:14:16.790
disasters or others.

01:14:16.790 --> 01:14:17.790
So it makes sense, as a matter of policy,
to try to see if you can have effective, efficient,

01:14:17.790 --> 01:14:18.790
low-cost programs that provide a basic safety
net.

01:14:18.790 --> 01:14:19.790
Well, many of you may know that there's a
program that started in Mexico -- it's got

01:14:19.790 --> 01:14:20.790
a lot of attention in Brazil -- called "Oportunidades";
"Bolsa Familia" in Brazil -- they're called

01:14:20.790 --> 01:14:21.790
conditional cash-transfer programs, and they
give money to the poorest people in communities.

01:14:21.790 --> 01:14:22.790
Varies on the program, normally maybe 15 to
20 percent of the population, on the conditions

01:14:22.790 --> 01:14:23.790
that the families send their children to school,
and they get health checkups.

01:14:23.790 --> 01:14:24.790
So it's probably done more for women's health
than anything in the history of the Mexico.

01:14:24.790 --> 01:14:25.790
The Bank has worked with over 40 countries
to expand this program, OK?

01:14:25.790 --> 01:14:26.790
And they customize it for different circumstances.

01:14:26.790 --> 01:14:27.790
And what's striking is in Mexico and Brazil,
it's done for about a half of 1 percent of

01:14:27.790 --> 01:14:28.790
GDP, OK?

01:14:28.790 --> 01:14:29.790
So if you consider entitlement programs in
the U.S. and Europe, this is a pretty darn

01:14:29.790 --> 01:14:30.790
efficient investment, OK?

01:14:30.790 --> 01:14:31.790
Now, you have other countries that may not
have the capacity to have those set of programs.

01:14:31.790 --> 01:14:32.790
But -- so what have we learned about school
feeding programs, community-based efforts,

01:14:32.790 --> 01:14:33.790
so that you have some basic safety-net protection?

01:14:33.790 --> 01:14:34.790
So that's an area where you take sort of knowledge
and learning and expand it across regions

01:14:34.790 --> 01:14:35.790
but always have to customize.

01:14:35.790 --> 01:14:36.790
And I'll give you the direct relevance: You
look at a lot of the subsidy programs across

01:14:36.790 --> 01:14:37.790
North Africa, and they're a huge waste of
money.

01:14:37.790 --> 01:14:38.790
I mean -- and so you're spending a lot of
money, not getting much for it, not focusing

01:14:38.790 --> 01:14:39.790
on those most in need, not protecting people.

01:14:39.790 --> 01:14:40.790
So one of the things -- I just came from a
function over the weekend, which was talking

01:14:40.790 --> 01:14:41.790
more about U.S. policy.

01:14:41.790 --> 01:14:42.790
What I find somewhat striking in the world
of so-called development is that there's a

01:14:42.790 --> 01:14:43.790
-- much more of a -- what I'll call a relentless
pragmatism about what works, as opposed to

01:14:43.790 --> 01:14:44.790
getting locked into ideologies, which I sometimes
see in the developed world.

01:14:44.790 --> 01:14:45.790
And just to give you one other example that
I think will be very important -- and this

01:14:45.790 --> 01:14:46.790
is true for developed countries as well -- I
can't go to a developing country that isn't

01:14:46.790 --> 01:14:47.790
interested in infrastructure investment, and
there's a certain amount you get from the

01:14:47.790 --> 01:14:48.790
public sector, but obviously, people want
to get public-private partnerships, not only

01:14:48.790 --> 01:14:49.790
for the money, but increasingly, in middle-
income countries, it's because they're run

01:14:49.790 --> 01:14:50.790
more efficiently; they -- the design package
makes more sense.

01:14:50.790 --> 01:14:51.790
So we've created a hub in Singapore that is
trying to sort of take the knowledge of that

01:14:51.790 --> 01:14:52.790
region and others about how to design these
models so we can move beyond a series of one-offs.

01:14:52.790 --> 01:14:53.790
But to give you a sense of the changing Bank,
we're also working with Singapore to create

01:14:53.790 --> 01:14:54.790
a special infrastructure investment fund that
will go along with this process.

01:14:54.790 --> 01:14:55.790
So -- and then to bring it back to, say, the
United States, obviously, a lot of the states

01:14:55.790 --> 01:14:56.790
are having financial problems, but people
are only looking at the liability side of

01:14:56.790 --> 01:14:57.790
the balance sheet; they're not looking at
the assets.

01:14:57.790 --> 01:14:58.790
So Mitch Daniels, the governor of Indiana,
was able to privatize or monetize his toll

01:14:58.790 --> 01:14:59.790
road for about $4 billion.

01:14:59.790 --> 01:15:00.790
It was controversial at the time -- ended
up being a big success.

01:15:00.790 --> 01:15:01.790
The state of Pennsylvania couldn't do it.

01:15:01.790 --> 01:15:02.790
So I point out to people that, you know, in
communist Chongqing, you can monetize a toll

01:15:02.790 --> 01:15:03.790
road, and in capitalist Pennsylvania, you
can't.

01:15:03.790 --> 01:15:04.790
So the -- part of the message of this is -- and
it's relevant, I think, for a group like this

01:15:04.790 --> 01:15:05.790
-- there's -- even 20 years ago, OK, when
Carla was working -- (inaudible) -- USTR,

01:15:05.790 --> 01:15:06.790
Charlene, there was still a common assumption
about a North-South knowledge transfer.

01:15:06.790 --> 01:15:07.790
Now it's increasingly South-South, South-North.

01:15:07.790 --> 01:15:08.790
And that's a good thing.

01:15:08.790 --> 01:15:09.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: That actually segues naturally to
my next question, which is I -- you made the

01:15:09.790 --> 01:15:10.790
shocking pronouncement a few minutes ago that
economists have been known to be wrong.

01:15:10.790 --> 01:15:11.790
(Laughter.)

01:15:11.790 --> 01:15:12.790
And it's good we're all sitting down in this
room.

01:15:12.790 --> 01:15:13.790
Where are we now, then, with this debate about
models?

01:15:13.790 --> 01:15:14.790
I remember when I was a graduate student looking
-- and studying economic development, and

01:15:14.790 --> 01:15:15.790
you used to have the debate between sort of
top-down and bottom-up approaches, market

01:15:15.790 --> 01:15:16.790
approaches versus so-called social overhead
capital and, you know, large state role.

01:15:16.790 --> 01:15:17.790
Where's the debate now intellectually?

01:15:17.790 --> 01:15:18.790
What do -- what -- how has the balance swung,
if you will -- and maybe not -- in this debate

01:15:18.790 --> 01:15:19.790
about markets, private sectors versus public?

01:15:19.790 --> 01:15:20.790
You just talked about public-private a bit.

01:15:20.790 --> 01:15:21.790
Where's the theology or the ideology now?

01:15:21.790 --> 01:15:22.790
What's happening?

01:15:22.790 --> 01:15:23.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, it varies a huge amount.

01:15:23.790 --> 01:15:24.790
And my major point is that I think in the
developing world, the key is that it's not

01:15:24.790 --> 01:15:25.790
theological; it's pragmatic about what works.

01:15:25.790 --> 01:15:26.790
And I guess I'd take the question and tilt
it in one direction that I've been more worried

01:15:26.790 --> 01:15:27.790
about because it may be relevant for the work
of these councils.

01:15:27.790 --> 01:15:28.790
There's a lot of focus in the globe today
about the macroeconomic stabilization, the

01:15:28.790 --> 01:15:29.790
-- you know, basic fiscal and monetary policies.

01:15:29.790 --> 01:15:30.790
One of my concerns is that -- and this is
another way of answering your question about

01:15:30.790 --> 01:15:31.790
debate -- is that there's going to be a need
in all countries to focus on the structural

01:15:31.790 --> 01:15:32.790
change.

01:15:32.790 --> 01:15:33.790
So if you're talking about models, this is
-- really takes you more to the microeconomic.

01:15:33.790 --> 01:15:34.790
So the China 2030 report was about this in
the Chinese context, but it's true in Brazil;

01:15:34.790 --> 01:15:35.790
it's true in India.

01:15:35.790 --> 01:15:36.790
And it's definitely true in the United States
-- (chuckles) -- Japan and in Europe as well.

01:15:36.790 --> 01:15:37.790
And the reason I worry this doesn't quite
get enough attention is if political leaders

01:15:37.790 --> 01:15:38.790
are unable to take this on, what I suspect
we'll see happen over the next year or two

01:15:38.790 --> 01:15:39.790
is that the monetary authorities -- and I'm
not being critical of what they've done so

01:15:39.790 --> 01:15:40.790
far, but the monetary authorities will feel
the pressure to use a tool that really isn't

01:15:40.790 --> 01:15:41.790
designed to fit the problem.

01:15:41.790 --> 01:15:42.790
And you're going to continue to see monetary
policies that are extraordinarily unusual

01:15:42.790 --> 01:15:43.790
by historical standards, and I think they
will plant the seeds of future problems, OK?

01:15:43.790 --> 01:15:44.790
Now, the nature of those problems is it's
always very difficult to predict, but I'll

01:15:44.790 --> 01:15:45.790
just give you one.

01:15:45.790 --> 01:15:46.790
I've spoken to some of the Federal Reserve
banks in the United States.

01:15:46.790 --> 01:15:47.790
I was out at the Kansas City Fed last year.

01:15:47.790 --> 01:15:48.790
And you know, when you go to places like that,
you ask local questions.

01:15:48.790 --> 01:15:49.790
I was asking about farmland prices.

01:15:49.790 --> 01:15:50.790
And farmland prices are really going up, really
at real levels, as I recall, similar to where

01:15:50.790 --> 01:15:51.790
they were at the height that led to the farm
credit system crisis in the early '80s.

01:15:51.790 --> 01:15:52.790
Now, right now they're still covered by cash
flow, but farm prices are relatively high.

01:15:52.790 --> 01:15:53.790
So what I'm -- my point is that I think if
the economies, developed and developing, don't

01:15:53.790 --> 01:15:54.790
focus on the structural issues and the microeconomic
issues that are fundamentals to growth, we

01:15:54.790 --> 01:15:55.790
could see that as we, you know, continue to
go to phases of this recovery, we could be

01:15:55.790 --> 01:15:56.790
planting the seeds for a very serious future
problem.

01:15:56.790 --> 01:15:57.790
And for emerging markets, which are the ones
I'm most concerned about, this will mean you'll

01:15:57.790 --> 01:15:58.790
probably have a rather modest growth environment
with lots of volatility and uncertainty, which

01:15:58.790 --> 01:15:59.790
means go back and build domestic demand, infrastructure
and be uncertain and be prepared for difficult

01:15:59.790 --> 01:16:00.790
moments like I mentioned with safety-net policies.

01:16:00.790 --> 01:16:01.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I'll just ask a couple more questions,
then we'll open it up.

01:16:01.790 --> 01:16:02.790
One of the issues historically has been uncertain
American and -- popular and congressional

01:16:02.790 --> 01:16:03.790
support for international institutions.

01:16:03.790 --> 01:16:04.790
Where does that stand with your institution?

01:16:04.790 --> 01:16:05.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, at least as of the last
year we're a bit of an anomaly, but this is

01:16:05.790 --> 01:16:06.790
-- let me explain the context.

01:16:06.790 --> 01:16:07.790
We achieved the first general capital increase
in the Bank in over 22 years.

01:16:07.790 --> 01:16:08.790
The last one, I was actually working for Secretary
Baker at the Treasury Department.

01:16:08.790 --> 01:16:09.790
So in an environment in which people are watching
their dollars closely, an environment which

01:16:09.790 --> 01:16:10.790
is sort of anti-foreign aid, where there's
populism, we were able to get this through

01:16:10.790 --> 01:16:11.790
a Democratic Senate and a Republican House.

01:16:11.790 --> 01:16:12.790
There's a actually a little bit of a lesson
there.

01:16:12.790 --> 01:16:13.790
(Chuckles.)

01:16:13.790 --> 01:16:14.790
And the lesson is I spend a reasonable amount
of my time working with legislators -- and

01:16:14.790 --> 01:16:15.790
I do this in other countries as well -- but
part of this, in the case of the United States,

01:16:15.790 --> 01:16:16.790
was trying to get an appreciation of the value
of the Bank, having those that might be against

01:16:16.790 --> 01:16:17.790
it to recognize -- at least maybe not attack
it.

01:16:17.790 --> 01:16:18.790
So it's a -- it's an -- I don't want to make
too much of it, but I happen to believe that

01:16:18.790 --> 01:16:19.790
on the key issues that are going to be facing
the United States, things like broad-based

01:16:19.790 --> 01:16:20.790
tax reform and entitlement reform, things
can be done, but you're going to need an executive

01:16:20.790 --> 01:16:21.790
leader, and you're going to have to figure
out how to work the system a little bit better

01:16:21.790 --> 01:16:22.790
than has been done before.

01:16:22.790 --> 01:16:23.790
But that takes us in another direction.

01:16:23.790 --> 01:16:24.790
But I -- but I would -- I guess I would say
you got, you know, Charlene here and Carla

01:16:24.790 --> 01:16:25.790
here too.

01:16:25.790 --> 01:16:26.790
Let's take trade, where we worked together.

01:16:26.790 --> 01:16:27.790
You know, in -- it just -- the reality is
unless an executive pushes this, it's not

01:16:27.790 --> 01:16:28.790
going to happen, you know.

01:16:28.790 --> 01:16:29.790
So don't -- you know, and it was actually
kind of amazing that in the case of the three

01:16:29.790 --> 01:16:30.790
FTAs that got done -- of course, that happened
because Congress decided they wanted three,

01:16:30.790 --> 01:16:31.790
not one, done.

01:16:31.790 --> 01:16:32.790
So that was to Congress' benefit.

01:16:32.790 --> 01:16:33.790
But the reason I say this is -- again, I don't
want to get too U.S.-centric here, but I was

01:16:33.790 --> 01:16:34.790
looking at the fact that, you know, with the
Colombia and Panama FTAs done, the United

01:16:34.790 --> 01:16:35.790
States will have FTAs with about 50 percent
of the GDP in the hemisphere, not including

01:16:35.790 --> 01:16:36.790
the U.S., so not a bad starting point.

01:16:36.790 --> 01:16:37.790
And we had tried the FTAA -- Charlene and
I had tried this, and it ran into difficulties

01:16:37.790 --> 01:16:38.790
with Brazil.

01:16:38.790 --> 01:16:39.790
The dynamic has changed, in part because of
the rise of China.

01:16:39.790 --> 01:16:40.790
So there could be some interesting opportunities
here in the Western Hemisphere, but they're

01:16:40.790 --> 01:16:41.790
not going to come just out of the sky.

01:16:41.790 --> 01:16:42.790
Somebody is going to have to push them.

01:16:42.790 --> 01:16:43.790
Again, that means organizing -- by the way,
this goes back to the Bank story.

01:16:43.790 --> 01:16:44.790
In the case of the Bank, I organized the Chamber
of Commerce; I organized other groups and

01:16:44.790 --> 01:16:45.790
so on and so forth, so I could go to members
and say, here are people that support it.

01:16:45.790 --> 01:16:46.790
And there is -- there is a -- there are -- there
are -- if you think about the U.S. role in

01:16:46.790 --> 01:16:47.790
the world, there's actually a lot of opportunities
out there.

01:16:47.790 --> 01:16:48.790
The United States is still the linchpin on
a lot of this.

01:16:48.790 --> 01:16:49.790
But it -- that doesn't just happen.

01:16:49.790 --> 01:16:50.790
So going back to working with the Congress
on international stuff, you kind of really

01:16:50.790 --> 01:16:51.790
have to -- the -- it's the responsibility
of the executive branch, and then working

01:16:51.790 --> 01:16:52.790
the others levers in the system.

01:16:52.790 --> 01:16:53.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I guess I'll ask you ask one last
question, which is close to our intellectual

01:16:53.790 --> 01:16:54.790
--

01:16:54.790 --> 01:16:55.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Just to -- well, just to give
you -- just in the spirit, because it might

01:16:55.790 --> 01:16:56.790
of interest of other people here: Take this
"China 2030" report we did.

01:16:56.790 --> 01:16:57.790
You take that report apart, and there's a
good discussion to be had about what China

01:16:57.790 --> 01:16:58.790
will do.

01:16:58.790 --> 01:16:59.790
But there are lots of opportunities there
for -- to change what is kind of the zero-sum

01:16:59.790 --> 01:17:00.790
U.S.-China economic agenda into cross-investment
issues, service industry expansion, energy

01:17:00.790 --> 01:17:01.790
sector, IPR and innovation.

01:17:01.790 --> 01:17:02.790
So you could build off what China itself wants
to do in a win-win fashion.

01:17:02.790 --> 01:17:03.790
So does that maybe make a little bit better
sense than continuing to have the same debates

01:17:03.790 --> 01:17:04.790
that people have over the rather narrow issues?

01:17:04.790 --> 01:17:05.790
But the point is -- I mean, I was actually
pleased Chairman Baucus raised this with the

01:17:05.790 --> 01:17:06.790
USTR in a hearing.

01:17:06.790 --> 01:17:07.790
OK.

01:17:07.790 --> 01:17:08.790
Well, it wasn't accidental, because Chairman
Baucus was interested in this, and I've gone

01:17:08.790 --> 01:17:09.790
up and talked to him about it.

01:17:09.790 --> 01:17:10.790
But to be honest, it should kind of come from
the other side too.

01:17:10.790 --> 01:17:11.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Duly noted.

01:17:11.790 --> 01:17:12.790
One thing that's come up here in our talks
this morning is the relationship between efforts

01:17:12.790 --> 01:17:13.790
at the regional and global level.

01:17:13.790 --> 01:17:14.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah.

01:17:14.790 --> 01:17:15.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Could you just say something about
-- and you got all these regional development

01:17:15.790 --> 01:17:16.790
banks.

01:17:16.790 --> 01:17:17.790
What's the relationship?

01:17:17.790 --> 01:17:18.790
And is there a conscious division of labor?

01:17:18.790 --> 01:17:19.790
How does that work?

01:17:19.790 --> 01:17:20.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah.

01:17:20.790 --> 01:17:21.790
Well, you know, my -- this happened in trade
as well.

01:17:21.790 --> 01:17:22.790
I believe multilateralism includes regionalism,
and I think, you know, there's a lot that

01:17:22.790 --> 01:17:23.790
we've learned about while economics are a
component of it, but whether it's immigration,

01:17:23.790 --> 01:17:24.790
whether it's security, whether it's narcotics
-- I mean, the whole history of the European

01:17:24.790 --> 01:17:25.790
Union -- and so people who decry regionalism,
I think, are not recognizing sort of realities.

01:17:25.790 --> 01:17:26.790
And so -- and moreover, the ability to interconnect
these regions in an open regionalism, as was

01:17:26.790 --> 01:17:27.790
discussed in the Asia-Pacific.

01:17:27.790 --> 01:17:28.790
So one of the things -- just a small insight
-- at the bank when I came, which we really

01:17:28.790 --> 01:17:29.790
really weren't doing that much of, is that
-- (I see Yusuf in the audience ?) -- we started

01:17:29.790 --> 01:17:30.790
to work much more with regional institutions
at institutions, ASEAN as an institution,

01:17:30.790 --> 01:17:31.790
in Africa not only the African Union but sort
of regional institutions.

01:17:31.790 --> 01:17:32.790
And so part of our service is to try to understand
how to help their capacity.

01:17:32.790 --> 01:17:33.790
And if you take development issues in sub-Saharan
Africa, for example, they're going to be critical

01:17:33.790 --> 01:17:34.790
to sort of build larger markets and how do
you have the infrastructure and how do you

01:17:34.790 --> 01:17:35.790
reduce the cost.

01:17:35.790 --> 01:17:36.790
Now, in the case of regional development banks,
one aspect of that was that I tried to organize

01:17:36.790 --> 01:17:37.790
in a more coherent fashion our cooperation
with the regional development banks.

01:17:37.790 --> 01:17:38.790
In some cases, they may take the lead; in
some cases, we take the lead; we work over

01:17:38.790 --> 01:17:39.790
that.

01:17:39.790 --> 01:17:40.790
The one part, though, where I think regionalism
could go to far is, remember, I talked about

01:17:40.790 --> 01:17:41.790
a knowledge -- a global knowledge transfer.

01:17:41.790 --> 01:17:42.790
One of the -- where the Bank is at its best
is to -- being able to transfer knowledge

01:17:42.790 --> 01:17:43.790
across regions.

01:17:43.790 --> 01:17:44.790
If you're a regional bank, you don't really
have that facility.

01:17:44.790 --> 01:17:45.790
But they undoubtedly will have capacities
that the World Bank doesn't have.

01:17:45.790 --> 01:17:46.790
So I tend to, in structural terms, look at
the world more as a Venn diagram with intersecting

01:17:46.790 --> 01:17:47.790
circles.

01:17:47.790 --> 01:17:48.790
And so I -- it doesn't -- in a sense, the
-- some people describe multilateralism in

01:17:48.790 --> 01:17:49.790
a(n) old- fashioned -- what I'll call mid-20th-century
hierarchical model.

01:17:49.790 --> 01:17:50.790
I think that's not going to work; it's a mistake.

01:17:50.790 --> 01:17:51.790
And I think it doesn't recognize the diversity
of the international system.

01:17:51.790 --> 01:17:52.790
And I'll just -- I'll give one other little
insight on this that some people here might

01:17:52.790 --> 01:17:53.790
be interested in.

01:17:53.790 --> 01:17:54.790
You know, climate change is an issue that
I first started to work on, actually, when

01:17:54.790 --> 01:17:55.790
I helped get the Rio agreement through in
Bush 41's administration.

01:17:55.790 --> 01:17:56.790
And the -- what happened in -- with the UNFCCC
process is there's kind of -- talking about

01:17:56.790 --> 01:17:57.790
international governance; I think that was
one of your topics -- there is this view,

01:17:57.790 --> 01:17:58.790
well, we must get all 195 countries to agree
to everything big enough for, you know, climate

01:17:58.790 --> 01:17:59.790
change (for the world ?) all at once.

01:17:59.790 --> 01:18:00.790
Well, good freaking luck.

01:18:00.790 --> 01:18:01.790
You know, and so to be honest, you know, I've
been part of more agreements and -- bilateral,

01:18:01.790 --> 01:18:02.790
multilateral than probably most people, and
I -- so I can know what they take to get done.

01:18:02.790 --> 01:18:03.790
But I'll give you a very practical aspect
to this.

01:18:03.790 --> 01:18:04.790
I went down to see President Calderon in Mexico
about six or nine months before the Cancun

01:18:04.790 --> 01:18:05.790
meeting, and I outlined this "weltanschauung."

01:18:05.790 --> 01:18:06.790
And then I said, you know, what I would do?

01:18:06.790 --> 01:18:07.790
I said, here's individual papers on building
blocks: energy efficiency, technology, avoided

01:18:07.790 --> 01:18:08.790
deforestation, less plant soil carbon.

01:18:08.790 --> 01:18:09.790
And if you actually go back and look at the
real agreement, it was that countries were

01:18:09.790 --> 01:18:10.790
supposed to build national action plans.

01:18:10.790 --> 01:18:11.790
I said, so what if you have 150 countries
agree on combined national action plans?

01:18:11.790 --> 01:18:12.790
So you don't have 190, but you got 150.

01:18:12.790 --> 01:18:13.790
And that's exactly the model that they followed,
and I -- that's the reason they were successful.

01:18:13.790 --> 01:18:14.790
And ironically, the more that you don't allow
one country to hold it up, the more you're

01:18:14.790 --> 01:18:15.790
actually going to have a chance of getting
it done.

01:18:15.790 --> 01:18:16.790
And the key on this was then I went to see
him the week of the final meeting, and I said,

01:18:16.790 --> 01:18:17.790
Mr. President, I said, you know, you probably
think that consensus means all countries.

01:18:17.790 --> 01:18:18.790
(Laughs.)

01:18:18.790 --> 01:18:19.790
I said, go talk to your lawyers in the Foreign
Ministry because there's varying views of

01:18:19.790 --> 01:18:20.790
consensus.

01:18:20.790 --> 01:18:21.790
And I said, you might find one country, like
Bolivia, that decides it wants to hold you

01:18:21.790 --> 01:18:22.790
up after you've got everything else all lined
up for whatever reason they have.

01:18:22.790 --> 01:18:23.790
And I said, so, you know, be prepared to think
of a way you can describe consensus without

01:18:23.790 --> 01:18:24.790
a country, and it was one of these forecaster
-- (inaudible).

01:18:24.790 --> 01:18:25.790
It was Bolivia that tried to hold them up.

01:18:25.790 --> 01:18:26.790
So the -- I emphasize these because, as you
-- as Richard and I both know, there are views

01:18:26.790 --> 01:18:27.790
in America that say -- that kind of says,
oh, well, you can't make any of these multilateral

01:18:27.790 --> 01:18:28.790
systems work, and they're sort of imperfect.

01:18:28.790 --> 01:18:29.790
I think that's a terrible mistake.

01:18:29.790 --> 01:18:30.790
But, on the other hand, I constantly find
myself having to sort of issue -- kind of

01:18:30.790 --> 01:18:31.790
argue a pragmatism or realism against those
who argue, you know, a rather utopian view.

01:18:31.790 --> 01:18:32.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: All or nothing, you may end up with
nothing.

01:18:32.790 --> 01:18:33.790
Why don't we open it up and, again, just wait
for a microphone, and just introduce yourself,

01:18:33.790 --> 01:18:34.790
your name and your institution, and toss up
a difficult question for Bob.

01:18:34.790 --> 01:18:35.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Or an easy one.

01:18:35.790 --> 01:18:36.790
(Laughter.)

01:18:36.790 --> 01:18:37.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Or an easy one, if you insist.

01:18:37.790 --> 01:18:38.790
Sure.

01:18:38.790 --> 01:18:39.790
Let's start here.

01:18:39.790 --> 01:18:40.790
QUESTIONER: Thank you.

01:18:40.790 --> 01:18:41.790
My name is Kuseni (Dlamini ?) from the South
African Institute of International Affairs.

01:18:41.790 --> 01:18:42.790
My question has to do with the World Bank
and its leadership.

01:18:42.790 --> 01:18:43.790
There've been some debates around the process
that's used to choose the head of the Bank.

01:18:43.790 --> 01:18:44.790
I'll be interested to hear your views, Bob,
as you're about to leave this very important

01:18:44.790 --> 01:18:45.790
institution and having done such a great job.

01:18:45.790 --> 01:18:46.790
Do you think we should move away from the
established practice of having America decide

01:18:46.790 --> 01:18:47.790
and having Europe decide on the IMF and allow
the emerging powers from emerging markets

01:18:47.790 --> 01:18:48.790
to also contest for the position?

01:18:48.790 --> 01:18:49.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, the process has gotten
better in the sense that our board -- our

01:18:49.790 --> 01:18:50.790
board meets and decides that there are certain
characteristics that they're looking for and

01:18:50.790 --> 01:18:51.790
whatever nominations will go forward.

01:18:51.790 --> 01:18:52.790
People will have to present their case and
so on and so forth.

01:18:52.790 --> 01:18:53.790
But the United States has 15 percent of the
votes and Europe has a larger amount, so that's

01:18:53.790 --> 01:18:54.790
the kabal that could guide it in the past.

01:18:54.790 --> 01:18:55.790
Let me start this level.

01:18:55.790 --> 01:18:56.790
I think it is actually critical for the institutions
to broaden their engagement at multiple levels

01:18:56.790 --> 01:18:57.790
with emerging markets, but it's -- and a lot
of this focused on voting shares, OK?

01:18:57.790 --> 01:18:58.790
And we made adjustments in voting shares.

01:18:58.790 --> 01:18:59.790
I honestly think sometimes that becomes an
overstated argument.

01:18:59.790 --> 01:19:00.790
So let me start with this.

01:19:00.790 --> 01:19:01.790
One thing is, we added a chair for sub-Saharan
Africa on our board, another chair, which

01:19:01.790 --> 01:19:02.790
actually South Africa and Nigeria share.

01:19:02.790 --> 01:19:03.790
And because we don't really take votes, that
was probably more important than kind of a

01:19:03.790 --> 01:19:04.790
voting adjustment.

01:19:04.790 --> 01:19:05.790
Second is how you run the institution.

01:19:05.790 --> 01:19:06.790
So yeah, you can actually see some of the
commentary about the Bank now is that I was

01:19:06.790 --> 01:19:07.790
-- been very much driven by a client-driven
approach, what are the clients interested

01:19:07.790 --> 01:19:08.790
in, and then share the knowledge, so on and
so forth.

01:19:08.790 --> 01:19:09.790
The -- interestingly, some people who actually
would come more from what I'll call the left-of-center

01:19:09.790 --> 01:19:10.790
side have what I'll call the monopolist view
that -- you know, that you should do this,

01:19:10.790 --> 01:19:11.790
this and this.

01:19:11.790 --> 01:19:12.790
That's actually, I think, what the Bank needs
to get away from.

01:19:12.790 --> 01:19:13.790
So -- and then another aspect is the staffing.

01:19:13.790 --> 01:19:14.790
And as you probably know, I brought -- about
half the officers are now women and in much

01:19:14.790 --> 01:19:15.790
larger numbers from the emerging markets.

01:19:15.790 --> 01:19:16.790
Then it just leads to the question on the
U.S.

01:19:16.790 --> 01:19:17.790
And I guess I'll pose -- for the presidency.

01:19:17.790 --> 01:19:18.790
I'll just pose this part.

01:19:18.790 --> 01:19:19.790
Some people pose this question to me in an
interesting way.

01:19:19.790 --> 01:19:20.790
They said, is it time for the U.S., you know,
not to be president of the Bank anymore, OK?

01:19:20.790 --> 01:19:21.790
And that's an interesting perspective because
it would suggest, in a merit-based system,

01:19:21.790 --> 01:19:22.790
that an American cannot run the Bank, OK?

01:19:22.790 --> 01:19:23.790
And here's where I guess I'll -- going to
twist this in a little bit different way.

01:19:23.790 --> 01:19:24.790
As someone who's worked a lot with multilateral
institutions and tried to get the United States

01:19:24.790 --> 01:19:25.790
to work more effectively in that system, I
think it's very important that the United

01:19:25.790 --> 01:19:26.790
States have the responsibility of running
some.

01:19:26.790 --> 01:19:27.790
And I'm not going to say which ones, but I
will say this: There's never been an American

01:19:27.790 --> 01:19:28.790
who's headed the WTO; there's never been an
American who's a U.N. secretary-general; there's

01:19:28.790 --> 01:19:29.790
never going to be an American who is going
to be head of the regional development banks.

01:19:29.790 --> 01:19:30.790
So you know, there's -- never had an American
who's headed the IMF.

01:19:30.790 --> 01:19:31.790
So if you're going to change the whole thing,
all I'm saying is somewhere in that system,

01:19:31.790 --> 01:19:32.790
I think it's good for the United States to
bear the responsibility of the multilateral

01:19:32.790 --> 01:19:33.790
system because, going back a little bit to
what Richard said, and some of my colleagues

01:19:33.790 --> 01:19:34.790
in the room here, I've found that my being
in that role actually compels people to engage

01:19:34.790 --> 01:19:35.790
with these institutions more than they might
otherwise.

01:19:35.790 --> 01:19:36.790
So I won't necessarily say which one and others,
but be careful, if you push the U.S. out of

01:19:36.790 --> 01:19:37.790
this system, what the net effect will be.

01:19:37.790 --> 01:19:38.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: (Off mic.)

01:19:38.790 --> 01:19:39.790
QUESTIONER: Thank you.

01:19:39.790 --> 01:19:40.790
Gilad Sher (ph), from the Institute for National
Security Studies in Israel, and excuse me

01:19:40.790 --> 01:19:41.790
for being a bit region- oriented.

01:19:41.790 --> 01:19:42.790
MR.

01:19:42.790 --> 01:19:43.790
: (Laughs.)

01:19:43.790 --> 01:19:44.790
QUESTIONER: In the absence of the --

01:19:44.790 --> 01:19:45.790
MR.

01:19:45.790 --> 01:19:46.790
: What's the World Bank going to do about
the Iranian nuclear program?

01:19:46.790 --> 01:19:47.790
(Laughter.)

01:19:47.790 --> 01:19:48.790
QUESTIONER: No --

01:19:48.790 --> 01:19:49.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: I was just at the Herzliya Conference.

01:19:49.790 --> 01:19:50.790
(Laughter.)

01:19:50.790 --> 01:19:51.790
I know the topic du jour.

01:19:51.790 --> 01:19:52.790
QUESTIONER: In the absence of any substantive
negotiation process between Israel and the

01:19:52.790 --> 01:19:53.790
Palestinians, what role do you see for the
World Bank in the Israel-Palestinian arena

01:19:53.790 --> 01:19:54.790
in particular and in the changing Middle East
in general?

01:19:54.790 --> 01:19:55.790
Thank you.

01:19:55.790 --> 01:19:56.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah.

01:19:56.790 --> 01:19:57.790
Very important one.

01:19:57.790 --> 01:19:58.790
Well, I've had the good fortune that Prime
Minister Fayyad in the Palestinian Authority

01:19:58.790 --> 01:19:59.790
is somebody I've got the highest respect for,
and I think he's got actually a very sound

01:19:59.790 --> 01:20:00.790
economic and political model, which is he's
trying to build the state from the bottom

01:20:00.790 --> 01:20:01.790
up.

01:20:01.790 --> 01:20:02.790
And because the Palestinian Authority is not
a member of the World Bank, I actually -- to

01:20:02.790 --> 01:20:03.790
support (by ?) them, we have to make a special
allocation out of our income, which we do.

01:20:03.790 --> 01:20:04.790
But we also, as we do in other areas, run
sort of trust funds.

01:20:04.790 --> 01:20:05.790
So one of the problems in the developing world
is people may have good intentions, but if

01:20:05.790 --> 01:20:06.790
they have 50 or a hundred different programs
for one small authority, you overwhelm it.

01:20:06.790 --> 01:20:07.790
So we will coordinate with that authority.

01:20:07.790 --> 01:20:08.790
So I think Fayyad has done a very important
job of building the institutions of governments,

01:20:08.790 --> 01:20:09.790
more transparency, fighting corruption, building
capabilities that, at least from my conversations

01:20:09.790 --> 01:20:10.790
with Israelis, are probably important for
Israeli security as well as economic development.

01:20:10.790 --> 01:20:11.790
So I certainly think it's short-sighted -- (chuckles)
-- when people do things like cut off the

01:20:11.790 --> 01:20:12.790
money flow of their own tax revenues for those
institutions, and I've said that to some of

01:20:12.790 --> 01:20:13.790
the Israeli authorities.

01:20:13.790 --> 01:20:14.790
And I think the Israelis, at least the vast
majority, recognize it too.

01:20:14.790 --> 01:20:15.790
And I think the bigger issue there also is
one of contesting in an open fashion kind

01:20:15.790 --> 01:20:16.790
of a modernization agenda.

01:20:16.790 --> 01:20:17.790
So I -- when I went to the Herzliya Conference,
I also went to see Fayyad in Ramallah; I thought

01:20:17.790 --> 01:20:18.790
it was a good thing to do.

01:20:18.790 --> 01:20:19.790
I have no idea what will happen, but he's
using modern communication -- Facebook, social

01:20:19.790 --> 01:20:20.790
media and other things, things his kids have
taught him -- to engage Palestinians -- public

01:20:20.790 --> 01:20:21.790
-- on these issues, to take a sense of responsibility.

01:20:21.790 --> 01:20:22.790
And he believes, actually, that in an electoral
sense, that what he stands for could do better

01:20:22.790 --> 01:20:23.790
than some people think, because he's been
actually trying to build the base up.

01:20:23.790 --> 01:20:24.790
Now, the real problem there -- and this is
one where I can observe; I don't -- I can't

01:20:24.790 --> 01:20:25.790
say with great specificity -- much of the
growth in the Palestinian Authority has been

01:20:25.790 --> 01:20:26.790
developed through public sector funding.

01:20:26.790 --> 01:20:27.790
He's trying to wean it off public sector funding.

01:20:27.790 --> 01:20:28.790
You need more of a private sector development.

01:20:28.790 --> 01:20:29.790
And it's going to be very hard to get the
private sector development with some of the

01:20:29.790 --> 01:20:30.790
movement restrictions.

01:20:30.790 --> 01:20:31.790
On the other hand, I understand the security
logic for some of those movement restrictions.

01:20:31.790 --> 01:20:32.790
But I think if it were up to me, I'd be testing
the border of what more I could do on movement

01:20:32.790 --> 01:20:33.790
to allow a greater sense of private sector
development and, frankly, personal dignity

01:20:33.790 --> 01:20:34.790
that will be the important part of building
that state and capacity.

01:20:34.790 --> 01:20:35.790
And I guess the -- what this would also suggest,
as I saw -- Dennis Ross, a former colleague,

01:20:35.790 --> 01:20:36.790
wrote an op-ed not too long ago about whether
one could open up other sections of the West

01:20:36.790 --> 01:20:37.790
Bank to be able to encourage this sort of
development.

01:20:37.790 --> 01:20:38.790
And the logic would lead me into that direction.

01:20:38.790 --> 01:20:39.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Do you want to say something about
the broader Middle East that would say that

01:20:39.790 --> 01:20:40.790
where you had regime changes -- places like
Egypt, Tunisia, Libya -- what sort of a role

01:20:40.790 --> 01:20:41.790
the bank is playing?

01:20:41.790 --> 01:20:42.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, you know, one of the other
things -- this gives you a little sense of

01:20:42.790 --> 01:20:43.790
how I try to guide the bank -- we need to
learn too, OK.

01:20:43.790 --> 01:20:44.790
So we've got a lot of smart people, but the
world changes and so on.

01:20:44.790 --> 01:20:45.790
We're trying to learn our own lessons from
this, I mean, not the least of which was,

01:20:45.790 --> 01:20:46.790
you know, an Egypt that was growing well and
frankly had a pretty good economic team, but

01:20:46.790 --> 01:20:47.790
nevertheless that wasn't sufficient -- and
this goes to some of these issues of dignity

01:20:47.790 --> 01:20:48.790
and also kind of, I think, a system whereby
you need to combine economic growth with a

01:20:48.790 --> 01:20:49.790
sense that people (feel ?), well, it's not
crony- and favoritism and others.

01:20:49.790 --> 01:20:50.790
What this has led us to is the idea of trying
to work with governments to build on their

01:20:50.790 --> 01:20:51.790
own movements to have greater openness, transparency,
social accountability, and this again connects

01:20:51.790 --> 01:20:52.790
from other regions.

01:20:52.790 --> 01:20:53.790
So what you now have with modern telecommunications
is the ability to have, you know, people in

01:20:53.790 --> 01:20:54.790
villages with cellphones be able to comment
and guide about the delivery of services.

01:20:54.790 --> 01:20:55.790
(Right ?). And this is -- this opens up not
only anti-corruption things, but it opens

01:20:55.790 --> 01:20:56.790
up a challenge for sort of bureaucratic performance.

01:20:56.790 --> 01:20:57.790
So with countries such as Tunisia and Morocco,
we've been trying to take those steps and

01:20:57.790 --> 01:20:58.790
encourage them.

01:20:58.790 --> 01:20:59.790
And that will be a big issue for us with Egypt,
and -- because we have certain programs that

01:20:59.790 --> 01:21:00.790
are of investment projects in agriculture
or energy or others, but the bank sometimes

01:21:00.790 --> 01:21:01.790
gives what's called development policy loans.

01:21:01.790 --> 01:21:02.790
This is money that goes to the budgets, but
it's based on various policy changes.

01:21:02.790 --> 01:21:03.790
And it's my own view that those need to include
these types of social accountability and openness

01:21:03.790 --> 01:21:04.790
and transparency measures.

01:21:04.790 --> 01:21:05.790
And it's things that we're applying to the
bank as well.

01:21:05.790 --> 01:21:06.790
So -- and just to give you one other connection,
because -- something probably many people

01:21:06.790 --> 01:21:07.790
may not be aware of, but with your research
institutes, I really hope you are -- one of

01:21:07.790 --> 01:21:08.790
the -- one of the most lasting things that
we've done at the bank during my time is we

01:21:08.790 --> 01:21:09.790
created an Open Data initiative.

01:21:09.790 --> 01:21:10.790
So we've taken all our data sets going back
decades and made them for -- available for

01:21:10.790 --> 01:21:11.790
free on the Web.

01:21:11.790 --> 01:21:12.790
And there's this interesting little bureaucratic
story behind this.

01:21:12.790 --> 01:21:13.790
(Chuckles.)

01:21:13.790 --> 01:21:14.790
I used to go to meetings, and I'd have, you
know, some slightly wizened professor come

01:21:14.790 --> 01:21:15.790
up to me and say, oh, you've got some great
data sets, but you still charge for them.

01:21:15.790 --> 01:21:16.790
And I'd go back to my economic staff, just
as you would, and say, why are we charging

01:21:16.790 --> 01:21:17.790
for them?

01:21:17.790 --> 01:21:18.790
And they'd say, well, we add value, and we
need to get compensated.

01:21:18.790 --> 01:21:19.790
You know, it took me about three rounds to
realize it was, like, a $3 million offset

01:21:19.790 --> 01:21:20.790
to their budget, right, or -- so -- and so
finally, we said, OK, we're going to wipe

01:21:20.790 --> 01:21:21.790
this out, and we're going to open all this
up.

01:21:21.790 --> 01:21:22.790
And then we started to get our economic staff
to work on different applications to make

01:21:22.790 --> 01:21:23.790
it easier to access.

01:21:23.790 --> 01:21:24.790
And we created an Apps for Development competition.

01:21:24.790 --> 01:21:25.790
So we opened up to the software community
and said, use our data, and the only requirement

01:21:25.790 --> 01:21:26.790
is relate it to the Millennium Development
Goals and use our data.

01:21:26.790 --> 01:21:27.790
And so we came up with mapping, games, other
things that you could never, you know, even

01:21:27.790 --> 01:21:28.790
with a very brilliant staff, have been able
to produce.

01:21:28.790 --> 01:21:29.790
What I'm finding is, as is often with an idea
like this, it starts to drive a whole series

01:21:29.790 --> 01:21:30.790
of other possibilities.

01:21:30.790 --> 01:21:31.790
So for example, when we're working with countries
on gender, another great possibility for growth,

01:21:31.790 --> 01:21:32.790
it's a little hard to know the effect of the
policies if people don't keep gender statistics,

01:21:32.790 --> 01:21:33.790
or beyond-border trade barriers -- getting
beyond the quotas and the tariffs to some

01:21:33.790 --> 01:21:34.790
of the logistics and the customs systems -- unless
you have the information, you can't do it.

01:21:34.790 --> 01:21:35.790
So this notion of -- that we've talked about
of democratizing development -- so it's the

01:21:35.790 --> 01:21:36.790
exact opposite of the monopolist model, but
openness, transparency -- and the relevance

01:21:36.790 --> 01:21:37.790
to the broader Middle East is that I think
this is going to be -- this is going to take

01:21:37.790 --> 01:21:38.790
awhile -- (chuckles) -- but I think building
in this concept of openness and engagement

01:21:38.790 --> 01:21:39.790
for the society and the government is important,
as well was for the Bank.

01:21:39.790 --> 01:21:40.790
QUESTIONER: Michael Emerson, Centre for European
Policy Studies in Brussels.

01:21:40.790 --> 01:21:41.790
Palestinian question.

01:21:41.790 --> 01:21:42.790
You mentioned Morocco.

01:21:42.790 --> 01:21:43.790
I wonder whether you could broaden this out
to talk a bit about the response to the Arab

01:21:43.790 --> 01:21:44.790
Spring, or let us call it the Arab political
earthquake, that happened last year.

01:21:44.790 --> 01:21:45.790
I'm sure that you've been faced with the task
of defining the World Bank's strategic response

01:21:45.790 --> 01:21:46.790
to this huge strategic event, earthquake,
and I'm wondering what conclusions you -- you

01:21:46.790 --> 01:21:47.790
came to.

01:21:47.790 --> 01:21:48.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, just to build on a couple
of the points I said: Number one, development

01:21:48.790 --> 01:21:49.790
has to be owned by the local people.

01:21:49.790 --> 01:21:50.790
So -- and this is a lesson from everywhere.

01:21:50.790 --> 01:21:51.790
You could have great money, ideas, so on and
so forth, but so this goes to the governance

01:21:51.790 --> 01:21:52.790
issue.

01:21:52.790 --> 01:21:53.790
So you have to build your development strategies
with the evolution of the governance topic.

01:21:53.790 --> 01:21:54.790
And obviously, you have countries at different
states.

01:21:54.790 --> 01:21:55.790
So some have had a revolution and are on the
way of developing a political system.

01:21:55.790 --> 01:21:56.790
Tunisia, Morocco and Jordan have sort of a
fast-paced evolution, OK?

01:21:56.790 --> 01:21:57.790
Egypt is -- I mean, I don't know, coup, partial
revolution.

01:21:57.790 --> 01:21:58.790
I mean, I can't tell yet, right?

01:21:58.790 --> 01:21:59.790
And so the key point is, we have to recognize
the local ownership but also that it may be

01:21:59.790 --> 01:22:00.790
going through phases of development, and then
what can we share -- and this is the important

01:22:00.790 --> 01:22:01.790
part about the south- south.

01:22:01.790 --> 01:22:02.790
So it's not just U.S. or the European, but
some of the ideas of what's worked with other

01:22:02.790 --> 01:22:03.790
developing countries.

01:22:03.790 --> 01:22:04.790
So if you work in this area, I'm sure you've
encountered there's a huge, huge interest

01:22:04.790 --> 01:22:05.790
in Turkish economic development, but also
there's an interest in some of the other models

01:22:05.790 --> 01:22:06.790
from East Asia as we go forward.

01:22:06.790 --> 01:22:07.790
And so what can we share about safety net
systems, governance systems, transparency

01:22:07.790 --> 01:22:08.790
systems?

01:22:08.790 --> 01:22:09.790
Then, as you get to other countries, in the
Gulf, for example, that don't necessarily

01:22:09.790 --> 01:22:10.790
need our money, or let's take Libya as a -- we
had the Libyan prime minister here.

01:22:10.790 --> 01:22:11.790
They don't need our money, but what they will
need is the ability to frankly develop public

01:22:11.790 --> 01:22:12.790
financial management systems so that the money
is effectively used, so it's not stolen, and

01:22:12.790 --> 01:22:13.790
more effective -- more importantly, how do
you avoid the resource curse?

01:22:13.790 --> 01:22:14.790
So this is an issue in much of the development
in sub-Saharan Africa.

01:22:14.790 --> 01:22:15.790
Many countries have great natural resources,
but how do you do it in a way that has inclusive

01:22:15.790 --> 01:22:16.790
growth, avoids the Dutch disease on the exchange
rate and sort of builds for a longer term?

01:22:16.790 --> 01:22:17.790
So we're working -- you know, we do some just
-- we get paid sometimes for some of our knowledge

01:22:17.790 --> 01:22:18.790
work with some of the Gulf countries, not
so we don't do loans, to be able to share

01:22:18.790 --> 01:22:19.790
that information.

01:22:19.790 --> 01:22:20.790
And this is, again, the neat thing about what's
happening in the world.

01:22:20.790 --> 01:22:21.790
You're finding that there's more and more
countries that now -- for example, I was thinking

01:22:21.790 --> 01:22:22.790
about the Gulf -- some of them are looking
at -- on Singapore as a logistics model.

01:22:22.790 --> 01:22:23.790
I'm going to be speaking to the Panamanians
here in town in a couple days.

01:22:23.790 --> 01:22:24.790
They're trying to develop this as a model.

01:22:24.790 --> 01:22:25.790
So I think it's a very healthy think that
people are sort of looking more broadly, and

01:22:25.790 --> 01:22:26.790
if we can share the information about choices,
and that there's an increasing number from

01:22:26.790 --> 01:22:27.790
the developing world and being able to draw
on it.

01:22:27.790 --> 01:22:28.790
This wasn't your question, but I want to -- I'll
take it one step further, because that's sort

01:22:28.790 --> 01:22:29.790
of -- I find it intriguing and so may some
of you.

01:22:29.790 --> 01:22:30.790
You know, as China's invested in sub-Saharan
Africa, there's been a lot of focus on resource

01:22:30.790 --> 01:22:31.790
development or infrastructure or others.

01:22:31.790 --> 01:22:32.790
And literally there's a small publication
we have come out, and I had -- was -- in discussion

01:22:32.790 --> 01:22:33.790
I had in China in recently, in Guangdong,
we were talking about it.

01:22:33.790 --> 01:22:34.790
China has about 85 million low-wage manufacturing
jobs.

01:22:34.790 --> 01:22:35.790
They got to move up the value added chain
if they want higher wages, they're going to

01:22:35.790 --> 01:22:36.790
have higher wages, and part of their whole
strategy is increased productivity, higher

01:22:36.790 --> 01:22:37.790
wages.

01:22:37.790 --> 01:22:38.790
Sub-Saharan Africa has about 8 million of
those jobs, North Africa maybe 1 (million)

01:22:38.790 --> 01:22:39.790
to 2 million, so you got about 10 million
of those jobs.

01:22:39.790 --> 01:22:40.790
We're in discussions with the Chinese, both
officials and companies, to say what is it

01:22:40.790 --> 01:22:41.790
that we could do to help the enabling environment
-- you know, ports, energy, infrastructure,

01:22:41.790 --> 01:22:42.790
customs rules, and also maybe IFC private
investment, to help some of that low-wage

01:22:42.790 --> 01:22:43.790
manufacturing move to sub-Saharan Africa?

01:22:43.790 --> 01:22:44.790
Because if you have 85 million versus 8 (million)
to 10 million, even if you just get 5 million,

01:22:44.790 --> 01:22:45.790
you've increased it by 50 percent.

01:22:45.790 --> 01:22:46.790
So I say that not that it's going to happen
overnight.

01:22:46.790 --> 01:22:47.790
And when I was in Guangdong province talking
to the provincial party secretary about this,

01:22:47.790 --> 01:22:48.790
my former colleague, Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala,
who is now the Nigerian economic coordinating

01:22:48.790 --> 01:22:49.790
minister, had been there visiting companies
that developed this.

01:22:49.790 --> 01:22:50.790
It will require some interesting intersectoral,
interministerial work in sub-Saharan Africa

01:22:50.790 --> 01:22:51.790
to make it happen.

01:22:51.790 --> 01:22:52.790
But it just gives you a sense of how fast
this is changing and if the bank as an institution

01:22:52.790 --> 01:22:53.790
can help prod it or push it or interconnect
it, you could have bit payoffs economically,

01:22:53.790 --> 01:22:54.790
politically and in stability terms.

01:22:54.790 --> 01:22:55.790
QUESTIONER: (Inaudible.)

01:22:55.790 --> 01:22:56.790
QUESTIONER: (Off mic.)

01:22:56.790 --> 01:22:57.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Is this my euro zone question?

01:22:57.790 --> 01:22:58.790
Don't I get a euro zone question?

01:22:58.790 --> 01:22:59.790
(Laughter.)

01:22:59.790 --> 01:23:00.790
QUESTIONER: No, no, no, it's not a euro zone.

01:23:00.790 --> 01:23:01.790
But you can't answer a question that hasn't
been asked to you.

01:23:01.790 --> 01:23:02.790
That's typically what de Gaulle used to do.

01:23:02.790 --> 01:23:03.790
No, in the last --

01:23:03.790 --> 01:23:04.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: (Inaudible.)

01:23:04.790 --> 01:23:05.790
(Laughs.)

01:23:05.790 --> 01:23:06.790
QUESTIONER: In the last five years or so,
the IMF has been at the forefront of all debates

01:23:06.790 --> 01:23:07.790
on the global, economic and financial governance,
and this not only because of mistrust or scandal.

01:23:07.790 --> 01:23:08.790
Apparently, the World Bank has been less on
the forefront of the debate, less visible.

01:23:08.790 --> 01:23:09.790
So my question is, how do you assess today
the importance of the role of the World Bank

01:23:09.790 --> 01:23:10.790
in the global economic governments debate?

01:23:10.790 --> 01:23:11.790
And to put it more sharply, could we live
without the World Bank?

01:23:11.790 --> 01:23:12.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, you know, you -- your -- (even
?) -- you're such a strategic thinker, Terry

01:23:12.790 --> 01:23:13.790
(sp), your perspective is a little short term.

01:23:13.790 --> 01:23:14.790
(Laughter.)

01:23:14.790 --> 01:23:15.790
Actually five years ago, we were kind of in
the news too at the bank on some governance

01:23:15.790 --> 01:23:16.790
issues.

01:23:16.790 --> 01:23:17.790
(Laughter continues.)

01:23:17.790 --> 01:23:18.790
So, to be honest, I take this as a --

01:23:18.790 --> 01:23:19.790
MR.

01:23:19.790 --> 01:23:20.790
HAASS (?): (Laughs.)

01:23:20.790 --> 01:23:21.790
(Inaudible) -- indirect compliment for the
--

01:23:21.790 --> 01:23:22.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: -- to stewardship.

01:23:22.790 --> 01:23:23.790
No, the World Bank has been very much a part
of this as well.

01:23:23.790 --> 01:23:24.790
I think the IMF -- and actually, five years
ago, the IMF was more -- there was as a sense

01:23:24.790 --> 01:23:25.790
of, what's its role in the system?

01:23:25.790 --> 01:23:26.790
But the international crisis, with its macroeconomic
nature, you know, sort of pushed that to the

01:23:26.790 --> 01:23:27.790
forefront.

01:23:27.790 --> 01:23:28.790
As for whether you could live without a World
Bank, I think the title of my article is,

01:23:28.790 --> 01:23:29.790
"Why We Need the World Bank."

01:23:29.790 --> 01:23:30.790
So you got to -- (inaudible) --

01:23:30.790 --> 01:23:31.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: "Why We Still Need" it.

01:23:31.790 --> 01:23:32.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: -- "Why We Still Need the World
Bank" -- but it really goes to this point

01:23:32.790 --> 01:23:33.790
that I've touched on, Terry (sp), which is,
is that I think that the international system

01:23:33.790 --> 01:23:34.790
actually has a dearth of these intermediating
institutions.

01:23:34.790 --> 01:23:35.790
And so -- and you know, so we have one in
the IMF and -- so we have one in trade, and

01:23:35.790 --> 01:23:36.790
we have one in macroeconomic, and we have
one in sort of microeconomic structural.

01:23:36.790 --> 01:23:37.790
You've got the U.N. system, some of which
is political, some of which is some very valuable

01:23:37.790 --> 01:23:38.790
operational arms.

01:23:38.790 --> 01:23:39.790
You've got some of the regional loan banks.

01:23:39.790 --> 01:23:40.790
In some cases, you have sort of military alliance.

01:23:40.790 --> 01:23:41.790
But given the nature of the interdependency
and interconnected problems -- and what I

01:23:41.790 --> 01:23:42.790
think will be the big issue for the rest of
our time, which is the fast rise of emerging

01:23:42.790 --> 01:23:43.790
markets and powers in the system -- I think
it helps enormously to have structures, institutions,

01:23:43.790 --> 01:23:44.790
regimes, whatever you want to describe them
as, that can play a mediating role to try

01:23:44.790 --> 01:23:45.790
to come together cooperatively on problems.

01:23:45.790 --> 01:23:46.790
And let me just take two dimensions of this.

01:23:46.790 --> 01:23:47.790
And I bet Carla and Charlene might have this
sense too.

01:23:47.790 --> 01:23:48.790
You know, this is sort of serendipitous, but
I became USTR in 2001, and now it's 2012.

01:23:48.790 --> 01:23:49.790
So it's a little bit over a decade.

01:23:49.790 --> 01:23:50.790
In historical terms, that's a rather short
period of time.

01:23:50.790 --> 01:23:51.790
And so I had the USTR, the State Department,
briefly at Goldman, then at the bank.

01:23:51.790 --> 01:23:52.790
What I have seen in terms of the role of emerging
markets, development, trade, investment, you

01:23:52.790 --> 01:23:53.790
know, climate change and, I think, increasingly,
security (issues ?), it's just ramped up enormously,

01:23:53.790 --> 01:23:54.790
OK?

01:23:54.790 --> 01:23:55.790
And so it's hard for me to conceive of an
issue now that can be addressed without their

01:23:55.790 --> 01:23:56.790
engagement and involvement.

01:23:56.790 --> 01:23:57.790
But -- and this is the big "but" -- the -- a
number of those countries, understandably,

01:23:57.790 --> 01:23:58.790
are still relatively poor.

01:23:58.790 --> 01:23:59.790
And so they want -- they're concerned about
having all the responsibilities of the developed

01:23:59.790 --> 01:24:00.790
countries.

01:24:00.790 --> 01:24:01.790
But then we had this economic crisis, so then
the developed countries are worried about

01:24:01.790 --> 01:24:02.790
sort of their sense of responsibility.

01:24:02.790 --> 01:24:03.790
So I know if you're going to discuss this
in your governance session, how you get that,

01:24:03.790 --> 01:24:04.790
quote, right -- you know, and we were talking
a little bit before as we were just coming

01:24:04.790 --> 01:24:05.790
in -- you know, the Kagan view of do you need
sort of a prime player to guide the system,

01:24:05.790 --> 01:24:06.790
which was a little bit -- in the economic
sphere, it was the Kindleberger view about

01:24:06.790 --> 01:24:07.790
the causes of the Great Depression.

01:24:07.790 --> 01:24:08.790
Britain was used to world leadership, no longer
had the capabilities in the '20s and '30s;

01:24:08.790 --> 01:24:09.790
U.S. had capabilities, wasn't used to the
leadership.

01:24:09.790 --> 01:24:10.790
How does the change in this system -- or the
Eikenberry view that the rules and norms of

01:24:10.790 --> 01:24:11.790
the international order are in place enough,
and so you -- that's going to be a very big

01:24:11.790 --> 01:24:12.790
topic.

01:24:12.790 --> 01:24:13.790
But what I've seen in a practical way and
what you'll see in the things I've written

01:24:13.790 --> 01:24:14.790
is it's fun to talk about this stuff, but
the reality is then you have to do it, OK?

01:24:14.790 --> 01:24:15.790
And so the best way to make it -- to make
a difference is to show how you can bring

01:24:15.790 --> 01:24:16.790
parties together.

01:24:16.790 --> 01:24:17.790
I have 187 shareholders -- (chuckles) -- in
very different countries.

01:24:17.790 --> 01:24:18.790
But you know, you could look at the record
on things from, you know, climate change to

01:24:18.790 --> 01:24:19.790
trade and structural reform and new capital
infusion, $90 billion raised for the poorest

01:24:19.790 --> 01:24:20.790
countries for IDA.

01:24:20.790 --> 01:24:21.790
We're able to bring people together in the
process.

01:24:21.790 --> 01:24:22.790
And if you didn't have the institution, that
wouldn't happen.

01:24:22.790 --> 01:24:23.790
And again, let me give you another practical
one from the climate area.

01:24:23.790 --> 01:24:24.790
You know, Hank Paulson, when he was secretary
of the Treasury, you know, came to me and

01:24:24.790 --> 01:24:25.790
we had this idea about creating global climate
investment funds.

01:24:25.790 --> 01:24:26.790
And so he and I went and got about $6 billion
of contributions from primarily developed

01:24:26.790 --> 01:24:27.790
countries, in different forms.

01:24:27.790 --> 01:24:28.790
It's gone up a little bit.

01:24:28.790 --> 01:24:29.790
And we created some for technology, some for
energy efficiency, some for adaptation.

01:24:29.790 --> 01:24:30.790
We've been able to leverage that to about
$50 billion and about 30 to 40 percent from

01:24:30.790 --> 01:24:31.790
private sector of that 50 billion (dollars),
the other from other bank and regional bank

01:24:31.790 --> 01:24:32.790
resources and so on and so forth.

01:24:32.790 --> 01:24:33.790
And equally important, we've now had sort
of projects launched in about 45 emerging

01:24:33.790 --> 01:24:34.790
market countries, and every time you launch
one of these projects, their investment in

01:24:34.790 --> 01:24:35.790
the climate change issue becomes somewhat
different.

01:24:35.790 --> 01:24:36.790
And the key example I'll give you is, with
India, you know, it's a very proud country

01:24:36.790 --> 01:24:37.790
about its role and sensitive to some of the
climate change topics.

01:24:37.790 --> 01:24:38.790
They wanted to do a project with us through
the Climate Investment Fund, so as to give

01:24:38.790 --> 01:24:39.790
the Climate Investment Fund additional legitimacy.

01:24:39.790 --> 01:24:40.790
So that was -- they wanted to do that support,
because we worked out the governance structure

01:24:40.790 --> 01:24:41.790
and the financing.

01:24:41.790 --> 01:24:42.790
Now this might just seem like it's, you know,
sort of an obscure thing, but you open up

01:24:42.790 --> 01:24:43.790
the newspaper and you've got endless discussions
about the green fund in the U.N., OK?

01:24:43.790 --> 01:24:44.790
So -- and people are still, you know, mapping
stuff on paper.

01:24:44.790 --> 01:24:45.790
Now and then I have to kind of pinch myself
because you got endless discussions about

01:24:45.790 --> 01:24:46.790
the green fund -- we got one up and running
with $50 billion that we'll learn lessons

01:24:46.790 --> 01:24:47.790
from.

01:24:47.790 --> 01:24:48.790
So I use that as an example of saying that
at the end of the day, you still have to make

01:24:48.790 --> 01:24:49.790
stuff happen, and then we as an institution
have to be more rigorous in measuring results,

01:24:49.790 --> 01:24:50.790
in learning where we screw it up and sort
of adapt the process.

01:24:50.790 --> 01:24:51.790
So for those reasons, if -- or post-conflict
states -- you know, we're probably the most

01:24:51.790 --> 01:24:52.790
effective player in Afghanistan today.

01:24:52.790 --> 01:24:53.790
So if you think the bottom billion matters,
if you think that climate change matters,

01:24:53.790 --> 01:24:54.790
if you think that trade matters, if you think
that emerging markets in the international

01:24:54.790 --> 01:24:55.790
system and how they fit in matter, then I
think there's a role for the bank, and for

01:24:55.790 --> 01:24:56.790
me that's a pretty clear exposition of why
you do need it.

01:24:56.790 --> 01:24:57.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Do we have any non-European hands
here?

01:24:57.790 --> 01:24:58.790
MR.

01:24:58.790 --> 01:24:59.790
: (Laughs.)

01:24:59.790 --> 01:25:00.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Sure.

01:25:00.790 --> 01:25:01.790
(Inaudible.)

01:25:01.790 --> 01:25:02.790
MR.

01:25:02.790 --> 01:25:03.790
: (Laughs.)

01:25:03.790 --> 01:25:04.790
QUESTIONER: (Off mic) -- non-European.

01:25:04.790 --> 01:25:05.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Oh, Turkey's -- I'm not sure how
we include Turkey on that one.

01:25:05.790 --> 01:25:06.790
QUESTIONER: This is, I suppose, partly European.

01:25:06.790 --> 01:25:07.790
Elizabeth Sidiropoulos, but from the South
African Institute of International Affairs.

01:25:07.790 --> 01:25:08.790
A question around a recent initiative by -- spearheaded,
seemingly, by India, which apparently will

01:25:08.790 --> 01:25:09.790
be publicly announced at the end of this month,
around the establishment of a BRICS development

01:25:09.790 --> 01:25:10.790
bank.

01:25:10.790 --> 01:25:11.790
I attended a presentation by one of the concept
writers in that regard, and he said, you know,

01:25:11.790 --> 01:25:12.790
could it play a role that the World Bank played
after World War II.

01:25:12.790 --> 01:25:13.790
And is it -- and my question is, I mean, how
would you at the World Bank view such an initiative,

01:25:13.790 --> 01:25:14.790
notwithstanding that I think the concept still
has to be further explored and defined, and

01:25:14.790 --> 01:25:15.790
also its purpose.

01:25:15.790 --> 01:25:16.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Yeah, I'm going to be in India
in about two weeks, so I'll have a better

01:25:16.790 --> 01:25:17.790
sense when I talk with people.

01:25:17.790 --> 01:25:18.790
And it -- and your -- the last phrase that
you -- (the clause ?) -- that those are actually

01:25:18.790 --> 01:25:19.790
kind of important, because you had -- you
had a Bank of a (sic) South movement here

01:25:19.790 --> 01:25:20.790
led by Chavez that I didn't fight because
I didn't think it was going to do anything

01:25:20.790 --> 01:25:21.790
and it didn't do anything, you know.

01:25:21.790 --> 01:25:22.790
And -- but also, as part of that, you have
to decide what they resisted.

01:25:22.790 --> 01:25:23.790
In that case, I think it was sometimes openness,
transparency, governance, things I think are

01:25:23.790 --> 01:25:24.790
good things.

01:25:24.790 --> 01:25:25.790
I think in this case what's driving it is
an Indian concern that because of some of

01:25:25.790 --> 01:25:26.790
the borrowing limits the bank has had, that
it's running up against (ceilings ?) about

01:25:26.790 --> 01:25:27.790
sort of investable money.

01:25:27.790 --> 01:25:28.790
I think that's a serious issue, and actually
it's a -- I've had to actually fight my board

01:25:28.790 --> 01:25:29.790
on this because I feel that if the bank takes
itself out of dealing with the Indias, the

01:25:29.790 --> 01:25:30.790
Brazils and the Chinas, as I said, it's bad
for these countries and the international

01:25:30.790 --> 01:25:31.790
system.

01:25:31.790 --> 01:25:32.790
How we engage can be very different.

01:25:32.790 --> 01:25:33.790
So with China, actually a lot of it is more
analytical work, and they use programs to

01:25:33.790 --> 01:25:34.790
-- as pilots that they test.

01:25:34.790 --> 01:25:35.790
India's at a stage where it is -- it's -- it's
-- is concerned for infrastructure development.

01:25:35.790 --> 01:25:36.790
I really don't know for sure how much of it
is capital and how much of it is the enabling

01:25:36.790 --> 01:25:37.790
environment.

01:25:37.790 --> 01:25:38.790
I kind of get mixed messages about that.

01:25:38.790 --> 01:25:39.790
But one of the things, just to give you a
sense, is, I actually pushed for an expansion

01:25:39.790 --> 01:25:40.790
of our borrowing limit for India -- if we
could get the Indians, for example, to invest

01:25:40.790 --> 01:25:41.790
in some of our securities, which would offset
it, so we've actually expanded it another

01:25:41.790 --> 01:25:42.790
4 1/2 billion (dollars) or something.

01:25:42.790 --> 01:25:43.790
I think for infrastructure, it relates to
this public-private partnership model.

01:25:43.790 --> 01:25:44.790
So I think what is often the case when somebody
promotes something, it's important to know

01:25:44.790 --> 01:25:45.790
kind of what is driving it, OK?

01:25:45.790 --> 01:25:46.790
Now, that's separate from the issue of will
this be -- is this positioning or reality.

01:25:46.790 --> 01:25:47.790
So -- because -- you know, it'd be interesting
to decide whether the BRIC countries decide

01:25:47.790 --> 01:25:48.790
to actually fund it, and where, for example,
will they put it, you know?

01:25:48.790 --> 01:25:49.790
So -- but I don't mean to be -- I think it's
a -- it's an idea that you need to understand

01:25:49.790 --> 01:25:50.790
what is driving it and to try to address it.

01:25:50.790 --> 01:25:51.790
And if we as an institution can't address
it effectively, well, then there may be other

01:25:51.790 --> 01:25:52.790
ways that people try to address it.

01:25:52.790 --> 01:25:53.790
So I mean, I'm not a monopolist.

01:25:53.790 --> 01:25:54.790
(Chuckles.)

01:25:54.790 --> 01:25:55.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Brenda (sp).

01:25:55.790 --> 01:25:56.790
Brenda (sp) there in the first table.

01:25:56.790 --> 01:25:57.790
QUESTIONER: Your -- is this working?

01:25:57.790 --> 01:25:58.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Yeah.

01:25:58.790 --> 01:25:59.790
Go ahead.

01:25:59.790 --> 01:26:00.790
QUESTIONER: Your comment about --

01:26:00.790 --> 01:26:01.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Where are you from?

01:26:01.790 --> 01:26:02.790
QUESTIONER: I'm from Turkey.

01:26:02.790 --> 01:26:03.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: Turkey, great.

01:26:03.790 --> 01:26:04.790
QUESTIONER: Your comment about moving some
manufacturing jobs from China to sub-Saharan

01:26:04.790 --> 01:26:05.790
Africa -- I think that's indicative of a broader
question in that given the demographic expectations,

01:26:05.790 --> 01:26:06.790
probably sub- Saharan Africa and parts of
the Indian subcontinent is where we expect

01:26:06.790 --> 01:26:07.790
the youth bulge.

01:26:07.790 --> 01:26:08.790
And that's probably bringing them into the
global system.

01:26:08.790 --> 01:26:09.790
Global trade and commerce will be a priority.

01:26:09.790 --> 01:26:10.790
But when that happens, if that happens, the
abundance of labor will probably impact intrastate

01:26:10.790 --> 01:26:11.790
income equality across the world, including
the Western world.

01:26:11.790 --> 01:26:12.790
I don't know if that's sustainable politically.

01:26:12.790 --> 01:26:13.790
So I'm wondering if when you look at the broader
picture in a 10, 20-year perspective, are

01:26:13.790 --> 01:26:14.790
we only talking about interstate sort of equality
and convergence and moving people -- sort

01:26:14.790 --> 01:26:15.790
of the average upwards, or do we also care
about intrastate equality as a development

01:26:15.790 --> 01:26:16.790
objective?

01:26:16.790 --> 01:26:17.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: I think we definitely have to.

01:26:17.790 --> 01:26:18.790
In our work in emerging-market countries,
in developing countries, we're very much focused

01:26:18.790 --> 01:26:19.790
on that, what we call inclusive growth.

01:26:19.790 --> 01:26:20.790
And in some cases, it starts with having the
fundamentals of nutrition and education.

01:26:20.790 --> 01:26:21.790
And in every country I deal with -- and I'm
going to come to the developed countries as

01:26:21.790 --> 01:26:22.790
well -- there's a huge interest in this connection
between education, skills and workforce.

01:26:22.790 --> 01:26:23.790
And our next World Development Report is actually
on jobs, just as we did one on gender and

01:26:23.790 --> 01:26:24.790
post-conflict.

01:26:24.790 --> 01:26:25.790
And this may seem, well, that's obvious.

01:26:25.790 --> 01:26:26.790
But what we were trying to get at is when
I studied labor economics, now getting to

01:26:26.790 --> 01:26:27.790
be quite distant, it -- the field actually
didn't strike me as too interesting because

01:26:27.790 --> 01:26:28.790
it was either discussions of kind of unions
and economies or kind of the labor market

01:26:28.790 --> 01:26:29.790
was a derivative of your macroeconomic policies.

01:26:29.790 --> 01:26:30.790
This is trying to look at the -- from a different
issue about the social capital that's developed,

01:26:30.790 --> 01:26:31.790
the productivity of individuals, how to sort
of see this as part of your development model.

01:26:31.790 --> 01:26:32.790
And the work isn't done yet, but it's an example
of where I hope we can make an added contribution,

01:26:32.790 --> 01:26:33.790
though the way that you started the question
is -- let me just say that I think is going

01:26:33.790 --> 01:26:34.790
to be a big issue for developed economies,
OK, and their willingness to sustain and participate

01:26:34.790 --> 01:26:35.790
in an open international system and resist
protectionism and populism and so on and so

01:26:35.790 --> 01:26:36.790
forth.

01:26:36.790 --> 01:26:37.790
And I think that's extremely important.

01:26:37.790 --> 01:26:38.790
It happens not to be the remit of the bank
-- (chuckles) -- although one of the things

01:26:38.790 --> 01:26:39.790
I've tried -- you know, and I do this with
the Congress as well as other forums in the

01:26:39.790 --> 01:26:40.790
United States -- to try to say there's actually
some very interesting lessons out there coming

01:26:40.790 --> 01:26:41.790
from emerging markets that you should be -- can
be able to draw from, OK?

01:26:41.790 --> 01:26:42.790
So I mentioned the infrastructure one.

01:26:42.790 --> 01:26:43.790
But in education, you know, it's very interesting.

01:26:43.790 --> 01:26:44.790
You see the debate in the United States about
measurement and performance standards.

01:26:44.790 --> 01:26:45.790
At least in the developing countries I deal
with, it's a no-brainer.

01:26:45.790 --> 01:26:46.790
Of course you've got to measure what the performance
is.

01:26:46.790 --> 01:26:47.790
You know, how else are you going to know what
the results are, you know?

01:26:47.790 --> 01:26:48.790
And -- but here if you test, oh, you might
learn something, you know?

01:26:48.790 --> 01:26:49.790
So I'm hopeful that over time -- you know,
each of these things create a risk and an

01:26:49.790 --> 01:26:50.790
opportunity that you could actually create
a greater flow of information and experience

01:26:50.790 --> 01:26:51.790
that developed countries could manage as well.

01:26:51.790 --> 01:26:52.790
But the core point that I just -- I want to
emphasize is -- was the one sort of inherent

01:26:52.790 --> 01:26:53.790
in this.

01:26:53.790 --> 01:26:54.790
Look, there's problems in the international
system, but by and large if you look at the

01:26:54.790 --> 01:26:55.790
growth rates for sort of the whole world over
the past 50 or 60 years, it hasn't been too

01:26:55.790 --> 01:26:56.790
darn bad, OK?

01:26:56.790 --> 01:26:57.790
And in -- from historical terms.

01:26:57.790 --> 01:26:58.790
But this is not pre-ordained.

01:26:58.790 --> 01:26:59.790
You could lose this, you know?

01:26:59.790 --> 01:27:00.790
And these factors that I mentioned in the
answer to Terry (sp), you know, the emerging

01:27:00.790 --> 01:27:01.790
markets feeling, oh, well, they have to take
on additional responsibilities, but you know,

01:27:01.790 --> 01:27:02.790
what's their say in the system, and have they
really -- you know, they've still got a lot

01:27:02.790 --> 01:27:03.790
of development issues.

01:27:03.790 --> 01:27:04.790
The developed countries, particularly ones
that are aging -- (inaudible) -- sort of their

01:27:04.790 --> 01:27:05.790
sort of role in the system.

01:27:05.790 --> 01:27:06.790
And that really goes back, I guess, to the
question that Terri (sp) and Richard (sp)

01:27:06.790 --> 01:27:07.790
mentioned.

01:27:07.790 --> 01:27:08.790
It's why institutions like the World Bank
-- (chuckles) -- they're important to try

01:27:08.790 --> 01:27:09.790
to interconnect people, and as opposed to
shout at each other, try to come up with cooperative

01:27:09.790 --> 01:27:10.790
solutions.

01:27:10.790 --> 01:27:11.790
But the other piece of it is my difference
with sort of -- it's not enough just to have

01:27:11.790 --> 01:27:12.790
the institution and talk to each other; you
have to try to resolve real problems.

01:27:12.790 --> 01:27:13.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: I know there's several of you who
still have questions.

01:27:13.790 --> 01:27:14.790
I -- OK, we'll do one last question.

01:27:14.790 --> 01:27:15.790
I want to make sure -- (inaudible) -- from
Japan -- I -- and then we'll do that.

01:27:15.790 --> 01:27:16.790
QUESTIONER: (Through interpreter.)

01:27:16.790 --> 01:27:17.790
I come from Japan.

01:27:17.790 --> 01:27:18.790
I have -- just have a couple of questions.

01:27:18.790 --> 01:27:19.790
The World Bank and China are doing 2030.

01:27:19.790 --> 01:27:20.790
And what is the significance of conducting
this kind of a research, right -- investigation

01:27:20.790 --> 01:27:21.790
at the time when China is to change their
guards in Beijing, so to speak?

01:27:21.790 --> 01:27:22.790
And what's your view on the dollar- renminbi
relationship in 2030 if the structural, you

01:27:22.790 --> 01:27:23.790
know, improvement succeeds?

01:27:23.790 --> 01:27:24.790
&gt;&gt; ZOELLICK: Well, let me focus on the first
question -- (laughter) -- because it's actually

01:27:24.790 --> 01:27:25.790
the more important question, just so people
have a little sense of this.

01:27:25.790 --> 01:27:26.790
The bank has had a very good brand in China,
dating back to Robert McNamara opening relations

01:27:26.790 --> 01:27:27.790
in 1980 with Deng Xiaoping.

01:27:27.790 --> 01:27:28.790
And over the course of some 30 years, there
were points in the idea development process

01:27:28.790 --> 01:27:29.790
in China where the bank seemed to play a catalytic
role.

01:27:29.790 --> 01:27:30.790
There -- I know this from talking -- is more
to the Chinese counterparts.

01:27:30.790 --> 01:27:31.790
In 1985 there was a floating seminar down
the Yangtze River for four or five days that

01:27:31.790 --> 01:27:32.790
led to a report.

01:27:32.790 --> 01:27:33.790
In the late '90s there was something held
in Dalian.

01:27:33.790 --> 01:27:34.790
And the relevance of this was -- a Chinese
official actually came to me before the 30th

01:27:34.790 --> 01:27:35.790
anniversary.

01:27:35.790 --> 01:27:36.790
And he said, Bob, you know, everybody thinks
China loves anniversaries and that we just

01:27:36.790 --> 01:27:37.790
want to celebrate, and he said, that's true,
but when you come for the 30th anniversary,

01:27:37.790 --> 01:27:38.790
he said, what about planting the idea of a
report that would deal with some of the issues

01:27:38.790 --> 01:27:39.790
that are stirring in China, like how to avoid
the middle-income trap, and kind of how do

01:27:39.790 --> 01:27:40.790
we -- we've talked in the next five-year plan
about rebalancing domestic demand, consumer

01:27:40.790 --> 01:27:41.790
-- but how do you really do that?

01:27:41.790 --> 01:27:42.790
And quite intriguingly, he also said -- he
said, as you know -- so you work a lot with

01:27:42.790 --> 01:27:43.790
China; people in Washington think, you know,
people are waking up in Beijing trying to

01:27:43.790 --> 01:27:44.790
take over the world every day.

01:27:44.790 --> 01:27:45.790
And he said, most of the time they're trying
to just, you know, work on the problems in

01:27:45.790 --> 01:27:46.790
Beijing and China.

01:27:46.790 --> 01:27:47.790
So he said, what would be the implications
of this internationally?

01:27:47.790 --> 01:27:48.790
First off -- and then -- so then I went to
China.

01:27:48.790 --> 01:27:49.790
I proposed this study.

01:27:49.790 --> 01:27:50.790
And Li Keqiang, the next -- or it looks like
the next premier, was very enthusiastic about

01:27:50.790 --> 01:27:51.790
it and, quite importantly, asked us to work
with the Development Research Council, which

01:27:51.790 --> 01:27:52.790
is the State Council under -- is the -- is
the research think thank under the State Council.

01:27:52.790 --> 01:27:53.790
We also work with the Ministry of Finance.

01:27:53.790 --> 01:27:54.790
And it was blessed by President Hu and Xi
Jinping, but Li Keqiang is kind of the godfather

01:27:54.790 --> 01:27:55.790
of the process.

01:27:55.790 --> 01:27:56.790
And we've been at it for about 18 months.

01:27:56.790 --> 01:27:57.790
We had a -- in September, we had a draft;
we had various international commentaries

01:27:57.790 --> 01:27:58.790
about, and so on and so forth.

01:27:58.790 --> 01:27:59.790
Now, the first -- from a strategic perspective,
since all of you aspire to strategy, I find

01:27:59.790 --> 01:28:00.790
it very impressive that China after 30 years
of 10 percent growth could say, you know what,

01:28:00.790 --> 01:28:01.790
this may not work in the future; we got to
rethink this model, OK?

01:28:01.790 --> 01:28:02.790
Whether they accomplish it, that's a different
question.

01:28:02.790 --> 01:28:03.790
But again, I would suggest that maybe the
U.S., Europe and Japan without 10 percent

01:28:03.790 --> 01:28:04.790
growth should be thinking a little bit in
these terms.

01:28:04.790 --> 01:28:05.790
The second thing is that what I think is also
going on was that this is to contribute to

01:28:05.790 --> 01:28:06.790
their own internal debate and discussion and
to be catalytic.

01:28:06.790 --> 01:28:07.790
So I asked Justin Lin, our chief economist
from China.

01:28:07.790 --> 01:28:08.790
And I said, Justin, what do you think is going
on here?

01:28:08.790 --> 01:28:09.790
Because you know, Chinese economists could
come up with a lot of this.

01:28:09.790 --> 01:28:10.790
And he said, yes; he said, but as you know,
in any country, people work on their own particular

01:28:10.790 --> 01:28:11.790
piece of the problem.

01:28:11.790 --> 01:28:12.790
This is a catalyst to get people to look at
the economywide pieces.

01:28:12.790 --> 01:28:13.790
And frankly, he said, the bank's got a very
good standing and reputation, and so it will

01:28:13.790 --> 01:28:14.790
be taken very seriously as we come in with
it together.

01:28:14.790 --> 01:28:15.790
Next point is, of course, the devil will be
in the details of implementation.

01:28:15.790 --> 01:28:16.790
But you know, even the summary report is 450
pages long.

01:28:16.790 --> 01:28:17.790
So I wouldn't expect everybody to have read
it.

01:28:17.790 --> 01:28:18.790
But you'll see, if you skim it, there's things
about phasing, transition, (quick wins ?) -- and

01:28:18.790 --> 01:28:19.790
going back to this question a little bit -- how
do you build public participation in it to

01:28:19.790 --> 01:28:20.790
build support along the process?

01:28:20.790 --> 01:28:21.790
So I think it's a very serious exercise.

01:28:21.790 --> 01:28:22.790
Now, the bottom line, my own guess -- but
it's only a guess -- is, is that, you know,

01:28:22.790 --> 01:28:23.790
this will wait until the 18th party congress;
there'll be some settling down after that.

01:28:23.790 --> 01:28:24.790
I don't expect China to follow a "big bang"
approach, like Margaret Thatcher did, because

01:28:24.790 --> 01:28:25.790
that's not the Chinese way.

01:28:25.790 --> 01:28:26.790
But my best guess is you'll see these ideas
pursued in pilots at local level, in some

01:28:26.790 --> 01:28:27.790
sense, to kind of test them as they move forward.

01:28:27.790 --> 01:28:28.790
And again, you know, some people are quick
to judge, oh, well, will China do this or

01:28:28.790 --> 01:28:29.790
this?

01:28:29.790 --> 01:28:30.790
And I just think it's important to also look
at how other countries are doing this.

01:28:30.790 --> 01:28:31.790
And then, again, what I suggested is in my
comments on the international side, there

01:28:31.790 --> 01:28:32.790
are some very interesting opportunities here
for creative minds to kind of develop some

01:28:32.790 --> 01:28:33.790
win-win agenda.

01:28:33.790 --> 01:28:34.790
And the last point is -- you know, what's
quite interesting, the reaction -- and this

01:28:34.790 --> 01:28:35.790
is what I'd hoped for -- we're getting -- one
of my colleagues was in Brazil last week.

01:28:35.790 --> 01:28:36.790
The topic was a very -- the report was a very
big topic because people are facing similar

01:28:36.790 --> 01:28:37.790
problems.

01:28:37.790 --> 01:28:38.790
And this also gives you, going back to where
I started, a little idea that if the bank

01:28:38.790 --> 01:28:39.790
were only working on the 79 poorest countries,
we'd actually miss out on this.

01:28:39.790 --> 01:28:40.790
And this question of rebalancing the international
system, how middle-income countries fit in

01:28:40.790 --> 01:28:41.790
the system, you know, their own growth model
-- those are going to be important questions

01:28:41.790 --> 01:28:42.790
for the world.

01:28:42.790 --> 01:28:43.790
So I'm -- at least so far, I'm very pleased
with the report.

01:28:43.790 --> 01:28:44.790
&gt;&gt; HAASS: We have a tradition here where we
end meetings on time, and today we violated

01:28:44.790 --> 01:28:45.790
that tradition because we went a few minutes
over, but I'm glad we did.

01:28:45.790 --> 01:28:46.790
Bob, thank you for being with us today, and
I think more important, thank you for all

01:28:46.790 --> 01:28:47.790
you've done over the last five years at the
bank.

01:28:47.790 --> 01:28:48.790
It's a --

01:28:48.790 --> 01:28:49.790
(inaudible).

01:28:49.790 --> 01:28:49.791
(Applause.)

